RGB inputs can be converted to spikes using four common encoding schemes: direct, phase, rate, and TTFS~\cite{adrian1926impulses, park2020t2fsnn, kim2018deep}. As shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:encoding_test}, direct encoding, a lossless approach ensures that no information is lost—which repeats the entire image at every time step matches current Spiking Transformers that compute attention independently across steps, and therefore yields the highest Top-1 accuracy on all models. In contrast, the sparser phase, rate, and TTFS encodings reduce spike density and weaken spatial coherence, leading to lower accuracy and highlighting the need for future architectures with temporally aware attention or recurrent mechanisms.


