\begin{abstract}
Ensuring equitable public transit access remains challenging, particularly in densely populated cities like New York City (NYC), where low-income and minority communities often face limited transit accessibility. Bike-sharing systems (BSS) can bridge these equity gaps by providing affordable first- and last-mile connections. However, strategically expanding BSS into underserved neighborhoods is difficult due to uncertain bike-sharing demand at newly planned (``cold-start'') station locations and limitations in traditional accessibility metrics that may overlook realistic bike usage potential.
We introduce \textbf{Transit for All (TFA)}, a spatial computing framework designed to guide the equitable expansion of BSS through three components: (1) spatially-informed bike-sharing demand prediction at cold-start stations using region representation learning that integrates multimodal geospatial data, (2) comprehensive transit accessibility assessment leveraging our novel weighted Public Transport Accessibility Level (wPTAL) by combining predicted bike-sharing demand with conventional transit accessibility metrics, and (3) strategic recommendations for new bike station placements that consider potential ridership and equity enhancement.
Using NYC as a case study, we identify transit accessibility gaps that disproportionately impact low-income and minority communities in historically underserved neighborhoods. Our results show that strategically placing new stations guided by wPTAL notably reduces disparities in transit access related to economic and demographic factors. From our study, we demonstrate that TFA provides practical guidance for urban planners to promote equitable transit and enhance the quality of life in underserved urban communities.

% Equitable access to public transit remains challenging in cities due to unevenly distributed infrastructure. This imbalance disproportionately affects low-income and minority communities and hinders progress toward the UN Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 11.2, which aims to provide accessible, safe, and sustainable transport for all. In New York City (NYC), a densely populated metropolis with extensive yet spatially unequal transit coverage, bike-sharing systems (BSS) offer a practical solution to bridge first- and last-mile transit gaps. However, existing methods for planning bike-sharing station locations rely on historical usage data unavailable for newly planned ("cold-start") stations or simplify interactions among urban characteristics, limiting their effectiveness in addressing equity gaps. To address this, we present \textbf{Transit for All (TFA)}, a spatial computing framework designed to support equitable, data-driven expansion of bike-sharing networks. TFA leverages multimodal urban data, including demographics, transit infrastructure, satellite imagery, and points-of-interest, using region representation learning to accurately predict demand at cold-start stations. To further inform equitable site selection, TFA introduces a weighted Public Transport Accessibility Level (wPTAL) metric that combines traditional transit accessibility with predicted bike-sharing demand. Evaluated using Citi Bike data in NYC, TFA improves demand prediction accuracy and identifies strategic locations to enhance accessibility in historically underserved neighborhoods. TFA provides targeted site recommendations to reduce existing transit gaps, demonstrating practical utility for planners aiming to achieve equitable urban mobility aligned with SDG 11.2.


% Equitable access to public transit remains a pressing challenge in many cities, where infrastructure disparities disproportionately affect low-income and minority communities. In New York City—a dense, transit-rich yet spatially inequitable metropolis—bike-sharing systems (BSS) offer a promising last-mile solution. To address challenges such as the lack of historical usage data, insufficient modeling of feature interactions, and the limitations of traditional accessibility metrics, we present Transit for All (TFA), a modular, deployable framework for equity-centered bike-sharing expansion. TFA integrates spatial representation learning to generate unified embeddings from multimodal urban data, including demographics, transit infrastructure, satellite imagery, and points of interest, predicts cold-start station demand using these embeddings, and introduces a novel weighted Public Transport Accessibility Level (wPTAL) that combines predicted demand with traditional metrics to guide strategic site placement. Deployed in New York City using Citi Bike data, TFA significantly improves demand prediction and identifies high-impact station locations that enhance mobility for underserved communities, offering a scalable solution for equitable transit planning and advancing UN Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 11.2 for the city.


\end{abstract}

