\section{Introduction}
\label{sec_introduction}
Beyond-5G (B5G) and 6G networks are expected to  accommodate  diverse quality of service (QoS) requirements~\cite{Vision6G}. Emerging Industry~$5.0$ scenarios--smart factories, and real-time motion control—tighten these requirements, including end-to-end latencies below $1\text{ms}$ and microsecond-level jitter, with reliability exceeding $99.999\%$~\cite{Industry5O}. Wired Ethernet already achieves such determinism through the IEEE Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN) tool-set, which combines per-flow traffic shaping with schedule-driven queuing to guarantee hard deadlines~\cite{Sasiain}. Replicating such stringent guarantees over the inherently stochastic wireless air interface is highly challenging~\cite{Sharma}.


%One promising approach draws inspiration from wired networks, where IEEE 802.1Qav's~\cite{standard} credit-based shaper (CBS) achieves precisely this kind of deterministic performance in Ethernet: as long as the aggregate reserved rate is strictly below link capacity, each flow's queuing delay is deterministically bounded~\cite{fi16090345}. Replicating the same credit logic to wireless networks would therefore extend deterministic--or, more precisely, deterministically bounded under the assumed error budget--latency to high-priority traffic while still limiting interference from lower-priority flows.

One promising approach  draws inspiration from Ethernet networks, where IEEE 802.1Qav's~\cite{standard} credit-based shaper (CBS) achieves deterministic performance. As long as the aggregate reserved rate is strictly below the link capacity, each flow's queuing delay is deterministically bounded~\cite{fi16090345}.  Adapting this credit logic for wireless networks would therefore extend predictable, bounded latency to high-priority traffic, while still ensuring that lower-priority flows cannot exceed their allocated share.

