\subsection{Why Does Classical CBS Fail in a 5G NR Scheduler?} 
\label{subsec:cbs_challenges_wireless}
The key assumptions in IEEE 802.1Qav CBS are:
(i) \emph{continuous time} credit evolution;
(ii) a \emph{single, fixed link rate} $R_{\text{link}}$; and
%This assumes a transmission medium where sending does not interfere with receiving, and more importantly for shaping, the egress path is essentially continuously available without timing gaps imposed by the medium access protocol itself (beyond potential blocking by higher priority traffic already using the egress path).
(iii) shaping a single queue at a particular time instant that outputs frames without needing  complex resource allocation (RA) procedures.
%This assumes CBS operates in a relatively simple environment – a queue outputting L2 frames onto a wire. It doesn't need to interact with complex resource allocation across multiple dimensions (time, frequency, users) or adaptive modulation.
On the other hand, 5G NR presents a fundamentally different operational context, invalidating these assumptions, posing significant challenges for implementing CBS within the RAN.  
\subsubsection{Slot-quantized time} NR operates in discrete slots ($T_{\text{slot}}$), requiring discrete credit updates. %NR admits or rejects traffic once every slot, so credit must be updated in \emph{discrete} steps of $T_{\text{slot}}$ rather than continuously. 
\subsubsection{Grant-dependent rate} 5G service rates  are grant-dependent, unlike the fixed link rate in CBS. %The bits delivered in a slot equal the product of (i) the gNB’s per-UE resource block (RB) grant and (ii) the modulation and coding scheme (MCS)-dependent spectral efficiency--both varying on a per-slot basis.
\subsubsection{Multi-user multiplexing} A wired port serves one frame at a time; NR can schedule multiple UEs/flows concurrently. %Credit therefore governs \emph{multiple} queues that share one slot, not one queue that owns the link.
\subsubsection{Feedback-coupled retransmissions} Automatic repeat request (ARQ) and Hybrid-ARQ (HARQ) can force a protocol data unit (PDU) to be transmitted multiple times; classical CBS is designed for reliable wires and  has no such mechanisms.
% \begin{enumerate} \item \textbf{Slot-quantized time.} NR operates in discrete slots ($T_{\text{slot}}$), requiring discrete credit updates, unlike continuous-time CBS. %NR admits or rejects traffic once every slot, so credit must be updated in \emph{discrete} steps of $T_{\text{slot}}$ rather than continuously. 
% \item \textbf{Grant-dependent rate.} 5G service rates per TTI are grant-dependent (varying with RBs and MCS), unlike the fixed link rate underpinning classical CBS's \texttt{sendSlope}. %The bits delivered in a slot equal the product of (i) the gNB’s per-UE resource block (RB) grant and (ii) the modulation and coding scheme (MCS)-dependent spectral efficiency--both varying on a per-slot basis.
% \item \textbf{Multi-user multiplexing.} A wired port serves one frame at a time; NR can schedule multiple UEs/flows concurrently. Credit therefore governs \emph{multiple} queues that share one slot, not one queue that owns the link.
% \item \textbf{Half-duplex air-interface constraints.} HARQ turn-around and TDD guard periods fragment service windows. \end{enumerate}
Consequently, these departures from the classical CBS--specifically,  discrete operational timeline, highly dynamic service rates tied to scheduling grants and channel quality, concurrent servicing of multiple users, and  the complexities introduced by wireless MAC/PHY feedback loops—invalidate key assumptions, making  direct porting  non-trivial. Effectively applying credit-based shaping within the 5G NR RAN mandates a rethinking of the mechanism, particularly requiring a discrete-time credit evolution logic accounting for the variable service provided in each transmission time interval (TTI). 
