\subsection{Latency Performance Evaluation}
\label{subsec:latency}
We measure  end-to-end downlink latency from packet generation at the gNB to successful reception at the UE application layer. % This metric inherently includes queuing delays at the gNB, CBS shaping delays, MAC scheduling delays, transmission time over the channel, and potential delays arising from HARQ retransmissions due to channel errors introduced by the UMa channel model.
Figure~\ref{fig:latency_cdf_combined} presents the empirical CDF by priority  under (a) nominal ($\rho=1.0$), (b) overload ($\rho=4.0$), comparing RR, CBS-DT, and CBS-PU. At nominal load, as expected, RR shows minimal class isolation, while both CBS variants provide clear latency differentiation favoring $p_1$ (P99 ${<}$ 5~ms) over $p_2$ and $p_3$. Notably, CBS-PU yields lower tail latency for $p_3$ compared to CBS-DT (e.g., 20~ms vs 80~ms 99P), directly reflecting its faster credit recovery mechanism (Fig.~\ref{fig:grid_br}). Under overload, RR differentiation collapses entirely. In contrast, both CBS variants robustly protect $p_1$ latency ($99P{<}$5~ms) while $p_3$ latency severely degrades ($99P{\approx}$350~ms) due to low reserved bandwidth share for $p_3$, with negligible difference between CBS-PU and CBS-DT under such extreme conditions. Thus, CBS provides essential QoS isolation, particularly under congestion, and CBS-PU offers additional latency benefits for lower priorities at moderate loads due to its refined credit accounting.
% Under nominal load ($\rho=1.0$), RR provides low average latency but exhibits poor isolation between classes; the CDFs for P1, P2, and P3 are closely clustered. CBS-PU, however, establishes clear latency differentiation: the P1 CDF is significantly steeper and shifted leftward, indicating much lower latency (e.g., 99th percentile below 5ms), while P2 and P3 CDFs are progressively shifted to the right, reflecting their lower scheduling priority.
% Under heavy overload ($\rho=4.0$), the RR scheduler fails to provide any QoS differentiation, with the CDFs for all priority classes collapsing into a single curve indicating high latency and large tails for everyone. In stark contrast, CBS-PU maintains robust priority enforcement even under extreme load. The P1 latency remains bounded within a few milliseconds, while P2 experiences moderate delays, and P3 latency degrades significantly, with a substantial fraction of packets experiencing delays exceeding 100ms. This confirms that the CBS eligibility gating, combined with the underlying priority-aware (implicit or explicit) MAC scheduling, effectively shields high-priority traffic from congestion caused by lower-priority flows, a crucial property RR inherently lacks. While not explicitly plotted, the latency profile for Standard CBS was observed to be very similar to CBS-PU in terms of priority differentiation, with minor differences potentially arising from the slightly longer credit recovery times under Standard CBS affecting lower priorities.
