\textbf{Conversion of allocation matrix $S$ to $A$.} The bidders' allocation matrix \( A \) contains the allocation of all brands and stores for each ad position. In matrix $A$, the allocation of brand $i$ (or store $j$) at position $k$ is denoted as $a_{i \cdot k}$ (or $a_{\cdot j k}$).
% In matrix $A$, the allocation of brand $i$ at position $k$ is denoted as $a_{i \cdot k}$, and the allocation of store $j$ at position $k$ is represented as $a_{\cdot j k}$. 
These values, $a_{i \cdot k}$ and $a_{\cdot j k}$, are computed based on the bundles' allocation matrix $S$ through:


\begin{align*}
\left\{\begin{array}{l}
a_{i \cdot k}=\sum_{c \in C_{i \cdot}} s_{c k},\quad \forall k \in \mathbb{K} \text{\hfill ,}\\
a_{\cdot j k}=\sum_{c \in C_{\cdot j}} s_{c k}, \quad \forall k \in \mathbb{K} \text{\hfill ,}
\end{array}\right.
\end{align*}
where $C_{i \cdot}$ and $C_{\cdot j}$ represent the sets of all bundles containing brand $i$ and store $j$, respectively.

% the allocation probability of store $i$ on slot $k$ is the sum of the allocation probabilities on slot $k$ for all bundles that include store $i$ and the calculation of the allocation probability of brand $j$ on slot $k$ is similar to this.

