We can now show that $\Flat(\varphi)$ is an $\LS$-formula defining $\varphi$ whenever such an $\LS$-definition exists.

\begin{theorem}\label{prop:definability1}
	Let $L/\LS$ be either $\CT/\FOT$ with equality-free $\CT$ and $\FOT$, or any pair of modal nominal-free logics from Table~\ref{table:results}. Then an $L$-formula $\varphi$ is $\LS$-definable iff $\models \varphi \leftrightarrow \Flat(\varphi)$.
\end{theorem}
