\subsection{\subsectiontexta}\label{subsection:encodings}

A core component of our general theory of \textit{Self-Reference Systems} is an encoding function $\mu: \E \rightarrow \C$ from a set of \textit{expressions} to a set of \textit{constants}. In this section, we define encodings in the fields of Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory that will be used throughout the remainder of this paper.

\pagebreak

\finestsection{Arithmetic Logic}

We first note that for this paper's purposes, it suffices to reason about formulas with 0 free variables (sentences) or 1 free variable (predicates). Then, we recall that given a set $\F_n$ of formulae with 0 up to $n$ free variables, the Lindenbaum Algebra $\L_n$ is the set of equivalence classes of formulae in $\F_n$ with respect to logical equivalence in the theory (Definition \ref{definition:lindenbaum-algebra-def}). We define an encoding $\NGodelvar: \L_1 \rightarrow \N$ as follows:

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:enc}
    Given a Gödel Numbering $G: \F_1 \rightarrow \N$, we define $\NGodelvar: \L_1 \rightarrow \N$ by mapping the equivalence class of a formula $f$ to $G(f')$, where $f'$ is the shortest formula among those logically equivalent to $f$.\footnote{Permuting different variable names (e.g., $x, y, z, \dots$ or $x_0, x_1, x_2, \dots$) in a formula produces a logically equivalent formula with a different Gödel number. Thus, for $\NGodelvar$ to be well-defined and computable, we can set a rule such as \say{if $f$ has $n$ free variables, $f'$ may only use propositional variables from a fixed set of $n$ variable names under a particular precedence}.} Formally, $\NGodel{f} = G(f')$.
\end{definition}

Analogously to the shorthand $\Obar{f} \coloneqq \underline{G(f)}$, i.e., the numeral of $G(f)$ (see Definition \ref{definition:lindenbaum-algebra-def}), we introduce $\DObar{f} \coloneqq \underline{\NGodel{f}} = \underline{G(f')} = \Obar{f'}$, i.e., the numeral of the shortest formula $f'$ logically equivalent to $f$.

\parsplit

There are two important reasons for defining $\NGodel{f}$ this way. Firstly, it is useful to ensure that if two formulae $f, g \in \F_1$ are logically equivalent then for any predicate $B(x)$ so are $B(\DObar{f})$ and $B(\DObar{g})$. This is not necessarily the case when using the original Gödel numerals $\Obarvar$ rather than $\DObarvar$. For instance, if $f$ and $g$ are distinct but logically equivalent formulae then $G(f) \neq G(g)$. So, if $G(f) = n$, $G(g) = m$ and $B(x)$ is the predicate $\saymath{x = \underline{m}}$ then $B(\Obar{f}) = \saymath{\underline{n} = \underline{m}}$ is not logically equivalent to $B(\Obar{g}) = \saymath{\underline{m} = \underline{m}}$. Thus, our definition of $\NGodelvar$ ensures $f$ and $g$ are logically equivalent --- i.e., $f = g$ in $\L_1$ --- implies $\NGodel{f} = \NGodel{g}$ and thus $B(\DObar{f}) = B(\DObar{g})$ in $\L_1$. Secondly, to prove Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem by contradiction using $\NGodelvar$ it is essential that $\NGodelvar$ is computable in a complete theory. In other words, given $f \in \F_1$, the task of finding the shortest $f' \in \F_1$ that is logically equivalent to $f$ can be achieved in finitely many steps. Indeed, $\NGodelvar$ is computable because given a formula $f$, there are only finitely many formulas that are as short or shorter than $f$ which we need to check for logical equivalence to $f$ (see Footnote 4). Completeness ensures that there is a proof which we can access for each check.

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:equiv-2}
    An alternative approach is to reason about formulae without the use of Lindenbaum Algebras (and hence without $\NGodelvar$) by defining properties in our general theory up to equivalence / isomorphism rather than equality. We forgo that generalisation in this paper to keep the definitions and results in our general theory simpler.
\end{note}

\finestsection{Social Choice Theory}

For $\P$, the set of weak linear orders (i.e., preference relations) on a fixed set of alternatives $\A$, an encoding function will correspond to a Social Welfare Function with an extended domain and codomain. Specifically, instead of functions $D \rightarrow \P$ for $\D \subseteq \PN$ (see Section \ref{subsection:background-arrow}), an encoding will be a function $\TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$, where $\TPrefs \coloneqq \P \cup \{\c\}$ for a distinct symbol $\c$ that corresponds a kind of Condorcet Paradox called a \textit{Complete Condorcet Paradox}, defined as follows.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:complete-condorcet}
    Given a finite set of alternatives $\A$, a \textit{Complete Condorcet Paradox} is a contradiction where for every pair of alternatives $a, b \in \A$: both $a \prec b$ and $b \prec a$ hold strictly, i.e., it is not the case that $a \sim b$.
\end{definition}

In the 3 alternative case, all Condorcet Paradoxes are complete and vice versa (see Note \ref{note:dantoni-2}). In Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-full} we will show that Arrow's Impossibility Theorem is equivalent to the statement that for any Social Welfare Function satisfying Unanimity, IIA and Non-Dictatorship, there is a profile that aggregates to a Complete Condorcet Paradox.

\parsplit

To formalise Complete Condorcet Paradoxes as an extension of the set $\P$, we first observe that we can partially order $\P$ by a strictness relation (see Appendix \ref{appendix:order-rel} for the necessary prerequisites in order theory). A weak linear order $r$ is stricter than a weak linear order $s$ (denoted $r \leq s$) if $r$ has at least all the strict preferences of $s$, i.e., for any alternatives $a, b \in \A$: $a \prec b$ (i.e., strictly) in $s \implies a \prec b$ in $r$. Note, the partial order $(\P, \leq)$ has a top element given by the weak-linear order indifferent on all alternatives, denoted $\i \in \P$. For example, for $\A = \{a,b,c\}$: $\i$ corresponds to $a \sim b \sim c$. Then, we extend $\P$ to include Complete Condorcet Paradoxes as follows.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:extending-order}
    Given the strictness ordering $(\P, \leq)$ on the set $\P$ weak linear orders on alternatives $\A$. We define $\TPrefs \coloneqq \P \cup \{\c\}$ for $\c \notin \P$ and extend $\leq$ to $\TPrefs$ by adding the minimal number of relations to satisfy $\forall r \in \TPrefs$: $\c \leq r$, i.e., for $\c$ to be the bottom element of $(\TPrefs, \leq)$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:interpretation}
    Maintaining our interpretation of $\leq$ as a strictness ordering, $\c$ as the bottom element of $(\TPrefs, \leq)$ means that $\c$ is stricter than every preference relation in $\P$. Moreover, because $r \leq s$ in $\P$ means $a \prec b$ in $s$ implies $a \prec b$ in $r$, then $\c$ being stricter than all preference relations represents a situation where for all alternatives $a, b \in \A$: $a \prec b$ in $\c$, i.e., $\c$ is a Complete Condorcet Paradox. This interpretation of a paradox being a bottom element is analogous to the bottom element $\bot$ in a Lindenbaum Algebra being equivalent to all contradictions.
\end{note}

Social Welfare Functions can be more generally defined as functions $\TWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \TPrefs$, where Unrestricted Domain holds only when $im(w) = \P$. The IIA, Unanimity and Non-Dictatorship conditions can be defined for these more general functions such that their standard counterparts (see Definition \ref{definition:fairness-conditions}) can easily be recovered (see Definition \ref{definition:new-fairness-conditions} and Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-equivalence-2}). For example, dictators can be defined more generally as follows:
\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:dictator-generalised}
    A Social Welfare Function $\TWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \TPrefs$ \textit{has a dictator at $i$} if and only if $\forall p \in \PN$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\TWelfare(p) = \c \implies p_i = \i$
        \item If $p_i \neq \i$ then $\TWelfare(p) \leq p_i$
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
In other words, if a Social Welfare Function has a dictator at $i$ then as long as individual $i$ has any strict preferences, not only must the aggregate outcome not contradict the dictator, the aggregate outcome must not be a Condorcet Paradox.

\parsplit

Returning to the original task of defining an encoding function to instantiate our general theory to Social Choice Theory, we simply proceed with functions $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$. Every such function $\TWelfare$ corresponds to a Social Welfare Function by restricting the domain from $\TWelfare$ to $\PN$ --- we allow any behaviour of $\TWelfare$ outside $\PN$.\footnote{A canonical choice of $\TWelfare$'s behaviour outside $\PN$ is mapping any tuple in $\TProfiles$ that contains $\c$ in any of its coordinates to $\c$. In that context, $\c$ is often referred to as a \textit{gap-value} with respect to the Social Welfare Function. In Computer Science, this is analogous to the addition of \textit{null}, \textit{nothing} or \textit{undefined} values to the codomain of a computation (see~\cite{gaifman, gaps-and-gluts} for more detailed discussions of this concept).}

\parsplit

To conclude this section, we define additional operations on $\TPrefs$ that are needed to characterise incomputability in Social Choice Theory. Firstly, we observe for any two weak-linear orders $r, s \in \P$, there always exists a least upper bound $r \vee s$, which is the strictest preference relation that is no stricter than either of $r$ and $s$. Conversely, the greatest lower bound $r \wedge s$ --- if it exists --- is the least strict preference relation that is at least as strict as $r$ and $s$. The greatest lower bound $r \wedge s$ does not exist when $r$ and $s$ have opposing strict preferences, say, $a \prec b$ in $r$ and $b \prec a$ in $s$. These statements are proven using relational algebra in Propositions \ref{proposition:bounds} and \ref{proposition:missing-bounds}.

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    If $r$ represents $a_0 \prec a_1 \prec a_2$, and $s$ represents $a_1 \prec a_0 \prec a_2$ then $r \vee s$ represents $a_0 \sim a_1 \prec a_2$. Alternatively, if $r'$ represents $a_0 \sim a_1 \prec a_2$ and $s'$ represents $a_0 \prec a_1 \sim a_2$ then $r' \wedge s'$ represents $a_0 \prec a_1 \prec a_2$.
\end{example}

Because $r \vee s$ always exists, $\vee$ is equivalently a binary operation on $\P$, and more, a join semi-lattice (see Appendix \ref{appendix:semi-lattice} for prerequisites in lattice theory). Importantly, this means that because $\vee$ is a least-upper bound operation: $r \leq s \iff r \vee s = s$. We can then also extend the behaviour of $\vee$ and $\wedge$ to $\TPrefs$ as follows: for $\wedge$, $\forall r, s \in \TPrefs$ we set $r \wedge \c = \c \wedge s = \c$. This adds all missing greatest lower bounds to $\P$ (see Proposition \ref{proposition:missing-bounds}). Likewise, $(\TPrefs, \wedge)$ is then a meet semi-lattice satisfying $r \wedge s = r \iff r \leq s$. For $\vee$, we extend its behaviour on $\c$ such that it is no longer equivalent to taking least upper bounds in $(\TPrefs, \leq)$ as follows.
\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:veebar}
    Given $\TPrefs$ as in Definition \ref{definition:extending-order} and least upper bounds in $\P$ denoted by $\vee$, we define the binary operation $\veebar: \TPrefs \times \TPrefs \rightarrow \TPrefs$ by mappings:
    \begin{equation}
        r \veebar s = \begin{cases}
            r \vee s & \text{If $r \in \P$ and $s \in \P$}             \\
            \i       & \text{Otherwise, i.e., if $r = \c$ or $s = \c$}
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}
$(\TPrefs, \veebar)$ is not a join semi-lattice because $\c \veebar \c = \i$ violates the absorption condition of semi-lattices. The interpretation of $\veebar$ is that maintaining that $r \veebar s$ is to represent the least strict preference relation that does not contradict the preferences of $r$ combined with $s$: because all strict preferences hold (paradoxically) in $\c$, only a preference relation without strict preferences (i.e., $\i$) avoids contradicting $\c$ combined with any other preference relation. This definition of $\veebar$ has an important correspondence with our generalised definition of dictators in Definition \ref{definition:dictator-generalised}.
\begin{restatable}{proposition}{dictatorequivalence}
    \label{proposition:dictator-equivalence}
    A Social Welfare Function $\TWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \TPrefs$ has a dictator at $i$ if and only if $\forall p \in \PN$: $\TWelfare(p) \veebar p_i = p_i$.
\end{restatable}
\begin{proof}
    See Appendix \ref{appendix:result-seciton-proofs}.\\
\end{proof}

\subsection{\subsectiontextb}\label{subsection:first-def}

In this section, we define the fundamental object of our general theory of Incomputability: Self-Reference Systems. Then, we provide examples of Self Reference Systems in Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory used throughout the remainder of this paper.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:self-reference-system}
    A \textit{Self-Reference System} $(\mu, \Phi)$ is a combination of:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item A set $\C$ of \textit{constants}
        \item A set $\E$ of \textit{expressions}
        \item An \textit{encoding function} $\mu: \E \rightarrow \C$
        \item An \textit{application function} $\Phi: \E \times \C \rightarrow \E$
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:ast-shorthand}
    To reduce bracketing, we introduce a binary operation $\ast: \E \times \E \rightarrow \E$ defined by $e \ast f \coloneqq \Phi(e, \mu(f))$.
\end{note}

In the following examples Self-Reference Systems we will motivate our use of the phrase \say{Self-Reference}. In short, Self-Reference typically arises out of applying expressions to their own encoding, i.e., expressions of the form $e \ast e$.

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{example}[Self-Reference Systems in Arithmetic Logic]\label{example:godel-1}
        For all Arithmetic Logic examples in this paper, fixing a theory of Arithmetic Logic and a Gödel Numbering, we define a Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ by taking:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item The Natural Numbers $\N$ for constants.
            \item The Lindenbaum Algebra $\L_1$ for expressions (predicates and sentences, see Definition \ref{definition:lindenbaum-algebra-def}).
            \item Encoding $\NGodelvar: \L_1 \rightarrow \N$ mapping predicates to the Gödel numeral of the shortest equivalent formula\\(see Definition \ref{definition:enc})
            \item Application $\Phi: \L_1 \times \N \rightarrow \L_1$ defined by:
                  \begin{align*}
                      \Phi(B(x) & , n) \coloneqq B(\underline{n}) &  & \text{For predicates $B(x) \in \L_1$ and numeral $\underline{n} \in \L_0$} \\
                      \Phi(D    & , m) \coloneqq D                &  & \text{Otherwise, i.e., for sentences $D \in \L_1$}
                  \end{align*}
        \end{itemize}
        In this example, the application function $\Phi$ is analogous to substitution.
        In terms of self-reference, for any predicate $B(x) \in \L_1$, the formula $B(x) \ast B(x) = \Phi(B(x), \NGodel{B(x)}) = B(\DObar{B(x)})$ can be considered self-referential. This is because the predicate $B(\DObar{B(x)}) = B'(\Obar{B'(x)})$ --- for $B'(x)$ is the shortest formula logically equivalent to $B(x)$ --- \textit{refers} to its own Gödel numeral.
    \end{example}
\end{godelbox}

\begin{arrowbox}
    \begin{example}[Self-Reference Systems in Social Choice Theory]\label{example:arrow-1}
        Recall our definition of $\TPrefs$ as the set of weak linear orders on a fixed set of alternatives and Complete Condorcet Paradoxes (see Definition \ref{definition:extending-order}). Fixing an individual $i$, we define a Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi_i)$ by taking:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Individual preference relations: $\TPrefs$ for constants.
            \item Profiles (i.e., tuples) of $N$ preference relations: $\TProfiles$ for expressions.
            \item A (Social Welfare) function $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$ for encoding.
            \item Application $\Phi_i: \TProfiles \times \TPrefs \rightarrow \TProfiles$, defined by mapping any profile $p \coloneqq (p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N)$ and preference relation $r$ to:
                  \begin{equation*}
                      \Phi_i(\ (p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N),\ r\ ) \coloneqq (p_1, \dots, p_i \veebar r, \dots, p_N)
                  \end{equation*}
                  (see Definition \ref{definition:veebar}).
        \end{itemize}
        In this example, the application function $\Phi$ \textit{merges} preference relation $p_i$ with another preference relation $r$. In terms of self-reference, consider expressions $p \ast p$, which at coordinate $i$ combine $p_i$ (individual $i$) with the aggregate $\TWelfare(p)$, i.e., $p_i \veebar \TWelfare(p)$. We are interested in cases where there is a coupling between group preferences and an individual's preference, despite $\TWelfare(p)$ being completely determined by $p$. For example, by Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-equivalence} there is a dictator at $i$ when the following is always satisfied:
        \begin{equation*}
            \TWelfare(p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N) \leq p_i \qquad \text{or equivalently} \qquad p_i \veebar \TWelfare(p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N) = p_i
        \end{equation*}

        Here, the presence of $p_i$ on both sides represents the coupling between \textit{expression} and \textit{encoding}, which can be illustrated by telescoping at the $i$-th coordinate in a self-referential fashion as:
        \begin{equation*}
            \TWelfare(p_1, \dots, \TWelfare(p_1, \dots, \TWelfare(\dots), \dots, p_N), \dots, p_N) \leq p_i
        \end{equation*}
        Another Self-Reference we define is $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ where:
        \begin{equation*}
            \Psi_i(\ (p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N),\ r\ ) \coloneqq (p_1, \dots, p_i \wedge r, \dots, p_N)
        \end{equation*}
    \end{example}
\end{arrowbox}

Now that we have our primary examples of Self-Reference Systems in Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory, we may proceed to define additional properties that Self-Reference Systems may satisfy, instantiated to these domains.

\pagebreak

\subsection{\subsectiontextc}\label{subsection:fp-property}

For any Self-Reference System, we can define a fixed-point property, which when satisfied in a certain manner for the Self-Reference Systems of Arithmetic Logic (Example \ref{example:godel-1}), implies the fixed-point condition of the standard Diagonalisation Lemma (Lemma \ref{lemma:diag-lemma}). Moreover, the fixed-point property being satisfied in a certain manner for the Self-Reference Systems of Social Choice Theory (Example \ref{example:arrow-1}), is equivalent to saying the Social Welfare Function has a dictator.

\parsplit

We begin by defining the fixed-point property for Self-Reference Systems in general. Then, we restate the Diagonalisation Lemma in Arithmetic Logic, and the definition of a Dictator in Social Choice Theory in terms of statements about the fixed-point property holding for particular Self-Reference Systems.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:fixed-point-property}
    A Self-Reference System $(\mu, \Phi)$ \textit{satisfies the fixed point property for an expression} $e \in \E$ if there exists an $f \in \E$ such that $\Phi(e, \mu(f)) = f$ (or $e \ast f = f$ using the shorthand of Note \ref{note:ast-shorthand}).
\end{definition}

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{proposition}[The Diagonalisation as the Fixed-Point Property]\label{proposition:diagonalisation-lemma-instance}
        If the Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ of Example \ref{example:godel-1} satisfies the fixed point property by for all expressions in $\L_1$ by fixed-points in $\L_0 \subset \L_1$, the standard Diagonalisation Lemma holds.
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        Recall by the definition of $\NGodelvar$ (Definition \ref{definition:enc}) that for a formula $f$, we write $f'$ to denote the shortest formula logically equivalent to $f$. If for an arbitrary predicate $Q(x) \in \L_1$ there is a sentence $C \in \L_0$ such that $C = Q(x) \ast C = Q(\DObar{C})$, then by the definition of Lindenbaum Algebras this implies $\vdash Q(\Obar{C'}) \leftrightarrow C'$ in the logical theory, thus satisfying the standard Diagonalisation Lemma by the arbitrariness of $Q(X)$.\\
    \end{proof}

    The converse to Proposition \ref{proposition:diagonalisation-lemma-instance} does not necessarily hold. However, we are able to construct fixed-points to expressions in $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ regardless.

    \begin{restatable}{theorem}{arithmeticlogicfixedpoints}\label{theorem:arithmetic-logic-fixed-points}
        The Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ satisfies the fixed-point property for all expressions in $\L_1$ by fixed-points in $\L_0 \subset \L_1$.
    \end{restatable}
    \begin{proof}
        This result proven in Appendix \ref{appendix:abstract-diag} by showing it is a special case of a more general result concerning Self-Reference Systems that we call the \textit{Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma}. The Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma requires additional definitions and properties that span Appendices \ref{appendix:embeddings-and-composition}-\ref{appendix:abstract-diag}.\\
    \end{proof}
\end{godelbox}

\begin{arrowbox}
    \begin{proposition}[Dictators as the Fixed-Point Property]\label{proposition:dictator-as-fixed-point}
        Given the Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi_i)$ of Example \ref{example:arrow-1}, the social welfare function corresponding to $\TWelfare$ has a dictator at individual $i$ if and only if for every valid profile $p \in \PN$: $(\TWelfare, \Phi_i)$ satisfies the fixed-point property by $p$ itself, i.e., $p \ast p = p$.
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        Given an arbitrary $p = (p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N) \in \PN$, $p \ast p = p$ occurs if and only if:
        \begin{equation}
            (p_1, ..., p_i \veebar \TWelfare(p), ..., p_N) = (p_1, ..., p_i, ..., p_N)
        \end{equation}
        This occurs if and only if $p_i \veebar \TWelfare(p) = p_i$. But by the arbitrariness of $p$ this is equivalent to $\TWelfare$ having a dictator at $i$ by Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-equivalence}.\\
    \end{proof}

    In the Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ of Example \ref{example:arrow-1}, the same fixed-point property corresponds to individual $i$ being what is known as a \textit{vetoer}~\cite{blau-veto}, which we define below.
    \parsplit
    \begin{definition}\label{definition:vetoer}
        For a subset of profiles $D \subseteq \PN$ and Social Choice Function $\Welfare: D \rightarrow \P$, an individual $i$ is a \textit{vetoer} if for every profile $p \in D$ if $a \prec b$ holds strictly in $p_i$ then $b \nprec a$ in $\Welfare(p)$. Equivalently, $p_i \leq w(p)$, i.e., the individual $i$'s preferences are stricter than the aggregate's.
    \end{definition}
    In other words, individual $i$ \textit{vetoes} the welfare function in the sense that if it holds a strict preference, the aggregate preference does not necessarily have to corroborate it (i.e., the aggregate preference may be indifferent) but the aggregate preference must not contradict it (i.e., the aggregate preference must not be strictly opposite to the vetoer's preference).

    \parsplit
    \begin{proposition}[Vetoers as the Fixed-Point Property]\label{proposition:vetoer-as-fixed-point}
        Given the Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ of Example \ref{example:arrow-1}, the social welfare function corresponding to $\TWelfare$ has a vetoer at individual $i$ if and only if for every valid profile $p \in \PN$ such that $\TWelfare(p) \neq \c$: $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ satisfies the fixed-point property by $p$ itself, i.e., $p \ast p = p$.
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        Given an arbitrary $p = (p_1, \dots, p_i, \dots, p_N) \in \PN$ such that $\TWelfare(p) \neq \c$, $p \ast p = p$ occurs if and only if:
        \begin{equation}
            (p_1, ..., p_i \wedge \TWelfare(p), ..., p_N) = (p_1, ..., p_i, ..., p_N)
        \end{equation}
        This occurs if and only if $p_i \wedge \TWelfare(p) = p_i$, which is equivalent to $p_i \leq w(p)$ by $(\TPrefs, \wedge)$ being a meet semi-lattice. Hence, by the arbitrariness of $p$, and Definition \ref{definition:vetoer}, individual $i$ is a vetoer.\\
    \end{proof}
\end{arrowbox}

We have shown that key components of Incomputability results in Arithmetic Logic (The Diagonalisation Lemma) and Social Choice Theory (The Existence of a Dictator) correspond to the fixed-point property being satisfied in a particular manner for particular Self-Reference Systems. However, to characterise incomputability, we proceed to define a notion of consistency between pairs of expressions, wherein consistency can be interpreted \textit{within} a Self-Reference System.

\subsection{\subsectiontextf}\label{subsection:truth-predicates}

In this section, we characterise incomputability in Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory in terms of \textit{Consistent Subsets} of expressions. A pair of expressions in the set are \textit{consistent} if holding them together (e.g., by logical conjunction) yields another expression in the consistent subset, the pair is called \textit{inconsistent} otherwise. \textit{Consistency-respecting} expressions are those that maintain certain facets of the consistency relationship by application with encodings alone. The incomputability of particular consistency-respecting expressions will characterise Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem and Arrow's Impossibility Theorem.

\parsplit

We begin with the definition of consistent subsets as a general construction on a semi-lattice (see Appendix \ref{appendix:semi-lattice} for prerequisites). Then, we identify consistent subsets of expressions in examples of Self-Reference Systems in Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:me}
    Given a set $S$ and a meet semi-lattice $(S, \wedge)$ with bottom element $\bot \in S$, a \textit{consistent subset} of $S$ with respect to $\wedge$ is a choice of a subset $C \subseteq S \setminus \{\bot\}$. For any $s, t \in C$, we say that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $s$ and $t$ are \textit{consistent} if $s \wedge t \in C$, and say that $s$ and $t$ are \textit{inconsistent} otherwise.
        \item An inconsistent pair $s$ and $t$ is \textit{contradictory} if $s \wedge t = \bot$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{example}[Consistent Subsets in Arithmetic Logic]\label{example:godel-consistent-pairs}
        In Arithmetic Logic, we take the non-contradictory sentences as our consistent subset, i.e., $\L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ with respect to logical conjunction $\wedge$. In this case, a pair of sentences is inconsistent if and only if they are contradictory. This is in contrast to certain non-classical logics, where inconsistency and contradiction in the sense of Definition \ref{definition:me} are not necessarily equivalent. For example, in Paraconsistent logics, an inconsistent formula $f \wedge \neg f$ is not necessarily logically equivalent to $\bot$ may be both true and false by not explosive (see~\cite[Section 4]{sep-contradiction}).
    \end{example}
\end{godelbox}

\begin{arrowbox}
    \begin{example}[Consistent Subsets in Social Choice Theory]\label{example:soc-me}
        In Social Choice Theory, recall that there is a meet semi-lattice $(\TPrefs, \wedge)$ on preference relations with bottom element $\c$ representing Complete Condorcet Paradoxes (see Definition \ref{definition:complete-condorcet}). Likewise, there is a meet semi-lattice $(\TProfiles, \bwedge)$ for $\bwedge$ defined as the coordinate-wise application of $\wedge$, and bottom element $(\c, \dots, \c)$. In this case, we use the consistent subset $\PN \subset \TProfiles \setminus \{(\c,\dots,\c)\}$. A pair of profiles $p, q \in \PN$ are consistent if $\forall i$: $p_i \leq q_i$ or $q_i \leq p_i$, i.e., one preference relation has all the strict relations of the other. Equivalently, $p$ and $q$ are inconsistent if there exists an individual $i$ and alternatives $a, b$ such that $a \prec b$ in $p_i$ and $b \prec a$ in $q_i$. Equivalently, $p_i \wedge q_i = \c$. Finally, profiles $p$ and $q$ are contradictory if for every individual $i$: $p_i \wedge q_i = \c$.
    \end{example}
\end{arrowbox}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:consistency-respecting}
    Given a Self-Reference System $(\mu, \Phi)$, a semi-lattice $(\E, \wedge)$ on expressions and a consistent subset $\D \subseteq \E$ with respect to $\wedge$, we say that an expression $\T \in \E$ is \textit{consistency-respecting} if $\forall d, d' \in \D$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $d$ and $d'$ are contradictory implies $\T \ast d$ and $\T \ast d'$ are inconsistent (but not necessarily contradictory).
        \item $\T \ast d$ and $\T \ast d'$ are inconsistent implies $d$ and $d'$ are inconsistent.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{note}
    The intuition behind the first condition of Definition \ref{definition:consistency-respecting} is that the encodings of contradictory pairs may not retain enough information about the pair for $\T$ to decode that property, however, decoding at least that the pair was inconsistent is essential. This will be particularly relevant in our applications to Social Choice Theory.
\end{note}

\parsplit
\begin{note}
    Other criteria could have been incorporated in our definition of consistency-respecting expressions and satisfied in our examples e.g., the criteria that $d$ and $d'$ are consistent implies $\T \ast d$ and $\T \ast d'$ are consistent. However, such criteria have been excluded due to being unnecessary to prove our main results (Theorem \ref{theorem:godel-main} and \ref{theorem:arrow-main}).
\end{note}

In Arithmetic Logic, we show a provability predicate is consistency-respecting in $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ with respect to $\L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ and $\wedge$ (see Examples \ref{example:godel-1} and \ref{example:godel-consistent-pairs}) if and only if the provability predicate is \textit{weakly $\omega$-consistent} (see Definition \ref{definition:weak-omega-consistency}). Furthermore, given a weakly $\omega$-consistent provability predicate, if the theory is both consistent and complete, a contradiction follows. The mutual exclusivity of consistency and completeness is the essence of Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem. Then, in Social Choice Theory, we will show that the incomputability (i.e., existence of Condorcet Paradoxes) that follows from Arrow's Impossibility Theorem implies that no consistency-respecting expression can exist for \textit{any} Self-Reference System that encodes with that Social Welfare Function. Conversely, for a certain type of Dictator, certain consistency-respecting expressions \textit{must} exist.

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{proposition}[Provability Predicates]\label{proposition:provability-predicate}
        Given the Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ (see Example \ref{example:godel-1}), its provability predicate $P(x) \coloneqq Provable(x)$ in $\L_1$ (see Section \ref{subsection:background-godel}) satisfies:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\forall D \in \L_0$:\ $D \leq P(\DObar{D})$ (i.e., a proof of sentence $D$ implies $D$ is provable).
            \item $\neg P(\DObar{\bot}) = \top$ (i.e., contradictions are not provable.).
        \end{itemize}
        Furthermore, if the underlying Arithmetic Logic:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item has a \textit{weakly-$\omega$-consistent} provability predicate $P(x)$ if and only if for every contradictory pair\\ $A, B \in \L_0$: $P(\DObar{A}) \leq \neg P(\DObar{B})$ (i.e., if $A$ is provable, no sentence that contradicts $A$ is provable).
            \item is \textit{complete} if for every $D \in \L_0$: $\neg P(\DObar{D}) \wedge \neg P(\DObar{\neg D}) = \bot$ (i.e., there is no sentence $D$ such that neither it nor its negation is provable).
        \end{itemize}
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        These are basic equivalence of our Arithmetic Logic definitions (see Section \ref{subsection:background-godel}), and our Algebraic Logic definitions (see Section \ref{subsection:encodings}).\\
    \end{proof}

    \parsplit
    \begin{note}
        Because expressions of the form $P(\DObar{D})$ can be written as $P(x) \ast D$ in the Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$, the properties of Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate} may instead be treated as a definition of an \textit{abstract provability predicate} for Self-Reference Systems in general (see Definition \ref{definition:proposition-as-definition}).
    \end{note}

    We proceed to show that in $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$, the provability predicate being weakly $\omega$-consistent (see Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate}) is equivalent to it being consistency-respecting expression. In order to prove this, we need the following lemma of classical logic.

    \begin{restatable}{lemma}{classicallogicfacts}
        \label{lemma:classical-logic-facts} For $A, B \in \L_0$: $A \wedge B = \bot \iff A \leq \neg B$.
    \end{restatable}
    \begin{proof}
        See Appendix \ref{appendix:result-seciton-proofs}.\\
    \end{proof}

    \parsplit
    \begin{proposition}[Provability Predicates as Consistency-Respecting Expressions]\label{proposition:provability-predicate-equivalence}
        Given the Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ and a provability predicate $P(x) \in \L_1$ is weakly $\omega$-consistent if and only if it is consistency-respecting with respect to the consistent subset $\L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ and $\wedge$.
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        Because inconsistent pairs $A, B \in \L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ are also contradictory pairs and vice versa (see Example \ref{example:godel-consistent-pairs}), $P(x)$ is consistency-respecting with respect to $\L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ if and only if $A \wedge B = \bot \iff P(\DObar{A}) \wedge P(\DObar{\neg B}) = \bot$ holds. By Lemma \ref{lemma:classical-logic-facts} we have that for any contradictory pair $A$ and $B$, $P(\DObar{A}) \wedge P(\DObar{\neg B}) = \bot \iff P(\DObar{A}) \leq \neg P(\DObar{B})$, which is precisely the weak $\omega$-consistency condition (see Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate}).\\
    \end{proof}

    Finally, we show that Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem --- that no $\omega$-consistent theory of Arithmetic Logic can be complete --- is equivalent to the statement that if $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ has a consistency-respecting provability predicate, it is incomplete

    \parsplit
    \begin{theorem}[Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem]\label{theorem:godel-main}
        An $\omega$-consistent theory of Arithmetic Logic cannot be complete.
    \end{theorem}
    \begin{proof}
        \allowdisplaybreaks
        Let $P(x)$ be a provability predicate and assume to the contrary that $\L_1$ is complete. By Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate} two inequalities follow. Firstly, by completeness: $\forall D \in \L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$: $D \leq P(\DObar{D})$, and by $\omega$-consistency implying $P(x)$ is weakly $\omega$-consistent (see Note \ref{note:intro-weak-omega-consistency}): $P(\DObar{D}) \leq \neg P(\DObar{\neg D})$. Combining these two inequalities, we have $D \leq \neg P(\DObar{\neg D})$ (i.e., $D$ implies $\neg D$ is not provable). By Theorem \ref{theorem:arithmetic-logic-fixed-points} there exists a fixed-point $\G = \neg P(\DObar{\G})$. The following derivation shows that $\G$ must be logically equivalent to $\bot$.
        \begin{align*}
            \bot  \quad  = \quad & \neg P(\DObar{\G}) \wedge \neg P(\DObar{\neg \G}) &  & \text{Assumption of completeness}                                                 \\
            \quad  = \quad       & \G \wedge \neg P(\DObar{\neg \G})                 &  & \text{Definition of $\G$ as a fixed-point of $\neg P(\DObar{-})$}                 \\
            \quad  \geq \quad    & \G \wedge \G                                      &  & \text{By $\forall D \in \L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}:\ D \leq \neg P(\DObar{\neg D})$} \\
            \quad  = \quad       & \G                                                &  & \text{Absorption property of $\wedge$}
        \end{align*}
        However, $\bot$ being a bottom element of $\L_1$ means $\bot \geq \G \implies \G = \bot$. Finally, combining $\G = \bot$ with $\G = \neg P(\DObar{\G})$ and $\neg P(\DObar{\bot}) = \top$ (see Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate}), we attain the following contradiction:
        \begin{equation*}
            \bot = \G = \neg P(\DObar{\G}) = \neg P(\DObar{\bot}) = \top
        \end{equation*}
    \end{proof}
    \parsplit
    \begin{corollary}
        Any complete theory of Arithmetic Logic cannot have a consistency-respecting provability predicate with respect to the consistent subset $\L_0 \setminus \{\bot\}$ and $\wedge$.
    \end{corollary}
    \begin{proof}
        We simply apply Proposition \ref{proposition:provability-predicate-equivalence} to Theorem \ref{theorem:godel-main}, noting only weak $\omega$-consistency of $P(x)$ was invoked in place of $\omega$-consistency of the whole theory.\\
    \end{proof}
\end{godelbox}

To demonstrate incomputability in Social Choice Theory in the same terms, we proceed by showing Arrow's Impossibility Theorem is equivalent to the statement that Social Welfare Functions satisfying IIA, Unanimity and Non-Dictatorship necessarily produce Complete Condorcet Paradoxes. Then, we show that no Self-Reference System using that Social Welfare Function as its encoding can have a consistency-respecting expression due to the existence of these Complete Condorcet Paradoxes.

\begin{arrowbox}
    \begin{restatable}[Arrow's Impossibility Theorem]{theorem}{arrowfull}
        \label{theorem:arrow-full}
        If a Social Welfare Function $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$ satisfies Unanimity, IIA and Non-Dictatorship then there exist profiles $q, q' \in \PN$ such that:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item $\TWelfare(q) = \TWelfare(q') = \c$
            \item $q \bwedge q' = (\c, \dots, \c)$
        \end{enumerate}
        In other words, there exists a pair profiles contradictory to one another that each map to a Condorcet Paradox.
    \end{restatable}
    \begin{proof}
        This result is a generalisation of D'Antoni's proof for strict preferences in~\cite{arrow-simple}. We prove this result in Appendix \ref{appendix:arrow-proof}.\\
    \end{proof}
    These conditions further imply incomputability as follows:

    \parsplit
    \begin{theorem}\label{theorem:arrow-main}
        If $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$ is a Social Welfare Function satisfying Unanimity and IIA and Non-Dictatorship, then no Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi)$ has a consistency-respecting expression with respect to $\PN \subseteq \TProfiles \setminus \{\c,\dots,\c\}$ and $\bwedge$.
    \end{theorem}
    \begin{proof}
        Assume to the contrary that $\TWelfare$ does not have a dictator but that there exists a Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi)$ with a consistency-respecting $\T$. By Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-full}: $\exists q, q' \in \PN$ such that $q$ and $q'$ are contradictory and $\TWelfare(q) = \TWelfare(q') = \c$. By condition (1) of $\T$ being consistency-respecting, $\T \ast q$ and $\T \ast q'$ are inconsistent. Moreover, we have that $\T \ast q = \Phi(\T, \TWelfare(q)) = \Phi(\T, \c) = \Phi(\T, \TWelfare(q')) = \T \ast q'$, which implies $\T \ast q$ is inconsistent with itself. If $\T \ast q$ is inconsistent with itself, then condition (2) of $\T$ being consistency-respecting implies that $q$ is inconsistent with itself as well. However, if $q$ is inconsistent with itself, for some coordinate $j$: $q_j \wedge q_j = \c$, which implies that $q_j = \c$, but this contradicts our assumption that $q \in \PN$.\\
    \end{proof}

    On the other hand, it is possible to define a consistency-respecting expression on Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi_i)$ (see Example \ref{example:arrow-1}) when $\TWelfare$ has a special type of dictator which we call a \textit{Strong Dictator}. Strong Dictators are those that the aggregate choice (if valid) always exactly mirrors the dictator's preferences (rather than the dictator's preferences merely being stricter than the aggregate's). We formally define Strong Dictators as follows.

    \parsplit
    \begin{definition}\label{definition:strong-dictator}
        A Social Welfare Function $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$ has a \textit{Strong Dictator} at $i$ if and only if $\forall p \in \PN$: $\TWelfare(p) = p_i$. A String Dictator is equivalently an individual that is both a dictator and a vetoer (see Definitions \ref{definition:dictator-generalised} and \ref{definition:vetoer}).
    \end{definition}

    \parsplit
    \begin{proposition}\label{proposition:dictator-consistency-respecting}
        A Social Welfare Function $\TWelfare: \TProfiles \rightarrow \TPrefs$ has a Strong Dictator at $i$ then Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ (see Example \ref{example:arrow-1}) has a consistency-respecting expression given by $\T \coloneqq (\i, \dots, \i$).
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        We prove this by verifying that $\T \coloneqq (\i, \dots, \i)$ satisfies the two conditions of consistency-respecting expressions (see Definition \ref{definition:me}) as follows. (1) If the profiles $p, q \in \PN$ are a contradictory pair of expressions then we must show that $\T \ast p$ and $\T \ast q$ are inconsistent. We first note that $p$ and $q$ being contradictory means that $p_i \wedge q_i = \c$. Combining this with $\TWelfare$ having a Strong Dictator at $i$, we have $\TWelfare(p) = p_i$ and $\TWelfare(q) = q_i$, so $\TWelfare(p) \wedge \TWelfare(q) = p_i \wedge q_i = \c$. Then, observing that $\T \ast p = (\i, \dots, \i \wedge \TWelfare(p), \dots, \i) = (\i, \dots, \TWelfare(p), \dots, \i)$, and similarly for $q$, $\T \ast p$ and $\T \ast q$ are inconsistent because:
        \begin{equation*}
            (\T \ast p) \wedge (\T \ast q) = (\i \wedge \i, \dots, \TWelfare(p) \wedge \TWelfare(q), \dots, \i \wedge \i) = (\i, \dots, \c, \dots, \i)
        \end{equation*}
        because $(\i, \dots, \c, \dots, \i) \notin \PN$ (i.e., is  not in the consistent subset $\PN$) as desired.\\

        \noindent (2) If $\T \ast p$ and $\T \ast q$ are inconsistent then it must be the case that $\TWelfare(p) \wedge \TWelfare(q) = \c$ because every other coordinate of $(\T \ast p) \wedge (\T \ast q)$ is $\i$. However, if $\TWelfare(p) \wedge \TWelfare(q) = \c$ then by $\TWelfare$ having a strong dictator at $i$ it must also be the case that $p_i \wedge q_i = \c$, which means $p$ and $q$ are inconsistent as desired.\\
    \end{proof}
\end{arrowbox}

We have thus related Incomputability in Arithmetic Logic and Impossibility in Social Choice Theory in terms of the incomputability of consistency-respecting expressions in Self-Reference Systems. In Arithmetic Logic, the assumption of completeness prohibits the existence of a Provability Predicate due to contradictions that follow as a result of the Provability Predicate being consistency-respecting (Theorem \ref{theorem:godel-main}). In Social Choice Theory, a Social Welfare Function that satisfies Unanimity, IIA and Non-Dictatorship produce Complete Condorcet Paradoxes (Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-full}). Moreover, no consistency-respecting expression can exist for any Self-Reference System that has the Social Welfare Function as its encoding function (Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-main}).

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{section:discussion}

Gödel's (First) Incompleteness Theorem maintains an ever-growing relevance to Computer Science, largely due to its correspondence to theorems about the non-existence of algorithms for solving particular problems i.e., the incomputability of those problems. Incomputability results in Computer Science are valuable due to their ability to inform practitioners whether they are attempting to solve problems that are equivalent to well-known unsolvable problems~\cite{hoare-incomputability}. For example, the incomputability of certain fluid flows~\cite{fluid-flows}, ray-tracing paths in computer graphics~\cite{ray-tracing}, and air travel planning optimisations~\cite{air-travel-planning} have all been shown to be equivalent to solving the incomputable Halting Problem.

\parsplit

Impossibility results in Social Choice Theory such as Arrow's Impossibility Theorem are crucial to Economics because they reveal inherent limitations in the design of decision-making systems that aggregate individual preferences into collective choices. They also have applied implications, by informing practitioners what trade-offs have to be considered, for example, when developing voting methods~\cite{intro-to-voting-theory}, land management policy~\cite{land-management}, and economic indicators~\cite{social-welfare-gdp}.

\parsplit

It has long been conjectured that there is a formal relationship between Arrow's Impossibility Theorem and Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem, which reflects that both  incomputability results can be considered as a failure of axiomatisation~\cite{arrow-godel-conjecture}. In this paper, we have confirmed this long standing conjecture, by deriving a formal relationship between the two results in terms of a specific mathematical object we introduced, called a \textit{Self-Reference System}. We were able to instantiate Self-Reference Systems in the fields of Arithmetic Logic and Social Choice Theory. Importantly, we were able to use the same general properties of Self-Reference Systems to characterise both Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem and Arrow's Impossibility Theorem, respectively.

\parsplit

The overlap between the Self-Reference Systems underlying Arrow's Impossibility Theorem and Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem primarily utilised abstract notions of encoding functions, consistency between expressions, and \textit{consistency-respecting expressions} that can decode consistency relationships from \textit{within} a Self-Reference System. Specifically, we reinterpreted Social Welfare Functions as encoding functions from profiles (i.e., collections of individual preferences) to group preferences, just as Gödel Numbering is often interpreted as an encoding function from statements of arithmetic to numbers. We defined inconsistency in Social Choice Theory as a kind of Condorcet Paradox, just as inconsistency in Arithmetic Logic is defined as a logical contradiction (i.e., a paradox). This allowed us to develop a new proof of Arrow's Impossibility Theorem (\ref{theorem:arrow-full}) expressed explicitly in terms of Condorcet Paradoxes and a specific notion of consistency, generalising D'Antoni's beyond the strict case \cite{arrow-simple}.

\parsplit

Our main results (Section \ref{section:results}) culminated in showing that Arrow's Impossibility Theorem, and the assumption of consistency and completeness in Arithmetic Logic, correspond to the impossibility of consistency-respecting expressions (Theorems \ref{theorem:godel-main} and \ref{theorem:arrow-main}). Moreover, we demonstrated in each setting that the expressions that make consistency-respecting expressions incomputable are Self-Referential. In Social Choice Theory, we showed that the self-referential expressions are profiles that aggregate to \textit{Complete Condorcet Paradoxes}, which are defined by every alternative being strictly preferred to themselves (Definition \ref{definition:complete-condorcet}). In Arithmetic Logic, identified the self-referential expressions as \textit{Gödel Sentences}, which are propositions logically equivalent to their non-provability.

\parsplit

There is also a more subtle overlap between the two fields in terms of diagonalisation and fixed-point arguments (see Appendix \ref{appendix:diagonalisation}). In Arithmetic Logic, the fixed-point property of Self-Reference Systems corresponded to the Diagonalisation Lemma (Theorem \ref{theorem:arithmetic-logic-fixed-points}), which was used to produce a Gödel Sentence, which in turn was instrumental to proving incompleteness. On the other hand, in Social Choice Theory, the fixed-point property of Self-Reference Systems corresponded to the existence of a dictator (Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-as-fixed-point}), which \textit{allows} rather than \textit{disallows} consistency-respecting expressions to be computable (Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-consistency-respecting}). An intuition for these differing roles of fixed-points arises out of viewing the requirement of fixed-points in a Self-Reference System as a \textit{constraint} that certain expressions must be computable by fixed-points. This constraint in Arithmetic Logic (i.e., the Diagonalisation Lemma) yields incompleteness by requiring Gödel Sentences exist. In Social Choice Theory, this constraint (i.e., the existence of a dictator) limits the Social Welfare Function to the point that consistency-respecting expressions may be computed. In the extreme case of constraining a Social Welfare Function to the point of having a Strong Dictator (Definition \ref{definition:strong-dictator}), a consistency-respecting expression \textit{must} be computable. Thus, the constraining effect of fixed-points may either yield or prevent incomputability.

\parsplit

Before concluding, we outline a number of promising topics of further research towards the theory of Self-Reference Systems and its applications. Our approach to developing the theory of Self-Reference Systems primarily involved generalising concepts from logic and computability theory, and characterising Social Choice Theory in those terms. These concepts include encoding, diagonalisation, fixed-point arguments, consistency, etc. This approach was powerful enough to express a proof of Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem in the language of Self-Reference Systems (Theorem \ref{theorem:godel-main}). On the other hand, another promising approach is generalising concepts from Social Choice Theory in the language of Self-Reference Systems in order to characterise problems in logic and computability theory.

\parsplit

The theory of Self-Reference Systems may also be developed by identifying additional application domains to those of this paper; we outline three avenues towards further application domains. The first avenue is to recast other well-known, impossibility or fixed-point results in terms of Self-Reference Systems, as we did for Arrow's Impossibility Theorem. For example, Chichilnisky's Impossibility Theorem of topological Social Choice Theory~\cite{chichilnisky-heal}, or the computability (i.e., existence) of Nash Equilibria (see~\cite{binmore} for a discussion of the relation of the concept to Diagonalisation). The second avenue is to investigate the overlap of the theory of Self-Reference System with existing general theories of Diagonalisation and Fixed-Point arguments. An example of such a general theory is Lawvere's Fixed-Point theorem~\cite{Lawvere}, which already has extensive applications~\cite{yanofsky}. However, Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem concerns functions with signature $\E \times \E \rightarrow \C$ rather than the signature $\E \times \C \rightarrow \E$, which is used for application functions in Self-Reference Systems (see Theorem \ref{theorem:lawvere-fixed-point}). Thus, further generalisations of the theory of Self-Reference Systems may be required. The third avenue is to investigate the overlap of the theory of Self-Reference System with general theories of self-reference and self-reproduction. For example, Moss' Equational Logic of Self-Expression~\cite{moss}, Kauffman's Paired Categories~\cite{categorical-pairs} and Gonda et al.'s Simulators in Target Context Categories~\cite{gonda}.

\pagebreak

To conclude, by introducing a theory of Self-Reference Systems, we were able to characterise impossibility in Social Choice Theory as the impossibility of a system to interpret its own internal consistency due to the existence of self-referential paradoxes. We were also able to provide a proof of Gödel's Incompleteness Theorem in the same terms. Together, this constitutes a recasting of Arrow's Impossibility Theorem as incomputability in the Gödelian sense. Thus, we have broadened the scope of incomputability studies to include problems of Social-Decision Making. Abstracting these concepts in search of a more general foundation of computability may facilitate the cross-pollination of methods from fields with incomputability results.

