\subsection{Diagonalisation and Fixed-Point Arguments}\label{appendix:diagonalisation}

Diagonalisation is a proof-technique characterised by the use of self-application or self-description, traditionally, to demonstrate whether two sets are in bijective correspondence~\cite{jacquette-diagonalisation,simmons-good-bad-diagonalisation}. In this section, we begin by outlining the basic structure of diagonalisation arguments through three well-known examples. Then, we will outline the relationship between diagonalisation arguments and fixed-point arguments.

\parsplit

Diagonalisation originates in Cantor's Theorem~\cite{cantor}, which states that there is no bijection between the set $\N$ of natural numbers and the set $P(\N)$ of all subsets of natural numbers (e.g., $\{2, 5, 1\} \subseteq \N$). In other words, the statement that $P(\N)$ is \textit{uncountable}. To prove this, we assume to the contrary that $P(\N)$ is countable. This means we can enumerate all the subsets of natural numbers as sets $E_0, E_1, E_2, \ldots $, etc. Given a subset of natural numbers $S \in P(\N)$, we write $\Obar{S} \in \N$ to denote the natural number such that $E_{\Obar{S}} = S$. Additionally, we refer to the number $\Obar{S} $ as a \textit{referent} of the \textit{expression} $S \in P(\N)$. Using this notation, we build an \textit{expression-referent grid} (see Table \ref{table:diagonalisation-table}). The rows and columns are indexed by the expressions $E_0, E_1, \dots$, and referents $\Obar{E_0}, \Obar{E_1}, \dots$, respectively. For each expression $E_i$ and referent $\Obar{E_j}$, the $i$-$j$ entry of the grid is denoted as $P_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$, where $P_{ij} = 1$ if $i \in E_j$, and $P_{ij} = 0$ otherwise.

\begin{table}[ht!]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{c | *{6}{c}}
        \multicolumn{1}{c}{} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\Obar{E_0}$} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\Obar{E_1}$} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\Obar{E_2}$} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\cdots$} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\Obar{E_k}$} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{$\cdots$} \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $E_0$                & $P_{00}$                         & $P_{01}$                         & $P_{02}$                         & $\cdots$                     & $P_{0k}$                         & $\cdots$                     \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $E_1$                & $P_{10}$                         & $P_{11}$                         & $P_{12}$                         & $\cdots$                     & $P_{1k}$                         & $\cdots$                     \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $E_2$                & $P_{20}$                         & $P_{21}$                         & $P_{22}$                         & $\cdots$                     & $P_{2k}$                         & $\cdots$                     \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $\vdots$             & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                         & $\ddots$                     & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                     \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $E_k$                & $P_{k0}$                         & $P_{k1}$                         & $P_{k2}$                         & $\cdots$                     & $P_{kk}$                         & $\cdots$                     \\
        \cline{2-7}
        $\vdots$             & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                         & $\vdots$                     & $\vdots$                         & $\ddots$                     \\
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{An Expression-Referent Grid.}
    \label{table:diagonalisation-table}
\end{table}

We will prove $P(\N)$ is uncountable (i.e., that the enumeration $E_0, E_1, \dots$, etc. is incomplete) by finding an $i$ and $j$ such that $P_{ij}$ cannot be determined. To do this, we use the \textit{diagonal} entries to construct the set $D \coloneqq \{k \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ P_{kk} = 0\}$. Because $D \subseteq \N$, i.e., $D \in P(\N)$, it must equal some $E_x$. However, any attempt to evaluate $P_{xx}$ leads to a contradiction, i.e., $P_{xx} = 1$ if and only if $x \in E_x$, which by definition of $E_x$ occurs if and only if $x \notin E_x$ i.e., $P_{xx} = 0$.\\\qed

\parsplit

Diagonalisation may involve more general choices of expressions and referents. This can be seen in a proof of the uncountability of the real numbers $\mathbb{R}$, which also originates with Cantor~\cite{cantor}. Here, we also build an expression-referent grid with rows indexed by a hypothetical enumeration of the real numbers $R_0, R_1, \dots$, etc. (between $0$ and $1$ for convenience), and a rather different choice of column indices. Each column index corresponds to a decimal place of each real number's decimal expansion. For example, if $R_i = \frac{\pi}{10} = 0.314159\dots$, $P_{i0} = 3$, $P_{i1} = 1$, $P_{i2} = 4$, etc. Just as we proved Cantor's Theorem, we prove the uncountability of $\mathbb{R}$ by using the diagonal entries to identify an entry $P_{ij}$ that cannot be determined. Indeed, if we modify each diagonal entry $P_{kk}$ to produce a new value $Q_{kk}$ (e.g., setting $Q_{kk} = P_{kk} + 1\ mod\ 10$) then there must be some real number with decimal expansion $Q_{00}, Q_{11}, \dots$, which must correspond to some $R_x$ in our enumeration of the reals.

\parsplit

However, this is impossible because at the $x^{th}$ decimal place $R_x$ is both equal to $P_{xx}$ and $Q_{xx}$, which are not equal by construction.\\\qed

\parsplit

Diagonalisation may also involve row and column indices that are a priori known to be uncountable. In other words, reasoning about a hypothetical grid and its diagonal are merely illustrations of the argument. This can be seen in the following proof of Russell's paradox~\cite{principles-of-mathematics}, which demonstrates any axiomatisation of set theory that allows sets to be members of themselves leads to a contradiction. To argue this by Diagonalisation, obviously, we cannot construct a literal expression-referent grid as the totality of sets is certainly uncountable. However, we instead metaphorically consider all sets $S, T, U, \dots$ as our rows, the same for our columns, and for sets $S, T$, we define entries $P_{ST}$, where $P_{ST} = 1$ if $S \in T$ and $0$ otherwise. Then, via the \textit{diagonal}, we can define a set $R \coloneqq \{S\ |\ P_{SS} = 0\}$, i.e. \textit{the set of sets which do not contain themselves}. Any evaluation of $P_{RR}$ leads to a contradiction.\\\qed

\parsplit

To summarise, the three Diagonalisation proofs we outlined used varying degrees of Self-Reference, ranging from \textit{literal self-reference} in Russell's Paradox (i.e., sets could literally be members of themselves), to \textit{indirect self-reference} in Cantor's Theorem (i.e., numbers merely indexed sets that could be understood to contain that index), and arguably no self-reference in the proof of the uncountability of the real numbers. However, these proofs followed very similar steps, this similarity can be formalised by understanding Diagonalisation arguments as Fixed-Point arguments as follows.

\parsplit

The key insight is recognising that the assignment of row-column indices to the cells in an expression-referent grid, i.e., the mapping $(i, j) \mapsto P_{ij}$ defines a function $entry: Expressions \times Referents \rightarrow Properties$. For example, in our proofs of the uncountability of $P(\N)$ and $\mathbb{R}$, the $entry$ function was a $\N \times \N \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ function. Moreover, each row $r$ corresponds to a function $r: Referents \rightarrow Properties$. Likewise, the diagonal corresponds to a function $diag: Expressions \rightarrow Properties$. However, when there was (or we assumed there was) a bijection $Expressions \cong Referents$, the diagonal also corresponds to a row, as do certain modifications of the diagonal entries. In each case we reached a contradiction by showing the row $diag$ corresponds to a row that could not have been part of the original grid; the mechanism underlying this contradiction is captured by Lawvere \cite{Lawvere} as follows:

\parsplit
\begin{theorem}[Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem]\label{theorem:lawvere-fixed-point}
    For any sets $E$ and $P$, let $P^E$ be the set of all $E \rightarrow P$ functions. If there exists a surjection $row: E \rightarrow P^E$ then every function $\sigma: P \rightarrow P$ has a fixed point, i.e., a $p \in P$ such that $\sigma(p) = p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Given an arbitrary surjection $row$ and an arbitrary function $\sigma$ as above. We begin by defining a function $entry: E \times E \rightarrow P$ by the mapping $(i, j) \mapsto row(i)(j)$ (recalling $row(i)$ is an $E \rightarrow P$ function so that $row(i)(j) \in P$). We can also define a function $g: E \rightarrow P$ defined by the mapping $e \mapsto \sigma(entry(e, e))$. If $row$ is surjective, there must be some element $x \in E$ such that $row(x) = g$, and we find a fixed-point of $\sigma$ in $entry(x, x) = row(x)(x) = g(x) = \sigma(entry(x, x))$.\\
\end{proof}

One can easily rewrite our three diagonalisation proofs as contradictions that follow from an application of Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem. For example, in the case of Cantor's Theorem, the assumption that every subset of $\N$ corresponds to a row in the $\N \times \N$ grid of $\{0,1\}$ values is precisely the assumption that there is a surjection $row: N \rightarrow \{0,1\}^\N$. However, by Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem this would imply that every $\{0,1\} \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ function has a fixed point. This is obviously not the case as the \textit{negation} function $\neg$ defined by $\neg(0) = 1$ and $\neg(1) = 0$ does not have a fixed-point. In fact, the function $g$ in the proof of Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem exactly corresponds to the subset (i.e., row) $\{k \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ P_{kk}\}$ used in our earlier proof of Cantor's Theorem. See, Yanofsky~\cite{yanofsky} for a survey of well-known diagonalisation arguments reinterpreted using Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem.

\subsection{Equivalence Relations}\label{appendix:equivalence}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Binary Relations]
    For any set $S$, a (binary) relation $R$ on $S$ is a subset $R \subseteq S \times S$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Equivalence Relations]
    For any set $S$, a relation $R$ on $S$ is an \textit{equivalence relation} if it is:
    \begin{adjustwidth}{5em}{0em} % Adjusts left by 2em, right by 0em
        \begin{itemize}
            \item[\textbf{Reflexive}] if $\forall a \in S$: $(a, a) \in R$
            \item[\textbf{Symmetric}] if $\forall a, b \in S$: $(a, b) \in R \implies (b, a) \in R$
            \item[\textbf{Transitive}] if $\forall a, b, c \in S$: $(a, b) \in R$ and $(b, c) \in R \implies (a, c) \in R$
        \end{itemize}
    \end{adjustwidth}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:infix}
    An equivalence relation $R$ on a set $S$ can be denoted by an infix binary operation $\approx$ such that $\forall a, b \in S$: $a \approx b \iff (a,b) \in R$.
\end{note}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Equivalence Classes]
    Let $S$ be a set, $R \subseteq S \times S$ be an equivalence relation and $a \in S$. The \textit{equivalence class} of $a$, denoted $[a]$ is the set of elements in $S$ equivalent to $a$, i.e., $[a] = \{s \in S\ |\ (a,s) \in R\}$. We write $[S]$ to denote the set of all equivalence classes of $S$, i.e., $[S] = \{[a]\ |\ a \in S\}$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}\label{example:rationals}
    Consider the set $F$ of all strings of the form $\frac{n}{m}$ for $n, m \in \N$. The set of rational numbers $\mathbb{Q}$ is the set of all equivalence classes of $F$ where $\frac{a}{b}$ is equivalent to $\frac{c}{d}$ when $a\ mod\ b = c\ mod\ d$.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Well-Definedness]
    Let $S$ be a set and $R \subseteq S \times S$ be an equivalence relation. A function $f: S \rightarrow X$ is \textit{well-defined} with respect to $R$ if $(a, b) \in R \implies f(a) = f(b)$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    Continuing Example \ref{example:rationals}, an example of a function $\varphi: F \rightarrow \N$ that is \textit{not} well-defined with respect to $\mathbb{Q}$ is one defined by adding the numerator of a rational number to its denominator e.g., $\frac{2}{4}$ is equivalent to $\frac{1}{2}$ but $\varphi(\frac{2}{4}) = 6 \neq 3 = \varphi(\frac{1}{2})$.
\end{example}

When a function $f: S \rightarrow X$ is well-defined, we may abuse notation and write $f: [S] \rightarrow X$ for the mapping $[s] \mapsto [f(s)]$, and even exclude the square brackets.

\subsection{Binary Relations and Order Theory}\label{appendix:order-rel}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Weak Linear Orders]\label{definition:linear-order}
    A \textit{weak linear order} on a set $\A$ is a relation $R \subseteq \A \times \A$ that is Transitive (see Appendix \ref{appendix:equivalence}) and Complete. Completeness means $\forall a, b \in \A$ such that $a \neq b$: $(a, b) \in R$ or $(b, a) \in R$.
\end{definition}

Just as in Note \ref{note:infix}, we also use the infix binary operation $\preceq$ to denote weak linear orders $R$ on $\A$, i.e. $\forall a, b \in R$: $a \preceq b \iff (a, b) \in R$.

\parsplit
\begin{example}\label{example:weak-linear-order}
    For any set $S$ of people, there is a weak linear order given by the \say{at least as tall} relation $\preceq$. Note that we can have $a \preceq b$ and $b \preceq a$ without $a = b$ as this just means $a$ and $b$ are of equal height, rather than $a$ and $b$ are the same person.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Strict Linear Orders]
    A (weak) linear order $R \subseteq \A \times \A$ is \textit{strict} if $(a, b) \in R \implies (b, a) \notin R$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    The \say{at least as tall} relation on people of Example \ref{example:weak-linear-order} can only be a strict linear order if no two people are of equal height.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Partial Orders]
    A partial order on a set a $\A$ is relation $R \subseteq \A \times \A$ that is Reflexive, Transitive and Anti-Symmetric. Anti-symmetry means $\forall a, b \in \A$:
    \begin{equation*}
        (a, b) \in R \iand (b, a) \in R \iff a = b
    \end{equation*}
\end{definition}

Just as in Note \ref{note:infix}, use also the infix binary operation $\leq$ to denote partial orders.

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    For any set $S$, the set of subsets of $S$ denoted $P(S)$ has a partial order given by the subset relation.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Transitive Closures]\label{definition:transitive-closure}
    Given any relation $R \in \A \times \A$, there exists a transitive closure $Trans(R)$, which is the smallest transitive relation on $\A$ such that $R \subseteq Trans(R)$, i.e., if $S$ is a transitive relation such that $R \subseteq S$ then $Trans(R) \subseteq S$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    If $\A$ is a set of airports and $R \subseteq \A \times \A$ is a relation denoting direct flights available (i.e., $(a, b) \in R$ if and only if there is a direct flight from $a$ to $b$), $Trans(R)$ relation of all multi-stop flights.
\end{example}

\subsection{Semi-Lattices and Partial Orders}\label{appendix:semi-lattice}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Semi-Lattices]
    Given a set $X$ and a binary operation $\star: X \times X \rightarrow X$, $(X, \star)$ is a \textit{semi-lattice} if the following properties are satisfied $\forall x, y, z \in X$:
    \begin{adjustwidth}{7em}{0em} % Adjusts left by 2em, right by 0em
        \begin{itemize}
            \item[\textbf{Associativity}] $x \star (y \star z) = (x \star y) \star z$
            \item[\textbf{Commutativity}] $x \star y = y \star x$
            \item[\textbf{Idempotency}] $x \star x = x$
        \end{itemize}
    \end{adjustwidth}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}\label{example-msl}
    Given a set $S$ and denoting the set of all subsets of $S$ as $P(S)$, set union $\cup: P(S) \times P(S) \rightarrow P(S)$ and set intersection $\cap$ are both semi-lattice operations.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:order-semi-lattice-equiv}
    Given a semi-lattice $(X, \vee)$, there exists a partial order $(X, \leq)$ given by setting:
    \begin{equation}\label{equation:join}
        \forall x, y \in X:\ x \leq y \iff x \vee y = y
    \end{equation}
    Additionally, for a semi-lattice $(X, \wedge)$, there exists a partial order $(X, \preceq)$ given by setting:
    \begin{equation}\label{equation:meet}
        \forall x, y \in X:\ x \preceq y \iff x \wedge y = x
    \end{equation}
    And the semi-lattice operations $\vee$ and $\wedge$ given by Equations \ref{equation:join} and \ref{equation:meet} are the least upper bound and greater lower bound operations on $(X, \leq)$, respectively.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    This is a standard result of Lattice Theory, e.g.,~\cite[Theorem 2.9]{lattice-order-theory}.\\
\end{proof}

\parsplit
\begin{note}[Join and Meet Semi-Lattices]
    For emphasis, when a semi-lattice operation $\vee$ is called a \textit{join semi-lattice} when it is defined with respect to a partial order as per Equation (\ref{equation:join}) in mind.  Likewise, a semi-lattice $\cap$ defined with respect to a partial order as per Equation (\ref{equation:meet}) is called a \textit{meet semi-lattice}. For example, the set union operation $\cup$ is a join semi-lattice with respect to the $\subseteq$ partial ordering, and $\cap$ is a meet semi-lattice with respect to $\subseteq$.
\end{note}


\subsection{Proofs and Suplementary Results for Section \ref{section:results}}\label{appendix:result-seciton-proofs}


\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:bounds}
    Let $\P$ be the set of weak linear orders on a set $\A$ and $\leq$ be the strictness ordering on $\P$. Then, for weak linear orders $r, s \in \P$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $r \leq s \implies r \subseteq s$ (recalling weak linear orders are relations, i.e., subsets of $\A \times \A$ --- see Appendix \ref{appendix:order-rel})
        \item The least upper bound $r \vee s = Trans(r \cup s)$ (see Definition \ref{definition:transitive-closure})
        \item The greatest lower bound $r \wedge s$ if it exists is $r \cap s$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    (1) We prove the contrapositive that $r \nsubseteq s$ implies $r \nleq s$. If $r \nsubseteq s$ then $\exists (a,b) \in r$ such that $(a,b) \notin s$. However, by the completeness property of weak linear orders, this implies $(b,a) \in s$, i.e., $b \prec a$ in $s$. However, if $(a,b)$ in $r$ then $b \nprec a$ in $r$. Hence, combining  $b \prec a$ in $s$ with $b \nprec a$ in $r$, we conclude $r \nleq s$.\\

    \noindent(2) We begin by noting that the relation $Trans(r \cup s)$ corresponds to a weak linear order because it is by definition transitive, and complete because $r$ is complete and $r \subseteq Trans(r \cup s)$. Then, it follows that $Trans(r \cup s)$ is an upper bound of $r$ and $s$ by (1). To show $Trans(r \cup s)$ is the least upper bound, it suffices to show for any other upper bound $t$ of $r$ and $s$, $Trans(r \cup s) \subseteq t$. Indeed, if a relation $t \in \P$ is a upper bound of $r$ and $s$, by (1), $r \subseteq t$ and $s \subseteq t$, which implies $r \cup s \subseteq t$. By definition of transitive closures, $Trans(r \cup s)$ includes all other transitive relations that include $r \cup s$. Hence, $Trans(r \cup s) \subseteq t$.\\

    \noindent(3) The intersection of two transitive relations is again transitive, so if $r \cap s$ is complete it corresponds to a weak linear order $v$ which is a lower bound of $r$ and $s$. By (1) to be the greatest lower bound, the relation must be the largest relation among lower bounds. Indeed, if we could remove an element $(a, b)$ from $r \cap s$ and have it still be a lower bound, then $b \prec a$ in $r$ and $s$ by completeness. But this means $(a, b)$ is not in either of $r$ or $s$, contradicting $(a,b) \in r \cap s$.\\
\end{proof}

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:missing-bounds}
    $\TPrefs$ (see Definition \ref{definition:extending-order}) has all greatest lower bounds.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Because $r \leq s \implies r \wedge s = r$, we need only show that $r \wedge s$ exists for incomparable elements $r$ and $s$ (with respect to $\leq$). Moreover, if $r$ and $s$ are incomparable then neither of them are $\c$, i.e., $r, s \in \P$. Hence, $r$ and $s$ must have opposing strict preferences (i.e., $a \prec b$ in $r$ and $b \prec a$ in $s$). By definition of $\c$ being a bottom element, $\c$ is the greatest lower bound of $r$ and $s$ if they have no other lower bound. Indeed, by see Proposition \ref{proposition:bounds}, if $r$ and $s$ had another lower bound, its underlying relation would be given by $r \cap s$. However, when $r$ and $s$ have opposing strict preferences, they do not have a lower bound in $\P$ and thus $\c$ is the only and hence greatest lower bound of $r$ and $s$.\\
\end{proof}

\dictatorequivalence*
