\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:proposition-as-definition}
    Let $(\Phi, \mu)$ be a Self-Reference System whose expressions $\E$ have the structure of an \textit{orthocomplemented lattice} with bottom $\bot$, top $\top$, and meet and complement operations $\wedge$, $\neg$ respectively (see~\cite[Section 1.5]{semi-modular-lattices}). Furthermore, recall that any meet semi-lattice has a corresponding partial ordering $\leq$ with $a \leq b \iff a \wedge b = a$ (see Proposition \ref{proposition:order-semi-lattice-equiv}). Then, we may say an \textit{abstract provability predicate} is an expression $p \in \E$ such that $\forall d \in \D$: $d \leq p \ast d$ and $(\neg p) \ast \bot = \top$.
\end{definition}

