\subsection{Embeddable Self-Reference Systems}\label{appendix:embeddings-and-composition}

To construct fixed-points for predicates in the Self-Reference Systems underlying Arithmetic Logic (Theorem \ref{theorem:arithmetic-logic-fixed-points}), we derive a generalised version of the Diagonalisation Lemma called the \textit{Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma} (Theorem \ref{theorem:diag-abstract}). In order to state and prove the \textit{Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma}, we need to define a special type of Self-Reference System called an Embeddable Self-Reference System. All examples of Self-Reference Systems introduced in this paper are Embeddable.

\parsplit

A Self-Reference System is Embeddable when --- informally --- \textit{there is a way to calculate everything at the expression level}. Or more specifically, a Self-Reference System is Embeddable when there is an associative, binary \textit{composition} operation on expressions and an \textit{embedding} operation from constants to expressions such that application (of an expression to a constant) is equivalent to composition with an embedding. We begin by defining Embeddable Self-Reference Systems and then demonstrate that our existing examples (\ref{example:godel-1} and \ref{example:arrow-1}) are embeddable for certain composition and embedding operations.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:embeddable}
    Given an associative binary \textit{composition} operation $\sbullet: \E \times \E \rightarrow \E$ and an \textit{embedding} function $\sigma: \C \rightarrow \E$, we say that a Self-Reference System $(\mu, \Phi)$ defined on expressions $\E$ and constants $\C$ is \textit{embeddable} in $(\sigma, \sbullet)$ if
    \begin{equation}\label{equation:embeddable}
        \forall e \in \E,\ \forall c \in \C:\ \Phi(e, c) = e \sbullet \sigma(c)
    \end{equation}
    As shorthand, instead of writing that the Self-Reference System $(\mu, \Phi)$ is embeddable / embeds in $(\sigma, \sbullet)$, we write the \textit{Embeddable Self-Reference System} $(\mu, \sigma, \sbullet)$ because $\Phi$ can be defined by Equation (\ref{equation:embeddable}).
\end{definition}

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{example}
        Continuing Example \ref{example:godel-1}, the Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ is embeddable in $(\sigma, \sbullet)$ for the number to numeral inclusion $\sigma: \N \hookrightarrow \L_1$ (i.e., $\sigma(n) = \underline{n}$) and substitution $\sbullet$ defined by
        \begin{align*}
            A(x) \sbullet B(y) \coloneqq A(B(y)) &  & \text{For $A(x), B(x) \in \L_1$}                          \\
            A(x) \sbullet C \coloneqq A(C)       &  & \text{For $A(x) \in \L_1$ and $C \in \L_0$}               \\
            D \sbullet f \coloneqq D             &  & \text{For any sentence $D \in \L_0$ and any $f \in \L_1$}
        \end{align*}
        Condition (\ref{equation:embeddable}) holds because for any $A(x) \in \L_1$ and $n \in \N$: $A(x) \sbullet \sigma(n) = A(\underline{n}) = \Phi(A(x), n)$, using the definition of $\Phi$ in Example \ref{example:godel-1}. This example shows that the embedding function $\sigma$ coupled with the composition operation $\sbullet$ succeeds in recovering the application function $\Phi$ of Self-Reference System in point. This demonstrates formally that the application function is given by substitution.\label{example:godel-embedding}
    \end{example}
\end{godelbox}

\begin{arrowbox}
    \begin{example}\label{example:arrow-embedding}
        Continuing Example \ref{example:arrow-1}, for a fixed voter $i$, we define $\sigma_i: \TPrefs \rightarrow \TProfiles$ such that for a preference relation $r \in \TPrefs$: $\sigma_i(r) \coloneqq (\i, \dots, r, \dots, \i)$, i.e., the profile with preference relation $r$ for the $i^{\text{th}}$ voter and $\i$ (the preference relation indifferent on all alternatives) otherwise. Then, the Self-Reference System $(\TWelfare, \Phi_i)$ embeds in $(\sigma_i, \sbullet_i)$ for $\sbullet_i$ defined coordinate-wise on profiles as follows:
        \begin{equation*}
            p \sbullet_i q \coloneqq (p_1 \wedge q_1, \dots, p_i \veebar q_i, \dots p_N \wedge q_N)
        \end{equation*}
        Equation (\ref{equation:embeddable}) is satisfied as:
        \begin{equation*}
            p \sbullet_i \sigma_i(r) = (p_1 \wedge \i, \dots, p_i \veebar r, \dots p_N \wedge \i) = (p_1, \dots, p_i \veebar r, \dots p_N) = \Phi_i(p, r)
        \end{equation*}
        using the definition of $\Phi_i$ in Example \ref{example:arrow-1}. Again, the combination of the embedding function $\sigma_i$ coupled with this coordinate-wise composition operation $\sbullet_i$ suffices to recover the application function $\Phi_i$ of this Self-Reference System, which merges the preference relations $p_i$ and $r$.

        \parsplit

        Likewise, we are able to embed $(\TWelfare, \Psi_i)$ in $(\chi_i, \bwedge)$, where:
        \begin{align*}
            \text{For a preference relation}\ r \in \TPrefs: & \quad \chi_i(r) \coloneqq (\i, \dots, r, \dots \i)                  \\
            \text{For profiles}\ p, q \in \TProfiles:        & \quad p \bwedge q \coloneqq (p_1 \wedge q_1, \dots, p_N \wedge q_N)
        \end{align*}
        and Equation (\ref{equation:embeddable}) is satisfied as:
        \begin{equation*}
            p \bwedge \chi_i(r) = (p_1 \wedge \i, \dots, p_i \wedge r, \dots, p_N \wedge \i) = (p_1, \dots, p_i \wedge r, \dots, p_N) = \Psi_i(p, r)
        \end{equation*}
    \end{example}
\end{arrowbox}

In short, Embeddable Self-Reference Systems are defined in such a way that an application function can be recovered by embedding the constants into the expressions. This allows us to reason about Self-Reference Systems entirely at the level of expressions, which we are able to exploit to prove the Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma in the next section.

\subsection{The Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma}\label{appendix:abstract-diag}

In this section, we derive a generalised version of the Diagonalisation Lemma for Self-Reference Systems called the \textit{Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma}. In the Self-Reference Systems of Arithmetic Logic (see Example \ref{example:godel-1}), the Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma yields the standard Diagonalisation Lemma (see Proposition \ref{proposition:diagonalisation-lemma-instance} and Theorem \ref{theorem:arithmetic-logic-fixed-points}).

\parsplit

This Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma exploits a new property of expressions in a Self-Reference System called \textit{internalisation}, which amounts to a particular expression in $\E$ of a Self-Reference System being a \textit{code} for a function \textit{external} to the Self-Reference System (e.g., a $\E \rightarrow \E$ function). We define internalisation generally as follows:

\parsplit
\begin{definition}
    A function $\alpha: X \rightarrow Y$ is \textit{internalised} by a function $\beta: Z \times X \rightarrow Y$ if $\exists\ z_\alpha \in Z$ such that:
    \begin{equation*}
        \forall x \in X:\ \alpha(x) = \beta(z_\alpha, x)
    \end{equation*}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{note}
    Related definitions exist in other approaches, e.g., Yanofsky uses the term \say{representable} for internalisation with respect to functions $T \times T \rightarrow Y$~\cite{yanofsky}. In Category Theory, Lawvere uses the term \textit{weakly point surjective} to refer to functions $\beta: Z \times X \rightarrow Y$ where that all functions of the form $X \rightarrow Y$ can be internalised with respect to $\beta$~\cite{Lawvere}.
\end{note}

\begin{godelbox}
    \begin{example}
        In Arithmetic Logic, internalisation can be motivated as follows: recall for a fixed theory of Arithmetic Logic the sets $\F_0$ and $\F_1$ of formulae with 0 and 0-1 free variables respectively (see Definition \ref{definition:lindenbaum-algebra-def}). Observe that substituting a sentence in $\F_0$ for the free variable of a predicate in $\F_1$ produces a new formula in $\F_0$. Thus, many predicates in $\F_1$ \textit{behave} like a $\F_0 \rightarrow \F_0$ function via substitution of sentences. Moreover, because the standard Gödel numbering $G$ is injective we have the following inclusions:
        \begin{center}
            \begin{tikzcd}
                \F_1 \arrow[r, "G", hook] & \N \arrow[rr, "Numeral", hook] && \F_0 \arrow[rr, "inclusion", hook] && \F_1
            \end{tikzcd} $\implies \F_0 \cong \F_1$
        \end{center}
        The bijection $\F_0 \cong \F_1$ means that many $\F_1 \rightarrow \F_1$ functions also correspond to elements of $\F_1$. Returning to the Embeddable Self-Reference System $(\NGodelvar, \sigma, \sbullet)$ on Lindenbaum Algebras (see Example \ref{example:godel-embedding}), the internalisation of the following \textit{diagonal function} \cite{gaifman} yields the Diagonalisation Lemma:
        \begin{equation*}
            \delta: \L_1 \rightarrow \L_1 \quad\text{defined by mappings}\quad B(x) \mapsto \DObar{B(\DObar{B(x})}
        \end{equation*}
    \end{example}

    \parsplit
    \begin{note}\label{note:internalisablility-caveat}
        Demonstrating the internalisability of $\delta$ is highly non-trivial. Often, more intricate variants of $\delta$ are used instead (see Salehi~\cite{salehi-presentation, salehi-diagonal-lemma} for examples of relevant approaches).
    \end{note}
\end{godelbox}

We proceed to define the diagonal function $\delta$ for Self-Reference Systems in general. Specifically, denoting function composition by $\circ$ (i.e., for functions $f: X \rightarrow Y$ and $g: Y \rightarrow Z$, $g \circ f: X \rightarrow Z$ is defined by $x \mapsto g(f(x))$):

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:composites}
    Given an Embeddable Self-Reference System $(\mu, \sigma, \sbullet)$ with expressions $\E$, we define $\DObarvar: \E \rightarrow \E$ as the composite $\sigma \circ \mu$, and $\delta: \E \rightarrow \E$ by the following composites (left), defined by mappings (right):
    \begin{center}
        \begin{tikzcd}[sep=large]
            E \times E \arrow[r, "\ast"]                                                     & E \arrow[r, "\DObarvar"] & E &  & {(e, e)} \arrow[r, maps to]                                               & {\Phi(e, \mu(e)) = e \ast e} \arrow[r, maps to] & \DObar{e \ast e} \\
            E \arrow[u, "\Delta"] \arrow[rru, "\delta"'] &                         &   &  & e \arrow[u, maps to] \arrow[rru, maps to] &                                        &
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{center}
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{lemma}
    \label{lemma:helper}
    Given an Embeddable Self-Reference System $(\mu, \sigma, \sbullet)$, for $\DObarvar \coloneqq \sigma \circ \mu$ we have:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $f \ast g = f \sbullet \DObar{g}$
        \item $(f \sbullet g) \ast h = f \sbullet (g \ast h)$
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    This is given by calculations:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $f \ast g = \Phi(f, \mu(g)) = f \sbullet \sigma(\mu(g)) = f \sbullet \DObar{g}$
        \item $(f \sbullet g) \ast h = (f \sbullet g) \sbullet \DObar{h} = f \sbullet (g \sbullet \DObar{h}) = f \sbullet (g \ast h)$
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proof}



\parsplit
\begin{theorem}[Abstract Diagonalisation Lemma]\label{theorem:diag-abstract}
    Given an Embeddable Self-Reference System $(\mu, \sigma, \sbullet)$, if $\delta$ (see Definition \ref{definition:composites}) is internalised by some $f_\delta$ with respect to $\ast$, then the fixed-point property is satisfied for all expressions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    For an arbitrarily $Q \in \E$, we define $q \coloneqq Q \sbullet f_\delta$, and $p \coloneqq q \ast q$, and find that $Q \ast p = p$ as desired, because:
    \begin{align*}
        Q \ast p =\  & Q \sbullet \DObar{q \ast q}   &  & \text{Lemma \ref{lemma:helper}-1 and definition of $p$}         \\
        =\           & Q \sbullet (f_\delta \ast q)  &  & \text{$f_\delta$ internalising $\delta$ with respect to $\ast$} \\
        =\           & (Q \sbullet  f_\delta) \ast q &  & \text{Lemma \ref{lemma:helper}-2}                               \\
        =\           & q \ast q                      &  & \text{Definition of $q$}                                        \\
        =\           & p                             &  & \text{Definition of $p$}                                        \\
    \end{align*}
\end{proof}

\begin{godelbox}
    \arithmeticlogicfixedpoints*
    \begin{proof}
        Recall that in Example \ref{example:godel-embedding} we saw that $(\NGodelvar, \Phi)$ is Embeddable. We then simply follow proof of Theorem \ref{theorem:diag-abstract}. In other words, for an arbitrary predicate $Q(x) \in \L_1$: we take $q(x)$ to be the predicate such that $\forall B(x) \in \L_1$: $q(\DObar{B(x)}) = Q(\DObar{B(\DObar{B(x)})})$, and the fixed-point $p(x)$ of $Q(x)$ is $q(\DObar{q(x)}) \in \L_0$.\\
    \end{proof}
    However, note that showing the predicate $q(x)$ exists is highly non-trivial (see Note \ref{note:internalisablility-caveat}).

\end{godelbox}

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:internalisablility-caveat-2}
    In an Embeddable Self-Reference System $(\mu, \sigma, \sbullet)$, by simple applications of Lemma \ref{lemma:helper}, it suffices to break down the internalisation of the diagonal function $\delta$ into the following internalisation of smaller parts:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\epsilon \coloneqq \ast \circ \Delta$ (i.e., defined by mappings $e \mapsto e \ast e$) internalised by some $f_\epsilon$ with respect to $\ast$.
        \item $\DObarvar$ internalised by some $f_{\DObarvar}$ with respect to composition $\sbullet$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Specifically, $f_{\DObarvar} \sbullet f_\epsilon$ internalises $\delta$ with respect to $\delta$ because:
    \begin{equation*}
        \delta(d) = \DObar{\epsilon(d)} = = \DObar{e \ast e} = \DObar{f_\epsilon \ast e} = f_{\DObarvar} \sbullet (f_\epsilon \ast e) = (f_{\DObarvar} \sbullet f_\epsilon) \ast d
    \end{equation*}
\end{note}

\subsection{A Condorcet Paradox Centric Proof of Arrow}\label{appendix:arrow-proof}

In this section, we prove Arrow's Impossibility Theorem by generalising D'Antoni's approach from~\cite{arrow-simple}. Recall, for 3 alternatives, D'Antoni defines strict linear orders and Condorcet Paradoxes alike as 3-tuples of values in $\textbf{2} \coloneqq \{0,1\}$ (see Example \ref{example:strict-order-sequences} and Note \ref{note:dantoni-2}). He then uses these tuples to define profiles, Social Welfare Functions, fairness conditions, and ultimately proves Arrow's Impossibility Theorem restricted to the strict case.

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:three-alt-induction}
    Proving Arrow's Impossibility Theorem by showing the assumption of Unanimity IIA and Non-Dictatorship leads to a Condorcet Paradox means it suffices to reason about the 3 alternative case. This is because by IIA, the necessitation of a Condorcet Paradox only depends on those 3 alternatives, and hence, the theorem holds for any number of alternatives beyond 3. As such, many results in this section only require a proof for the 3 alternative case.
\end{note}

\parsplit

The primary generalisation of this section is to reason about weak rather than strict linear orders. We begin by defining weak linear orders and Condorcet Paradoxes alike as follows:
\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:abstract-pref}
    Given a finite set of alternatives $\A \coloneqq \{x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots, x_A\}$ and $\3 \coloneqq \{0, \indiff, 1\}$, an \textit{abstract preference relation} on $\A$ is a function $\varphi: \A \rightarrow \3$. Moreover, given a weak-linear order $\preceq$ on $\A$ and writing $s(i) = i + 1\ mod\ |\A|$: $\varphi$ uniquely corresponds to $\preceq$ if:
    \begin{equation*}
        \phi(x_i) = \begin{cases}
            \indiff \iff x_i \sim x_{s(i)} \\
            0 \iff x_i \prec x_{s(i)}      \\
            1 \iff x_i \succ x_{s(i)}
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    $\varphi: \A \rightarrow \3$ is equivalently an $|\A|$ long tuple of elements in $\3$, i.e., $(\varphi(x_1), \varphi(x_2), \varphi(x_3), \dots, \varphi(x_A))$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}\label{example:order-sequences}
    For $ \A \coloneqq \{x_1, x_2, x_3\}$, the weak linear order $x_1 \sim x_2 \prec x_3$ can be written as $(\indiff, 0, 1)$, and $x_2 \prec x_1 \sim x_3$ as $(0, \indiff, 1)$, and $x_3 \sim x_1 \prec x_2$ as $(0, 1, \indiff)$.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:condorcet-condition}
    Given alternatives $\A \coloneqq \{x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots, x_A\}$ and an abstract preference $\varphi: \A \rightarrow \3$, $\varphi$ corresponds to a weak-linear order if and only if $im(\varphi) = \{\indiff\}$ or $\2 \subseteq im(\varphi)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    For the first case, $im(\varphi) = \{\indiff\}$ if and only if $\varphi$ corresponds to $x_1 \sim x_2 \sim x_3 \sim \dots \sim x_A$, a valid weak linear order. For the second case, consider to the contrary that $im(\varphi) \neq \{\indiff\}$ but $\2 \nsubseteq im(\varphi)$; we will show this forces $\varphi$ to represent a Condorcet Paradox. Indeed, $im(\varphi) = \{0, \indiff\}$ if and only if $x_1 \preceq x_2 \preceq \dots \preceq x_A \preceq x_1$. However, by transitivity and $0 \in im(\varphi)$, there is a pair $j, k \in \{1,2,\dots,A\}$ such that $x_j \prec x_k \preceq x_j$ or $x_j \preceq x_k \prec x_j$. In other words, $\varphi$ has a Condorcet Paradox.\\
\end{proof}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:inclusion}
    Recall the set $\TPrefs = \P \cup \{\c\}$ of weak linear orders $\P$ on $\A$ and $\c$, a distinct element representing all complete Condorcet Paradoxes (see Definition \ref{definition:extending-order}). There is an inclusion $\iota: \APrefs \hookrightarrow \TPrefs$ where $\iota(\varphi) = \c$ if and only if $im(\varphi) \neq \{\indiff\}$ and $\2 \nsubseteq im(\varphi)$, and $\iota(\varphi)$ is the weak linear order specified by Definition \ref{definition:abstract-pref} otherwise (see Proposition \ref{proposition:condorcet-condition}).
\end{definition}

Before continuing to define Profiles and hence Social Welfare Functions and the fairness conditions, we define some additional useful notation:

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:notation}
    For two sets $X$ and $Y$, we write $Y^X$ to denote the set of functions from $X \rightarrow Y$. Additionally, if $N \in \N$, we write $X^N$ for the set of functions $\{1, \dots, N\} \rightarrow X$, which is equivalent to the set $\underbrace{X \times \dots \times X}_{N\ \text{times}}$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}
    Given a set of alternatives $\A$ and $N \geq 2$ individuals, the corresponding set of all \textit{abstract profiles} is $(\APrefs)^N$, which we denote as $\AProfiles$.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}
    An abstract profile is equivalent to a $\3$ valued $|\A| \times N$ matrix.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    This equivalence arises out of noting a profile is $N$ copies of $\APrefs$, i.e., a function $N \rightarrow \APrefs$. This function is equivalent to a $N \times \A \rightarrow \3$ function, which in turn is equivalent to a $\3$ valued $\A \times N$ matrix (by transposition).\\
\end{proof}
\noindent For an example of a matrix representation of a profile, see Table \ref{table:dantoni-profile}.

\begin{table}[ht!]
    \centering
    \begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|p{1.8in}|X|X|X|}
        \hline
        \diagbox{Alternative}{Individual} & \textbf{1} & \textbf{2} & \textbf{3} \\\hline
        \textbf{$x_1$} (vs $x_2$)         & 0          & 1          & 0          \\\hline
        \textbf{$x_2$} (vs $x_3$)         & 0          & 0          & 1          \\\hline
        \textbf{$x_3$} (vs $x_1$)         & 1          & 0          & 0          \\\hline
    \end{tabularx}
    \caption{The Condorcet profile of Section \ref{subsection:condorcet-paradox} Table \ref{table:condorcet-plain}, written with alternatives $x_1,x_2,x_3$ in place of $a,b,c$ respectively}
    \label{table:dantoni-profile}
\end{table}

\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:matrix-tuples}
    An element of $\AProfiles$ can be written as an $N$ tuple of elements in $\APrefs$ (i.e., an $N$ tuple of $\A \rightarrow \3$ functions), and also in a transposed form as an $|\A|$ tuple of elements in $\3^N$ (i.e., an $|\A|$ tuple of $N$ tuples of values in $\3$).
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    For $\A = \{x_1, x_2, x_3\}$, given row tuples $r_1, r_2, r_3 \in \3^N$, the tuple $(r_1, r_2, r_3)$ corresponds to a profile. In other words, $r_1$ records each voter's preferences on $x_1$ vs $x_2$, and $r_1$ and $r_2$ does the same for $x_2$ vs $x_3$, and $x_3$ vs $x_1$, respectively. Likewise, a profile is equivalently a tuple $(c_1, c_2, c_3)$ of individual preference relations on $\A$, i.e., functions $c_1, c_2, c_3 \in \APrefs$, which are equivalently tuples (see Definition \ref{definition:abstract-pref}).
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{note}\label{note:neg}
    Valid profiles, i.e., $\PN \subset \AProfiles$ are those where for each individual $i = 1, \dots, N$, the tuple representing that individual's (abstract) preference relation is not a Condorcet Paradox.
\end{note}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    For example, the valid profile of Table \ref{table:dantoni-profile} having rows $r_1, r_2, r_3 \in \3^N$, for each individual $i$, each $(r_1(i), r_2(i), r_3(i))$ is a column, and we find that none of the columns are Condorcet Paradoxes by Proposition \ref{proposition:condorcet-condition}.
\end{example}

We now define Social Welfare Functions and fairness conditions and show how they relate to their counterparts in standard Social Choice Theory (i.e., Definition \ref{definition:fairness-conditions}).
\parsplit
\begin{definition}\label{definition:new-fairness-conditions}
    A Social Welfare Function on abstract preference relations on $\A = \{x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots, x_A\}$ is a function $\AWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \APrefs$. $\AWelfare$, and satisfies:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Unrestricted Domain} if $im(\AWelfare) = \P$.
        \item \textbf{IIA} if $\AWelfare$ can be expressed as the product of two-alternative welfare functions $s_1$, $s_2, \dots$, $s_A: \3^N \rightarrow \3$. In other words for a profile $p \in \PN$ i.e., a matrix made up of row tuples $r_1, r_2, \dots, r_A \in \3^N$ (see Definition \ref{definition:matrix-tuples}): $w(p) = (s_1(r_1), s_2(r_2), \dots, s_A(r_A))$.
    \end{itemize}
    And given IIA:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Unanimity} if writing $\Delta x = (\underbrace{x, \dots, x}_{N\ \text{times}})$ we have $\forall j = 1,2,\dots,A$: $s_j(\Delta 0) = 0 \iand s_j(\Delta 1) = 1$
        \item \textbf{Non-Dictatorship} if $\nexists i = 1,2,\dots,N$ such that $\forall j = 0,1,\dots,A$:
              \begin{equation*}
                  \forall (l_1,\dots,l_j,\dots,l_N) \in \2^N:\ s_j(l_1,\dots,l_i,\dots,l_N) = l_i
              \end{equation*}
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Unrestricted domain and IIA clearly generalise their standard counterparts. By which we mean, restricting the fairness conditions of Definition \ref{definition:new-fairness-conditions} to profiles that $\AWelfare$ does not map to Condorcet Paradoxes, yields conditions equivalent their counterparts in Definition \ref{definition:fairness-conditions}. This is also the case for Unanimity and Non-Dictatorship but requiring IIA already holds. We are also able to recover the Dictatorship condition of Definition \ref{definition:new-fairness-conditions} for Social Welfare Functions $\TWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \TPrefs$ (see Definition \ref{definition:dictator-generalised}). We prove this less obvious fact as follows:

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:dictator-equivalence-2}
    If a Social Welfare Function $\AWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \APrefs$ has a Dictator at $i$ then the function $\TWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \TPrefs$ given by the inclusion $\iota: \APrefs \hookrightarrow \TPrefs$ (see Definition \ref{definition:inclusion}) as follows:
    \begin{center}
        \begin{tikzcd}[sep=large]
            \PN \arrow[r, "\AWelfare"] \arrow[rd, "\TWelfare"'] & \APrefs \arrow[d, "\iota"] \\
            & \TPrefs
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{center}
    Satisfies the Dictator Condition of \ref{definition:dictator-generalised}, i.e.:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\TWelfare(p) = \c \implies p_i = \i$
        \item If $a \prec b$ holds strictly in the preference relation $p_i$ (i.e., $p_i \neq \i$) then $a \prec b$ holds strictly in $\TWelfare(p)$ (i.e., $\TWelfare(p) \leq p_i$)
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    (1) We prove this via the contrapositive, i.e., for any $p \in \PN$: if $p_i \neq \i$ then $\AWelfare(p) \neq \c$. If $p_i \neq \i$ then by Proposition \ref{proposition:condorcet-condition}, $p_i$ corresponds to a function $\varphi: \A \rightarrow \3$ such that $\{0,1\} \subset im(\varphi)$. However, by the dictator condition of Definition \ref{definition:new-fairness-conditions} it must then be the case that $\{0,1\} \subset im(\AWelfare(p))$, i.e., $\AWelfare(p) \neq \c$. (2) If $p_i \neq \i$ then clearly $\TWelfare(p) \leq p_i$ by the dictator condition of Definition \ref{definition:new-fairness-conditions}.\\
\end{proof}

To prove Arrow's Impossibility Theorem, we utilise some useful lemmas about how Social Welfare Functions satisfying Unrestricted Domain, IIA and Unanimity behave. To begin, we define a negation like operation on abstract preference relations and profiles.

\parsplit
\begin{definition}[Negation of Preference Relations]\label{definition:preference-negation}
    We define the function $\neg: \3 \rightarrow \3$ by the mappings $0 \mapsto 1$, $1 \mapsto 0$ and $\indiff \mapsto \indiff$. Then, abusing notation, we define the function $\neg: \APrefs \rightarrow \APrefs$ such that for $\varphi: \A \rightarrow \3$ and $a \in \A$: $(\neg(\varphi))(a) = \neg(\varphi(a))$. Brackets are excluded when clear.
\end{definition}

\parsplit
\begin{example}
    Using the standard notation for the set $\P$ of weak linear orders on $\A \coloneqq \{a, b, c\}$, if $p \in \P$ corresponds to: $a \prec b \sim c$, then $\neg p$ corresponds to $b \sim c \prec a$.
\end{example}

\parsplit
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:neg}
    For the 3 alternative case, if $x \in \2^N$ then for any $y \in \3^N$ we have that any permutation of $(x, \neg x, y)$ is a valid profile (i.e., member of $\PN$).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    For every $i = 1, \dots, N$ we have that $\{x(i), \neg x(i)\} = \2$, thus any permutation of $(x(i), \neg x(i), y(i))$ does not correspond to a Condorcet Paradox by Proposition \ref{proposition:condorcet-condition} and hence any permutation of $(x, \neg x, y)$ corresponds to a valid profile by Note \ref{note:neg}.\\
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strictness Preservation]
    \label{lemma:strict-preservation}
    For any Social Welfare Function $\AWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \APrefs$ satisfying Unrestricted Domain, IIA and Unanimity with respect to $s_1, s_2, \dots, s_A$: $\3^N \rightarrow \3$, $\AWelfare$ maps strict profiles to strict preferences, i.e.:
    \begin{equation*}
        \forall j = 1,2,\dots,A:\ \forall (l_1,\dots,l_N) \in \2^N: s_j(l_1,\dots,l_N) \in \2
    \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    We will assume to the contrary and produce a Condorcet Paradox, furthermore, by Note \ref{note:three-alt-induction} it suffices to prove the theorem for the 3 alternative case. Indeed, assume to the contrary that $\exists\ l \coloneqq (l_1,\dots,l_N) \in \2^N$ and (without loss of generality) $s_1(l_1,\dots,l_N) = \indiff$. Then, because $l$ is strict, by Proposition \ref{proposition:neg} $(l, \Delta 0, \neg l)$ and $(l, \Delta 1, \neg l)$ are both valid profiles (i.e., in $\PN$). Then, applying $\AWelfare$ to each profile yields $(\indiff, 0, v)$ and $(\indiff, 1, v)$ respectively, for some $v \in \3$. Indeed, for $(\indiff, 0, v)$ to not be a Condorcet Paradox, we require $v = 1$ but that renders $(\indiff, 1, v)$ a Condorcet Paradox, contradicting Unrestricted Domain.\\
\end{proof}

We can show Social Welfare Functions satisfying IIA and Unanimity not only map strict profiles to strict profiles but on strict profiles, they are both negation preserving (see Definition \ref{definition:preference-negation}) and treat every alternative equivalently (a property often called \textit{neutrality}).

\parsplit
\begin{lemma}[Strict Neutrality and Negation Preservation]
    \label{lemma:neutrality}
    For any Social Welfare Function $\AWelfare: \PN \rightarrow \APrefs$ satisfying Unrestricted Domain, Unanimity and IIA (i.e., $\AWelfare$ is expressible as $(s_1, s_2, \dots, s_A)$ for $s_j: \3^N \rightarrow \3$):
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\forall x \in \2^N:\ s_1(x) = s_2(x) = \dots = s_A(x)$
        \item $\forall j \in \{1,2,\dots,A\}$ and $\forall x \in \2^N$: $s_j(\neg x) = \neg s_j(x)$
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    As in Lemma \ref{lemma:strict-preservation}, we will assume to the contrary and produce a Condorcet Paradox in 3 alternative case. Furthermore, without loss of generality, it suffices to prove $\forall x \in \2^N$: $s_1(x) = s_2(x) = \neg s_3(\neg x)$. Firstly, we assume to the contrary that $s_2(x) \neq \neg s_3(\neg x)$, which implies $s_2(x) = s_3(\neg x)$. We denote $t \coloneqq s_2(x)$ and observe that by Lemma \ref{lemma:strict-preservation} that $t \in \2$ and so by IIA and Unanimity:
    \begin{equation}
        \AWelfare(\Delta t, x, \neg x) = (s_1(\Delta t), s_2(x), s_3(\neg x)) = (t, t, t)
    \end{equation}
    And $(t, t, t)$ is a Condorcet Paradox, contradicting Unrestricted Domain. Note that $(\Delta t, x, \neg x)$ is a valid input to $\AWelfare$ by Proposition \ref{proposition:neg}. Thus the above contradiction forces us to conclude $s_2(x) = \neg s_3(\neg x)$. Now, to complete the proof by showing $s_1(x) = s_2(x)$, assume that $s_1(x) \neq s_2(x)$, i.e., $t \coloneqq s_1(x) = \neg s_2(x) = s_3(\neg x)$. Again, $\AWelfare(x, \Delta t, \neg x) = (t, t, t)$, a contradiction. Hence, $s_1(x) = s_2(x) = \neg s_3(\neg x)$ as desired.\\
\end{proof}

Below is D'Antoni's proof of Arrow's Impossibility Theorem in the strict case, adapted to use the notation of this section. We will then repurpose the proof to prove the full theorem.

\parsplit
\begin{theorem}[Arrow's Impossibility Theorem (Strict Case)]\label{theorem:arrow-strict-case}
    For any finite set $\A$ of alternatives with at least 3 elements and two individuals: any Social Welfare Function on strict linear orders that satisfies Unanimity, IIA and Non-Dictatorship does not satisfy Unrestricted Domain (i.e., produces Condorcet Paradoxes).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Recall by Note \ref{note:three-alt-induction} that it suffices to prove the theorem in the 3 alternative case, and by Lemma \ref{lemma:neutrality} there exists an $s: \2^N \rightarrow \2$ such that $\AWelfare$ is the product $s \times s \times s$. Consider the following two sets:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $I(s) \coloneqq \{m \in \2^N |\ s(m) = 1\}$
        \item $\forall m \in \2^N$: $A(m) \coloneqq \{i \in \{1,2,\dots,N\} |\ m_i = 1\}$
    \end{itemize}
    Then, we note the following two possibilities regarding these sets:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\exists m \in I(s)$: $A(m) = \{i\}$
        \item $\exists m \in I(s)$: $1 < |A(m)| < N$ minimally: i.e., $\nexists\ l \in I(s)$ such that $A(l) \subset A(m)$.
              \noindent \end{enumerate}
    \noindent And show in either case, we can construct a profile in $q \in \PN$ such that $\AWelfare(q) = (1,1,1)$.\\

    \noindent (1) If $\exists m \in I(s)$: $A(m) = \{i\}$ then by definition $s(0, \dots, 1, \dots, 0) = 1$ where all arguments of $s$ are $0$ except at the $i^{th}$ place. Then, by Non-Dictatorship, $\AWelfare$ must contradict voter $i$ for some profile. In other words, $\exists m' \in I(s)$ such that $m'_i = 0$. The profile $(m, m', \Delta 1)$ is valid (see Table \ref{table:case-1}) but $\AWelfare(m, m', \Delta 1) = (1, 1, 1)$, a Condorcet Paradox.

    \parsplit

    \begin{table}[H]
        \centering
        \begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|p{1.8in}|X|X|X|X|X|}
            \hline
            \diagbox{Alternative}{Individual} & \textbf{1} & $\dots$ & \textbf{i} & $\dots$ & \textbf{N} \\\hline
            \textbf{$m$}                      & $0$        & $\dots$ & $1$        & $\dots$ & $0$        \\\hline
            \textbf{$m'$}                     & $m'_1$     & $\dots$ & $0$        & $\dots$ & $m'_N$     \\\hline
            \textbf{$\Delta 1$}               & $1$        & $\dots$ & $1$        & $\dots$ & $1$        \\\hline
        \end{tabularx}
        \caption{This is a valid profile because every voter's preferences has a $0$ or a $1$ in it (see Note \ref{note:neg})}
        \label{table:case-1}
    \end{table}

    \noindent (2) Given a minimising $m \in \2^N$, we can construct $m', m'' \in \2^N$ such that $\forall i \notin A(m)$: $m'(i) = m''(i) = 1$ and $\forall i \in A(m)$: $m''(i) = \neg m'(i)$ (see Table \ref{table:case-2}). Moreover, $(m, m', m'')$ is valid profile. Observe that $A(\neg m') \subset A(m)$, which by our assumption implies $s(\neg m') = 0$, which by Theorem (\ref{lemma:neutrality}) means $s(m') = 1$. We can repeat this argument for $m''$ to conclude that $s(m'') = 1$. Together, this implies $\AWelfare(m, m', m'') = (1, 1, 1)$, a Condorcet Paradox.

    \begin{table}[H]
        \centering
        \begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|p{1.8in}|X|X|X|X|X|}
            \hline
            \diagbox{Alternative}{Individual} & \textbf{i}  & $\dots$ & \textbf{$i'$}  & $\dots$ & \textbf{j} \\\hline
            \textbf{$m$}                      & $1$         & $\dots$ & $1$            & $\dots$ & $0$        \\\hline
            \textbf{$m'$}                     & $m'_i$      & $\dots$ & $\neg m'_{i'}$ & $\dots$ & $1$        \\\hline
            \textbf{$m''$}                    & $\neg m'_i$ & $\dots$ & $m'_{i'}$      & $\dots$ & $1$        \\\hline
        \end{tabularx}
        \caption{This is a valid profile because every voter's preferences has a $0$ or a $1$ in it by $m_i$ and $m_{i'}$ being $0$ or $1$.}
        \label{table:case-2}
    \end{table}
\end{proof}

\parsplit
\begin{theorem}[Arrow's Impossibility Theorem]\label{theorem:arrow-intermediate-case}
    For any finite set $\A$ of alternatives with at least 3 elements and two individuals: any Social Welfare Function on weak linear orders that satisfies Unanimity, IIA and Non-Dictatorship does not satisfy Unrestricted Domain (i.e., produces Condorcet Paradoxes).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Again, by Note \ref{note:three-alt-induction} it suffices to prove this in the 3 alternative case. By IIA, $\AWelfare$ is expressible as $(s_1, s_2, s_3)$ for $s_j: \3^N \rightarrow \3$ and by Theorems \ref{lemma:neutrality} and \ref{lemma:strict-preservation} $(s_1, s_2, s_3)$ restricts on strict profiles to $(s, s, s)$ for some $s: \2^N \rightarrow \2$. Thus, we can still define $I(s)$ and $A(m)$ for and $m \in \2^N$ as in Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-strict-case}. We likewise complete our proof by showing the two cases of Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-strict-case} (see Table \ref{table:case-1} and \ref{table:case-2}) allows us to construct a profiles leading to Condorcet Paradoxes.

    \parsplit

    Case $(2)$ can be argued verbatim because it only involves strict profiles. On the other hand, case (1) needs to be modified. Given an $m \in I(s)$ such that $A(m) = \{i\}$, by Non-Dictatorship there exists a $j \in \{1,2,3\}$ and $m' \in \3^N$ such that $m'_i = 0$ and $s_j(m') = 1$. Without loss of generality, if $j = 2$, we have that $(m, m', \Delta 1)$ is a valid profile (see Table \ref{table:case-1}) and still:
    \begin{align*}
        \AWelfare(m, m', \Delta 1)  =\  & (s_1(m), s_2(m'), s_3(\Delta 1)) &  & \text{IIA}                                                  \\
        =\                              & (s(m), s_2(m'), s(\Delta 1))     &  & \text{$s_1 = s_3 = s$ equal on strict preferences}          \\
        =\                              & (1, 1, 1)                        &  & \text{Definition of $s$, $m'$ and Unanimity, respectively.}
    \end{align*}
    I.e., the profile $(m, m', \Delta 1)$ produces Condorcet Paradox. If $j = 1$ or $3$ we can likeiwse produce Condorcet Paradoxes via profiles $(m', m, \Delta 1)$ or $(m, \Delta 1, m')$, respectively.\\
\end{proof}

We can expand on the above proofs to show that our more detailed version of Arrow's Impossibility Theorem (i.e., Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-full}) holds. To do this, we need a final lemma about the behaviour of Dictators.

\parsplit
\begin{lemma}
    Given a Social Welfare Function that satisfies IIA, if there is an individual $i$ such that for two alternatives $a$ and $b$: $a \prec b$ holding for individual $i$ implies it holds in the aggregate, then the Social Welfare Function has a dictator at $i$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    This is a well-known result. For example, see Yu~\cite{arrow-one-shot}.\\
\end{proof}
\parsplit
\begin{corollary}\label{corollary:dictator-sides}
    If a Social Welfare Function satisfies IIA and Non-Dictatorship then for every individual $i$, and two alternatives $a$ and $b$, there is a profile such that $a \prec b$ holds for individual $i$ but $b \prec a$ holds in the aggregate.
\end{corollary}

\parsplit

\arrowfull*
\begin{proof}
    Recall the inclusion $\iota: \APrefs \hookrightarrow \TPrefs$ (see Proposition \ref{proposition:dictator-equivalence-2}). Then, note that in our proof of Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-intermediate-case}, in the 3 alternative case, we were able to use Table \ref{table:case-1} or \ref{table:case-2} to construct a profile $q \in \PN$ such that $\AWelfare(q) = (1,1,1)$, which means $\TWelfare(\iota(q)) = \iota((1,1,1)) = \c$. Likewise, repeating same argument replacing $1$ with $0$, we could also have constructed a distinct profile $q' \in \AProfiles$ such that $\AWelfare(\iota(q')) = (0, 0, 0)$, which implies $\TWelfare(q) = \TWelfare(q') = \c$. Hence, Condition (1) is satisfied. Condition (2) is satisfied, i.e., $q \bwedge q' = (\c, \dots, \c)$ because investigating Tables \ref{table:case-1} and \ref{table:case-2}, there is always an alternative (i.e., the row $m$ in each table) that consists entirely of $0$'s and $1$'s. Moreover, $q$ and $q'$ can be constructed to be opposites of each other on that row, so that $q \wedge q' = \c$ by Proposition \ref{proposition:missing-bounds}. We construct $q$ and $q'$ as follows: if Table \ref{table:case-2} is used then $q$ is strict so that $q' = \neg q$ yields:
    \begin{equation*}
        \AWelfare(q') = \AWelfare(\neg q) = \neg \AWelfare(q) = \neg (1, 1, 1) = (0, 0, 0)
    \end{equation*}
    And $q \wedge q' = q \wedge \neg q = (\c, \dots, \c)$ because $q$ is strict. If Table \ref{table:case-1} is used, we observe without loss of generality if we take $q = (m, m', \Delta 1)$ such that $\AWelfare(q) = (1,1,1)$ as in Theorem \ref{theorem:arrow-intermediate-case}, then by Corollary \ref{corollary:dictator-sides} $\exists m'' \in \3^N$ such that $s_2(m'') = 0$, so that we can take $q' = (\neg m, m'', \Delta 0)$ and find that both $\AWelfare(q') = (0,0,0)$ and $q \bwedge q' = (\c, \dots, \c)$ because $m$ and $\neg m$ are strict and so contradict each other for every individual.\\
\end{proof}

