    \end{minipage}}
    \caption{Visualization interfaces for exploring hierarchical clusters: (a) Tree View displaying the hierarchical organization with nested clusters and their sizes; (b) Map View showing semantic relationships between topics through UMAP dimensionality reduction, with similar topics positioned closer together; (c) Detailed view of a selected cluster showing its summary and constituent sub-clusters with their specific focus areas.}
    \label{fig:visualizations}
\end{figure}


\paragraph{Tree View.} The tree visualization (\Cref{fig:visualizations}a) presents clusters in a hierarchical format, with high-level clusters containing their associated low-level clusters. One can click the button for each high-level cluster to show and hide the associated low-level clusters.
This view enables users to navigate from broad topics (e.g., "AI Ethics, Harmful Content, and Societal Impact") to more specific subtopics (e.g., "AI Ethics, Society, and Cultural Impact"). The tree structure includes the number of contained conversations for each cluster, providing a sense of topic prevalence.

\paragraph{Map View.} The map visualization (\Cref{fig:visualizations}b) projects cluster embeddings into a two-dimensional space using UMAP~\cite{mcinnes2018umap}. This representation preserves semantic relationships between clusters, with similar topics appearing closer together. The map view offers an intuitive understanding of the thematic landscape and reveals natural groupings of related topics. Users can toggle between high-level and low-level views.

When a user selects a specific cluster (\Cref{fig:visualizations}c), the interface displays detailed information about that cluster, including its summary and a list of its sub-clusters. For high-level clusters, users can explore the constituent low-level clusters, each with its own descriptive title. This allows users to quickly understand the scope and content of each cluster without needing to examine individual conversations.

This visualization system enhances the utility of our differentially private summarization framework by making the results more navigable and interpretable, even when dealing with thousands of conversation summaries organized into a complex hierarchy.

