\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
In recent years, quantum computing has emerged as a promising approach to solving complex optimization problems such as TSP (Traveling Salesman Problem) \cite{Laporte1992}, VRP (Vehicle Routing Problem) \cite{Commander2008}, and the Max-Cut problem \cite{Laporte2013}. We are currently in the NISQ (Noisy Intermediate Scale Quantum) era \cite{Preskill2018}, characterized by devices with limited numbers of qubits that are susceptible to noise and decoherence. These constraints have inspired the development of hybrid quantum-classical algorithms, which leverage the strengths of both paradigms. In such frameworks, quantum circuits handle computationally intensive tasks, while classical optimizers iteratively refine results \cite{McClean2016}.

One such hybrid quantum-classical algorithm is QAOA \cite{Farhi2014}, which is particularly suited for tackling combinatorial optimization problems like the Max-Cut problem. Max-Cut problem is a NP-hard problem that involves partitioning a graph's vertices into two subsets to maximize the number of edges between them. QAOA operates by preparing candidate solutions on a quantum circuit using parameters $\gamma$ and $\beta$. A classical optimizer then adjusts these parameters to improve the solution quality \cite{Egger2021}.

Finding optimal parameters for QAOA can be challenging due to issues like barren plateaus \cite{McClean2018} and local minima in the optimization landscape \cite{Wierichs2020}. To address these challenges, effective warm-start methods are crucial for ensuring that the algorithm begins its search near promising regions of the solution space.

To address these challenges in parameter optimization for QAOA, we propose using Adam-assisted Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (Adam-FIPSO) to predict near-optimal parameters. By combining a custom objective function with Adam-FIPSO, our method effectively searches for parameters that balance performance and optimization efficiency. The key components of our approach are listed below:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Custom Objective Function:} We introduce a custom objective function that aims to achieve near-optimal performance while incorporating the Approximation Ratio (AR). This function helps in guiding the optimization process to improve the quality of the predicted solution.
    \item \textbf{Adam-Assisted FIPSO for Parameter Prediction:} We employ the Adam-FIPSO algorithm to predict near-optimal QAOA parameters $\gamma_1, \gamma_2, \gamma_3$ and $\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3$ for varying circuit depths ($p=1, 2, 3$). This optimization approach enhances the search process by utilizing the collective knowledge of the swarm.
    % \item \textbf{A Parallel variant of the Adam-Assisted FIPSO} To solve higher order graphs we alsl propose a parallel variant of the Adam-Assisted FIPSO algorithm
    \item \textbf{Improved Approximate Ratios:} Evaluations using the approximate ratio parameter (comparing the quality of the cut found with the best possible cut) demonstrate that our Adam-FIPSO-based initialization significantly outperforms random initialization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

Several recent studies have investigated machine learning techniques to improve parameter initialization in the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) for combinatorial problems such as Max-Cut. Liang et al.\cite{Liang2024} employ Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) to learn mappings from graph structures to effective QAOA parameters, aiming to reduce reliance on iterative quantum evaluations. Similarly, Meng et al.\cite{Meng2025} introduce a denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) to generate high-quality parameter samples that serve as strong initializations.

While these methods are model-driven and rely on training over large graph datasets, they do not explicitly utilize the QAOA cost landscape during optimization. In contrast, our approach directly optimizes QAOA parameters by evaluating the expectation value of the QAOA circuit for each graph instance. This is achieved using an Adam-assisted Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (Adam-FIPSO) strategy, which combines swarm-based exploration with gradient-based refinement.

By operating directly on the QAOA objective function without the need for learned surrogates, our method provides a flexible and model-free alternative for parameter tuning across varying graph types and circuit depths.

\section{Preliminaries}
\subsection{QAOA}
QAOA is a variational quantum algorithm designed to solve combinatorial optimization problems by leveraging quantum mechanics\cite{Farhi2014}. It operates by encoding an optimization problem into a quantum circuit consisting of alternating cost and mixer Hamiltonians, whose parameters are optimized to approximate the optimal solution.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[scale=0.18]{Pictures/QAOA.png}
  \caption{The QAOA Circuit mechanism showing the cost and mixer circuit components}
  \label{fig:figure_circuit}
\end{figure}

QAOA applies a sequence of unitary transformations using the cost and mixer Hamiltonians. Given a depth parameter $p$, the quantum state evolves as:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \ket{\psi_p(\gamma, \beta)} = e^{-i \beta_p H_M} e^{-i \gamma_p H_C} \\\dots e^{-i \beta_1 H_M} e^{-i \gamma_1 H_C} \ket{+}^{\otimes n}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item The cost Hamiltonian evolution $e^{-i \gamma H_C}$ encodes problem constraints.
    \item The mixer Hamiltonian evolution $e^{-i \beta H_M}$ introduces transitions between states.
    \item The initial state $\ket{+}^{\otimes n}$ is a uniform superposition:
\end{itemize}

\begin{equation}
    \ket{+} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (\ket{0} + \ket{1}).
\end{equation}

The circuit alternates between these operators for $p$ layers, with variational parameters $\gamma = (\gamma_1, \dots, \gamma_p)$ and $\beta = (\beta_1, \dots, \beta_p)$ optimized to minimize the expectation value of $H_C$:

\begin{equation}
    \langle H_C \rangle = \bra{\psi_p(\gamma, \beta)} H_C \ket{\psi_p(\gamma, \beta)}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Max Cut Problem}
The Max-Cut problem is a well-known combinatorial optimization problem where the goal is to partition the vertices of a given graph \( G(V,E) \) into two sets such that the number of edges crossing the partition is maximized. Formally, for a graph \( G \) with \( |V| = n \) vertices and edges \( (i,j) \in E \), the problem can be expressed as maximising the objective function:
% 
\begin{equation}
C(z) = \sum_{(i,j) \in E} \frac{1}{2} (1 - z_i z_j),
\end{equation}
where \( z_i \in \{-1,1\} \) represents the assignment of vertex \( i \) to one of the two partitions. The corresponding cost Hamiltonian in QAOA is:

\begin{equation}
H_C = \sum_{(i,j) \in E} \frac{1}{2} (1 - Z_i Z_j),
\end{equation}


where \( Z_i \) is the Pauli-Z operator acting on the \( i \)th qubit. The ground state of \( H_C \) corresponds to the optimal Max-Cut partition.

