% The velocity update term directs each particle to move towards its personal best and the global best position, while the position update term moves the particle to its new location in the search space. The process is iterated until convergence or a predefined stopping criterion is met.
Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) is a population-based stochastic optimization algorithm originally introduced by Kennedy and Eberhart in 1995~\cite{Kennedy1995}. It draws inspiration from the collective behavior observed in natural systems, such as bird flocking and fish schooling, wherein individual agents (particles) share information and adapt their trajectories based on both individual experiences and social interactions.

In PSO, each particle represents a candidate solution in the search space and iteratively updates its position by two factors: the best solution it has encountered so far which is called the personal best and the best solution identified by any particle in the swarm which is known as the global best. This combination allows the swarm to balance exploration and exploitation during the optimization process.

At each iteration \(t\), the velocity update is given by the following equation:
\begin{equation}
v_i^{(t+1)} = w \cdot v_i^{(t)} + c_1 r_1 \left( p_{\text{best},i} - x_i^{(t)} \right) + c_2 r_2 \left( g_{\text{best}} - x_i^{(t)} \right)
\end{equation}

and the position update is given by the following equation:
\begin{equation}
x_i^{(t+1)} = x_i^{(t)} + v_i^{(t+1)}
\end{equation}

% Where:
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item \(x_i^{(t)} \in \mathbb{R}^d\) denotes the position of particle \(i\) at iteration \(t\)
%     \item \(v_i^{(t)} \in \mathbb{R}^d\) represents the velocity of particle \(i\)
%     \item \(p_{\text{best},i}\) is the best position found so far by particle \(i\)
%     \item \(g_{\text{best}}\) is the best position found by the entire swarm
%     \item \(w\) is the inertia weight controlling the momentum of the particle
%     \item \(c_1, c_2\) are the cognitive and social acceleration coefficients
%     \item \(r_1, r_2 \sim \mathcal{U}(0,1)\) are independent random scalars drawn from a uniform distribution
% \end{itemize}
where \(x_i^{(t)} \in \mathbb{R}^d\) denotes the position of particle \(i\) at iteration \(t\), and \(v_i^{(t)} \in \mathbb{R}^d\) represents its velocity. The term \(p_{\text{best},i}\) refers to the best position discovered by particle \(i\) so far, while \(g_{\text{best}}\) indicates the best position found by any particle in the entire swarm. The scalar \(w\) is the inertia weight that controls the influence of the previous velocity, and \(c_1\) and \(c_2\) are the cognitive and social acceleration coefficients, respectively. Finally, \(r_1\) and \(r_2\) are independent random variables sampled from the uniform distribution \(\mathcal{U}(0,1)\), used to introduce stochasticity into the learning components.

The inertia component \(w \cdot v_i^{(t)}\) encourages particles to continue in their current direction (exploration), while the cognitive and social components bias the motion toward promising regions discovered by the individual or the swarm (exploitation). This iterative process continues until a termination criterion is met, such as convergence to a solution or a maximum number of iterations.


\subsection{Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization}
Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (FIPSO) is an enhanced version of the standard PSO algorithm. In FIPSO, each particle uses information from all other particles in the swarm to update its velocity and position, rather than just using its personal best and the global best\cite{Mendes2004}. This allows the particles to have a more collaborative and informed search for the optimal solution. In essence, each particle is influenced by the positions and velocities of all other particles in the swarm, leading to a more global search and often better convergence behavior.

% The velocity update in FIPSO is given by:

% \begin{equation}
%     \mathbf{v}_i^{(t+1)} = w \mathbf{v}_i^{(t)} + c_1 \sum_{j=1}^{N} \Big[ r_{1,ij} \big(\mathbf{x}_j^{(t)} - \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\big) \Big],
% \end{equation}
% % \mathbf{v}_i^{(t+1)} = w \mathbf{v}_i^{(t)} + c_1 \sum_{j=1}^{N} \Big[ r_{1,ij} \big(\mathbf{x}_j^{(t)} - \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\big) \Big]

% \begin{equation}
%     \mathbf{x}_i^{(t+1)} = \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)} + \mathbf{v}_i^{(t+1)},
% \end{equation}
% where:
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item \(\mathbf{v}_i^{(t)}\): Particle \(i\)'s velocity at iteration \(t\),
%     \item \(\mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\): Particle \(i\)'s position at iteration \(t\),
%     \item \(N\): Total number of particles in the swarm,
%     \item \(r_{1,ij}\): Random number associated with the influence of particle \(j\) on particle \(i\),
%     \item \(w\): Inertia weight controlling exploration vs. exploitation,
%     \item \(c_1\): Acceleration coefficient.
% \end{itemize}
The velocity and position update in Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (FIPSO) is given by the following equations:

\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{v}_i^{(t+1)} = w \mathbf{v}_i^{(t)} + c_1 \sum_{j=1}^{N} \Big[ r_{1,ij} \big(\mathbf{x}_j^{(t)} - \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\big) \Big]
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{x}_i^{(t+1)} = \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)} + \mathbf{v}_i^{(t+1)}
\end{equation}

% Where:
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item \(\mathbf{v}_i^{(t)}\) is the velocity of particle \(i\) at iteration \(t\)
%     \item \(\mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\) is the position of particle \(i\) at iteration \(t\)
%     \item \(N\) is the total number of particles in the swarm
%     \item \(r_{1,ij}\) is a random scalar determining the influence of particle \(j\) on particle \(i\)
%     \item \(w\) is the inertia weight that balances exploration and exploitation
%     \item \(c_1\) is the acceleration coefficient governing social influence
% \end{itemize}
where \(\mathbf{v}_i^{(t)}\) denotes the velocity of particle \(i\) at iteration \(t\), and \(\mathbf{x}_i^{(t)}\) is its corresponding position. The variable \(N\) represents the total number of particles in the swarm. The term \(r_{1,ij}\) is a random scalar drawn from a uniform distribution, representing the stochastic influence of particle \(j\) on particle \(i\). The inertia weight \(w\) controls the trade-off between exploration and exploitation, while the acceleration coefficient \(c_1\) governs the magnitude of social influence exerted by the swarm on each particle.

In this update, instead of just following its own best position and the global best, each particle considers the influence of all other particles in the swarm. This results in each particle receiving a more "fully informed" update and contributes to more diverse exploration of the search space.

\subsection{Adam Assisted FIPSO}
Adam-Assisted Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (Adam-FIPSO) combines the strengths of Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (FIPSO) and the Adam optimization algorithm, which is widely used for adaptive learning rate adjustments in machine learning\cite{Mei2024}. The idea behind Adam-FIPSO is to enhance the convergence speed and stability of the original FIPSO by incorporating Adam's adaptive learning rates into the velocity update mechanism of the particles. The adaptive learning rate helps the particles move efficiently through the search space, particularly in high-dimensional or complex optimization problems.

In Adam-FIPSO, the velocity update equation is modified to include the adaptive moment estimates used in the Adam optimizer, which adjusts the learning rates based on both the first and second moments of the gradients.

