\subsection{Objective Function}
The objective function for the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) is defined as:

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \text{MSE}( \hat{C}, C_{\text{target}}) + \lambda \cdot \text{AR penalty},
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \(\hat{C} = \text{predicted cut}\), the output of the QAOA cost function.
    \item \(C_{\text{target}}\) is the target cut calculated classically.
    \item MSE stands for the Mean Squared Error:
    \[
    \text{MSE} = (\hat{C} - C_{\text{target}})^2
    \]
    \item AR represents the Approximate Ratio, which is calculated as:
    \[
        AR = 
        \begin{cases}
            \dfrac{\hat{C}}{C_{\text{target}}} & \text{if } y_{\text{target}} \neq 0, \\
            0 & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases}
    \]
    The approximate ratio measures how close the predicted cut is to the target cut.

    \item The AR penalty is defined as the squared difference between 1 and the approximate ratio:
    \[
    \text{AR penalty} = (1 - AR)^2
    \]
    This term penalizes predictions that are far from the target cut in terms of accuracy ratio.

    \item \(\lambda\) is a regularization parameter that controls the weight of the AR penalty in the objective function. If the AR penalty is larger, the objective function will put more emphasis on minimizing the difference in accuracy ratio.

\end{itemize}

The final objective function, \(\mathcal{L}(\theta)\), thus combines both the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss and the AR penalty to guide the optimization towards both accurate predictions and maintaining a reasonable accuracy ratio.

\subsection{Synthesis}
Each graph instance, generated from either the Erdős--Rényi (ER) or Watts--Strogatz (WS) model, is first encoded as an adjacency matrix and used to construct the corresponding QAOA circuit. The edges in the graph correspond to the connections between the qubits and the gates in the circuit. Figure \ref{fig:q_circuit_4node} shows the equivalent QAOA circuit for a 4 node graph. The depth of the QAOA circuit, defined by the number of alternating mixer and cost layers, is varied to evaluate performance across multiple configurations. To optimize the QAOA parameters $(\gamma, \beta)$, the Adam-assisted Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (Adam-FIPSO) algorithm is employed. At each iteration, candidate solutions are refined using gradient information estimated via finite differences, and the Adam optimizer is applied to adaptively update the search trajectory. This combination of collective intelligence and gradient-based correction ensures both exploration and precision. For every graph and circuit depth, the optimizer seeks parameter values that maximize the expected cut value as estimated by the QAOA circuit.

