\section{Evaluation Methodology}

We evaluate our QAOA optimization framework on randomly generated graphs drawn from two families: Erdős–Rényi (ER) \cite{Erdos1959} and Watts–Strogatz (WS)\cite{Watts1998}. Graph sizes range from 3 to 16 nodes. For each graph model and node size, five unique graph instances are generated and evaluated. Below, we describe the graph generation process, QAOA configuration, and how performance is assessed across different initialization strategies.

\subsection{Graph Generation}
For each graph size \( n \in [3, 16] \), we generate five unique instances for both ER and WS models. Erdős–Rényi graphs are constructed using a fixed edge probability \( p = 0.5 \). Watts–Strogatz graphs are generated with a rewiring probability \( p = 0.3 \) and neighborhood parameter \( k \), which is set to 4 when \( n \geq 4 \), and adjusted to 2 otherwise to ensure a valid WS structure.

Each graph is represented as an adjacency matrix and used to build the corresponding QAOA circuit.

\subsection{Parameter Optimization and Evaluation}
For each graph instance and QAOA depth \( p \in \{1, 2, 3\} \), the initial cut value is computed using randomly sampled QAOA parameters from the range \( [-\pi, \pi] \). These are then compared against the optimized parameters produced by the Adam-assisted Fully Informed Particle Swarm Optimization (Adam-FIPSO) algorithm.

Performance is evaluated in terms of the cut value obtained from the QAOA circuit, with the classical cut value computed using the one-exchange approximation algorithm as a reference. The improvement percentage of PSO-optimized parameters over random initialization is also reported for each configuration.

\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{Adam\_FIPSO}} = \frac{ \mathrm{QAOA\text{-}Cost}(\gamma_{\mathrm{Adam\_FIPSO}},\ \beta_{\mathrm{Adam\_FIPSO}}) }{ \mathrm{MaxCut}(G) }
\end{equation}


\subsection{Random Baseline}
To establish a baseline for comparison, a single random sample of QAOA parameters \((\gamma,\beta)\) is drawn from a uniform distribution over the range \([-\pi, \pi]\). This process is repeated separately for each QAOA depth \( p \in \{1, 2, 3\} \). 

The resulting QAOA cost is computed for each graph instance and normalized by the classical MaxCut value to yield the approximation ratio. 

\begin{equation}
     \mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{random}} = 
    \frac{\mathrm{QAOA\text{-}Cost}(\gamma, \beta)}{\mathrm{MaxCut}(G)}
\end{equation}


This approximation ratio quantifies the performance of a randomly initialized QAOA circuit without optimization. It serves as a baseline for evaluating the effectiveness of the optimized parameters produced by Adam-FIPSO across different circuit depths.

\subsection{Maximum Cut Computation}
The exact maximum cut value is determined through brute force by evaluating all \(2^n\) possible partitions of the graph. 
Algorithm \ref{alg:maxcut} outlines this computation process.


\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\textsc{Compute MaxCut Bruteforce}}
\label{alg:maxcut}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $\text{adj\_matrix}$, an $n \times n$ adjacency matrix.
\Ensure $\text{best\_cut}$, the size of the maximum cut.

\State $G \gets \text{GraphFromAdjacency}(\text{adj\_matrix})$
\State $n \gets \text{NumberOfNodes}(G)$
\State $\text{best\_cut} \gets 0$

\For{$\text{subset\_mask} \gets 0$ \textbf{to} $2^n - 1$}
    \State $\text{subset:}$
    \For{$i \gets 0$ \textbf{to} $n-1$}
        \If{$(\text{subset\_mask} \,\textbf{AND}\, (1 \ll i)) \neq 0$}
            \State $\text{subset} \gets \text{subset} \cup \{i\}$
        \EndIf
    \EndFor
    \State $\text{cut\_size} \gets \text{CutSize}(G,\;\text{subset})$
    \State $\text{best\_cut} \gets \max(\text{best\_cut}, \text{cut\_size})$
\EndFor

\Return $\text{best\_cut}$

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Improvement Metric}

The performance enhancement achieved through Adam-FIPSO is quantified using the normalized approximation ratio (AR). Let \(\mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{Adam\_FIPSO}}\) denote the mean approximation ratio obtained using Adam-FIPSO initialized QAOA parameters, and \(\mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{random}}\) represent the average approximation ratio under random parameter initialization. The improvement is defined as the relative percentage increase over the baseline and computed as:

\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{Improvement} 
  = 
  \frac{\mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{Adam\_FIPSO}} - \mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{random}}}
       {\mathrm{AR}_{\mathrm{random}}}
  \times 100\%
\end{equation}


\subsection{Results and Discussion}

Tables \ref{tab:erdos_renyi_improvements} and \ref{tab:watts_strogatz_improvements} present the improvement percentages across QAOA depths \(p = 1, 2, 3\) for Erdos Renyi and Watts Strogatz graphs respectively. These results reflect the effectiveness of Adam-FIPSO in boosting the approximation ratio compared to randomly initialized QAOA parameters.

For Erdos Renyi graphs, Adam-FIPSO consistently improves performance across all node sizes, with particularly large gains at lower node counts. Notably, Nodes 3, 4, and 5 exhibit dramatic improvements at all depths, peaking at over 195\% for Node 5 at \(p=2\). As the number of nodes increases, the average improvement tends to decline, which is consistent with the increased complexity and parameter space of larger graphs.

% Watts Strogatz graphs show a similar pattern. Exceptional improvements are observed at small node sizes, such as a remarkable 1547.7\% gain for Node 3 at \(p=3\), and consistent improvements between 20\% and 60\% across most mid-sized graphs. These results suggest that Adam-FIPSO is highly effective not only at providing better initial parameter guesses than random baselines but also in navigating the optimization landscape of structured graphs.
Watts--Strogatz graphs demonstrate a clear trend of performance improvement with Adam-FIPSO across all QAOA depths. Notably, Node~3 exhibits the most substantial gains, with approximation ratio improvements of 404.9\% at $p=1$, 449.7\% at $p=2$, and 162.5\% at $p=3$. While the magnitude of improvement decreases with increasing node size, mid-sized graphs (Nodes~5--16) still achieve consistent enhancements ranging from approximately 20\% to 50\%. These findings confirm that Adam-FIPSO significantly outperforms random initialization, not only by offering superior parameter estimates but also by effectively traversing the complex optimization landscape inherent in structured WS graphs.

This framework is particularly well-suited for domains where MaxCut formulations are frequently encountered, such as statistical physics, circuit layout design, portfolio optimization, and image segmentation. In these applications, the ability to rapidly compute high-quality solutions is critical, especially under real-time or near-real-time constraints. In scenarios where problem instances are dynamic or evolve continuously, a lightweight, population-based optimizer like Adam-FIPSO could offer a practical advantage by producing near-optimal QAOA parameters without requiring full retraining.
