The Adam-FIPSO framework demonstrates robust and effective performance in guiding QAOA parameter initialization, consistently outperforming random baselines in terms of approximation ratio. This advantage is particularly evident in low-depth QAOA circuits and smaller graph instances, where the optimization landscape tends to be smoother and the parameter space more tractable.

For a fixed QAOA depth \(p\), Adam-FIPSO reliably converges toward regions of the parameter space that yield near-optimal cut values achievable under the given circuit constraints. Notably, the primary limitations in performance arise not from the optimizer itself, but from the expressive capacity of the QAOA ansatz and the computational cost associated with larger graphs and deeper circuits. As the graph size increases and circuit depth \(p\) grows, the dimensionality of the parameter space and the quantum simulation complexity both increase substantially. While higher-depth QAOA circuits allow for more expressive quantum states and potentially better approximation ratios, they also impose greater computational demands. Within these practical constraints, Adam-FIPSO effectively identifies high-quality parameters, and further gains are expected with enhanced computational resources and deeper circuit implementations.

