\section{Introduction}
Multi-agent navigation plays a critical role in many real-world applications, including autonomous mobile robots in automated warehouses, ports, and manufacturing factories~\cite{Wang2023SMC,zhang2021safe,Mestres2024ral,Fu2023tac}, drone swarms~\cite{zhang2025tro}, and unmanned surface vehicles~\cite{huang2024tiv}. However, for agents operating with limited sensing radii in unknown and cluttered environments, existing approaches often struggle with collisions and deadlocks, particularly as the number of agents scales to several hundreds~\cite{Wang2023SMC,zhang2025tro,farivarnejad2024local,Wang2017tro}. Centralized control strategies suffer from poor scalability and limited real-time adaptability to dynamic environments~\cite{Safaoui2024tro}. As a result, distributed control is often preferred for large-scale multi-agent systems (MAS), where each agent makes decisions based solely on local neighbor information~\cite{wu2025smc,Fu2023tac,yan2025tnnls,Mrdjan2024cst}. Nevertheless, developing distributed navigation strategies that provide explicit safety guarantees for MAS remains a significant challenge.



% it is essential to ensure that agents operate without collisions in obstacles environments under limited sensing radius. Distributed control provides a scalable and robust way for multi-agent systems by using only local information from each agent and its neighbors \cite{yan2025tnnls,Tan2022csl}. Up to now, safety guarantees under distributed control for large-scale multi-agent systems have not been adequately addressed under limited sensing radius, particular under arbitrary time-varying neighborhoods.



To encourage safety, artificial potential fields (APF) have been proposed in MAS navigation~\cite{farivarnejad2024local,abaunza2024quadrotor}. However, they typically require obstacles to be convex, and the design of APF can be complex. Recent works have proposed control barrier functions (CBFs) to ensure safety by enforcing the controlled invariance of a designated safe set~\cite{Amestac2017,Sabouni2024auto,xiao2022sufficient,han2021reinforcement,yang2023cdc}. Most of these studies adopted quadratic programs (QPs) with CBF constraints (referred to as QP-CBFs) to provide safety assurances for MAS navigation~\cite{wu2025smc,yan2025tnnls,Mrdjan2024cst}. To improve scalability, distributed QP-CBF approaches have been developed~\cite{Mestres2024ral,Wang2017tro}.
These existing methods rely on hand-crafted CBFs designed using prior knowledge of the centers and radii of circles that bound the obstacles. In unknown environments, however, such information is typically unavailable, making it difficult to construct suitable hand-crafted CBFs in advance. Moreover, hand-crafted CBFs are highly sensitive to the chosen analytical forms and hyperparameters. Poor designs can lead to deadlocks, sharp and unnecessary evasive actions, or inefficient trajectories, particularly in dense environments with frequent agent interactions.
