Recent years have witnessed the rise of learning-based safe control methods for MAS. Several works have integrated model predictive control (MPC) into reinforcement learning frameworks to enable safety-aware planning~\cite{Vinod2024tcst,Safaoui2024tro,zhang2025towardtro}. However, the high computational cost of MPC limits scalability in large-scale systems. Furthermore,~\cite{zhang2025towardtro} assumed a fixed number of neighbors per agent, which restricts applicability in dynamic environments with time-varying interaction topologies.
To address such limitations, recent studies have explored graph neural networks (GNNs), which naturally handle arbitrarily time-varying interaction graphs, as a means to parameterize MAS navigation policies~\cite{Ma2023ITSC,yu2023learning}. However, these GNN-based policies promote safety only implicitly through loss design, without formal guarantees.

To provide more explicit safety assurances, recent works have proposed parameterizing CBFs for MAS using GNNs~\cite{zhang2023neural,zhang2025tro,zhang2025discrete}. For example,\cite{zhang2023neural,zhang2025tro} introduced a GNN-parameterized CBF (referred to as a graph CBF) framework, where the learned distributed controller imitates the solution of a QP-graph-CBF (QP-GCBF) formulation with predefined nominal controllers. However, this approach inherits the short-horizon limitations of QP-CBFs: agents can approach obstacles or each other too closely, leading to collisions or deadlocks in dense environments. Meanwhile, the constrained QP lacks recursive feasibility guarantees and can become infeasible at certain time instances. Moreover, the method relies on a precomputed nominal controller for goal-reaching, which often results in poor trade-offs between safety and goal-reaching performance~\cite{zhang2023neural,zhang2025tro}.

To eliminate dependence on nominal controllers, \cite{zhang2025discrete} proposed learning graph CBF-constrained navigation policies directly in a discrete-time formulation. Nevertheless, this method still requires manually tuned static parameters to conservatively balance safety and goal-reaching. As in many existing safe learning approaches\cite{Dawood2025RAL,Ma2023ITSC,yu2023learning,han2021reinforcement}, such static trade-offs are sensitive to environmental variations and agent density, often resulting in inefficient navigation, failure to reach goals, and even safety violations in practice.
