To tackle the aforementioned challenges, we propose a paradigm shift toward an \textit{infinite-horizon} CBF-constrained optimal control formulation for safe multi-agent navigation. We analyze its solution through the lens of Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equations and Lagrange duality, and develop a novel physics-informed learning framework that approximates the solution using GNNs. The resulting distributed control policy effectively avoids deadlocks and significantly improves navigation performance. More specifically, our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item We develop a novel HJB-GNN learning framework for distributed safe multi-agent navigation. It jointly learns a value function, a graph CBF, and a GNN-based distributed tracking controller. Leveraging the ability of GNNs to process variable-sized inputs, our method removes the fixed-neighbor assumption commonly made in existing results~\cite{zhang2025towardtro,saravanos2023distributed}. The resulting controller eliminates structural limitations caused by the use of predefined nominal controllers in~\cite{zhang2025tro,zhang2023neural}, which lead to suboptimal safety-task trade-offs. Our approach also overcomes the short-horizon limitation inherent in QP-CBF methods~\cite{zhang2025tro,Mrdjan2024cst,Mestres2024ral,Wang2017tro,Fu2023tac,wu2025smc,yan2025tnnls}, which often cause deadlocks or collisions, while simultaneously avoiding the significant computational burden associated with MPC-based safe learning frameworks~\cite{Vinod2024tcst,Safaoui2024tro,zhang2025towardtro}.  

%     We establish a new HJB-based theoretical result that guarantees safety via a single graph CBF for MASs without the need of fixed number of neighbors assumed in \cite{zhang2025towardtro,saravanos2023distributed}. Based on such result, we develop the HJB-GNN learning framework, which jointly learns a value function, a graph CBF, and a distributed controller, where GNNs are used to capture arbitrarily time-varying neighborhood topologies under limited sensing radius.
% Compared to most existing QP-CBF methods \cite{Fu2023tac,zhang2025tro,wu2025smc,gao2023mrs}, our infinite-horizon formulation effectively mitigates the limitations of short-horizon QPs, such as deadlocks, infeasibility, and collisions in dense and cluttered environments, 


\item By solving HJB equations with graph CBF constraints, we introduce state-dependent Lagrange multipliers via Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions, enabling dynamic trade-offs between safety and goal-reaching performance. Such design removes the need of manually tuned static weights in most existing safe learning methods~\cite{Dawood2025RAL,zhang2025discrete,zhang2023neural,yu2023learning,Yang2024tac}, which are typically sensitive to environmental variations and prone to performance degradation in dense environments, thereby enhancing robustness and generalizability of our approach.


\item We propose a new loss function that includes Bellman errors, Lyapunov candidate conditions, graph CBF constraint penalties, and supervised guidance from KKT-based safe controllers, enabling the learning of infinite-horizon distributed safe control policy. In contrast to traditional approaches that rely on hand-crafted APFs~\cite{farivarnejad2024local,abaunza2024quadrotor} or CBFs~\cite{Mrdjan2024cst,Mestres2024ral,cohen2023safe,Wang2017tro,Amestac2017,Sabouni2024auto,bandyopadhyay2023hjb,xiao2022sufficient,Fu2023tac}, our learned graph CBF via GNNs provides a scalable, data-driven safety certification mechanism that generalizes to unseen and dynamic environments. Notably, policy trained on a handful of agents generalize effectively to systems with hundreds or even thousands of agents.





\item  We perform extensive numerical simulations on three representative systems, i.e., 2D nonlinear unmanned surface vessels, 2D linear double integrators, and 3D nonlinear Crazyflie drones, demonstrating significantly improved safety, scalability, and generalizability compared with the state-of-the-art QP-GCBF+ method in \cite{zhang2025tro}. Moreover, we conduct comprehensive hardware experiments using Crazyflie drones swarms on challenging antipodal position-swapping tasks under static and dynamic obstacles, demonstrating the real-world applicability of our approach\footnote{Simulation and  experiment videos can be found at: \href{https://nus-core.github.io/assets/standalone/HJB-GNN/index.html}{https://nus-core.github.io/assets/standalone/HJB-GNN/index.html}.}.

% \item We introduce a new HJB-based loss function that unifies graph CBF safety constraint and goal-reaching objective into a single optimization formulation. This eliminates the need for handcrafted CBFs typically required for single-agent \cite{Amestac2017,Sabouni2024auto,cohen2023safe,bandyopadhyay2023hjb} and MASs \cite{Wang2023SMC,gao2023mrs,yan2025tnnls,Fu2023tac}, as well as the dependence on predefined nominal controllers required in \cite{zhang2023neural,zhang2025tro}. Such design enhances scalability, generalizability, and applicability across diverse MAS scenarios,   

\end{enumerate}

