\section{Preliminaries}\label{Section2}

\subsection{System Dynamics}\label{Section2.1}
In this paper, we study the problem of distributed safe multi-agent navigation where  each agent $i\in V_a$, $V_a=\{1,2,...,N\}$, is required to reach its goal position while 
avoiding collisions with other agents and obstacles. The agents are governed by the following dynamics:
% Consider a class of nonlinear multi-agent systems with 
% $N$ agents, whose dynamics are described by
\begin{align}\label{mas}
\dot{x}_i=f_i(x_i)+g_i(x_i)u_i,\ i=1,2,...,N,
\end{align}
where for the $i$-th agent, $x_i\in X\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ and $u_i\in U\subset\mathbb{R}^m$ denote the state and control input, respectively, and $f_i:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $g_i:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$ are two locally Lipschitz continuous functions. Denote $\bar{x}=(x_1,x_2,...,x_N)\in X^N$ and $\bar{u}=(u_1,u_2,...,u_N)^T\in U^N$ as the state vector and the control input vector of the MAS (\ref{mas}), respectively. For notational simplicity and to facilitate analysis, we assume that all agents share identical dynamic and thus write $f=f_i$ and $g=g_i$ for all $i=1,2,...,N$. The proposed approach can be extended to heterogeneous MASs where the dynamics of agents can be different.




\emph{Topology Graph:} 
Let $R_a>0$ denote the sensing radius of each agent. It is assumed that each agent can observe other agents and static obstacles located within the sensing radius. The interactions among agents and between agents and obstacles are represented by a direct time-varying graph $G(t)=(V(t), E(t))$, where $V(t)=V_a\cup V_o(t)$ is the set of nodes which contains the set of agent nodes $V_a$ and the set of obstacle nodes $V_o(t)$, and $E(t)\subset\{(i,j)|i\in V_a,j\in V(t)\}$ denotes the set of edges. An edge $(i,j)\in E(t)$ with $i\in V_a,j\in V(t)$ implies that agent $i$ senses an agent or an obstacle, i.e., their Euclidean distance is \textit{less than or equal} to $R_a$. The topology graph $G(t)$ and its edge set $E(t)$ are allowed to vary arbitrarily over time. In contrast to the fixed number of neighbors assumed in \cite{zhang2025towardtro,saravanos2023distributed}, such arbitrarily time-varying topologies naturally capture realistic settings in which agents move, and consequently, their local neighborhoods evolve due to limited sensing capabilities. 
% It s allowed that the graph $G(t)$ and its edge set $E(t)$ are arbitrarily time-varying. The arbitrarily time-varying topology can better capture practical scenarios where agents move and then their neighborhoods change due to limited sensing radius.

% All agents are assumed to have the same dynamics that mean $f_i=f$, $g_i=g$, $ X_i= X$, and $U_i=U$ for all $i=1,2,...,N$. The states of each agent may denote continuous quantities with respect to the multi-agent system dynamics such as position, velocity, attitude, and so on. 
\subsection{Safety of Multi-Agent Systems}\label{Section2.2}
To formally characterize the safety of MAS, we consider a position space $\mathbb{P}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n_d}$, where $n_d=2$ or $n_d=3$ represents the spatial dimension of the considered navigation problem. In the system (\ref{mas}), it is assumed that the first $n_d$ entries of the state vector $x_i\in X$ correspond to the position $p_i\in\mathbb{P}$ of agent $i\in V_a$. For each agent $i\in V_a$, we define a local obstacle sensing function to represent the set of neighboring obstacles within its sensing radius, i.e.,  
\begin{align}
O_i=(O_{i1},O_{i2},...,O_{in_{oi}}),
\end{align}
where, for example, in the case of a LiDAR-based sensing system, $O_{ij}\in X$ represents the sensed information obtained by the $j$-th LiDAR ray, $j=1,2,...,n_{oi}$, $n_{oi}\in\mathbb{N}$ denoting the total number of LiDAR rays. For convenience, each vector $O_{ij}$ is defined such that its first $n_d$ elements represent the relative position $P^o_{ij}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n_d}$ with respect to agent $i$ obtained by $j$-th LiDAR ray, while the remaining $n-n_d$ entries are zero-padded. 
% where each $O_{ij}\in X$ represents the encoded information of the $j$-th sensed obstacle, $j=1,2,...,n_{oi}$, and $n_{oi}\in\mathbb{N}$ is the number of obstacles sensed by agent $i$. 
To ensure obstacle avoidance, the agent must maintain a minimum safety distance $r>0$ from each sensed obstacle, leading to the constraint
\begin{align}\label{obs}
\|O_{ij}\|>r,\ \forall j=1,2,...,n_{oi}.
\end{align}
Additionally, inter-agent collision avoidance requires that the Euclidean distance between any pair of agents $i,j\in V_a,i\neq j$ remains strictly greater than $2r$ with $2r<R_a$, where $r$ represents the radius of a minimal circle enclosing each agent's physical body. The overall safe set $S\subset X^{N}$ of the MAS (\ref{mas}) is thus defined as 
\begin{subequations}\label{S}
\begin{align}
 S=&\cap_{i=1}^{N}S_i,\\
 S_i=&\Big\{\bar{x}\in X^N\Big|\big\|
O_{ij}\big\|>r,\, \forall j=1,2,...,n_{oi},\nonumber\\ &\qquad\qquad\min_{i,j\in V_a,i\neq j}\big\|p_{i}-p_j\big\|>2r\Big\}.   
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Such set characterizes the overall safety requirements of agent-obstacle and inter-agent collision avoidance under limited sensing radius. 
% The unsafe set $A_N$ is denoted by the complement of the safe set $S$ in $ X^N$, i.e., $A_N= X^N\setminus S^{N}$.
Based on these, the following definition about the safety of MAS (\ref{mas}) is given. 



\begin{definition} (Controlled Invariance and Safety of MAS) The set $S$ is controlled invariant with respect to the MAS (\ref{mas}), if, for any initial state $\bar{x}(t_0)\in S$, there exist controller $\bar{u}$, such that $\bar{x}(t)\in S$, $\forall t\ge t_0$. The MAS (\ref{mas}) is called safe with respect to the set $S$ if the set $S$ is controlled invariant. 
\end{definition}



