\section{Safe Multi-Agent Navigation via Constrained Optimal Control}\label{Section3}
In this section, we first formulate the safe optimal control problem for multi-agent navigation. We then reformulate it as a graph CBF-constrained HJB equation. To solve this, we derive and analyze the associated KKT conditions, which lead to a navigation policy that dynamically balances safety and goal-reaching performance.



\subsection{Safe Optimal Control Formulation}

For each agent $i \in V_a$, let $p_i^g \in \mathbb{P}$ denote its desired goal position, and define the overall goal position vector as $p^g = (p_1^{g}, p_2^{g}, \dots, p_N^{g})$. Based on this, we consider an infinite-horizon cost function for each agent $i \in V_a$ defined as
\begin{align}\label{cost}
J_i(e_i(t_0),u_i)=\int_{t_0}^{\infty}r_i(e_i(\tau),u_i(\tau))d\tau,\ i\in V_a,
\end{align}
where each vactor $e_i\in  X$ is defined such that its first $n_d$ elements represent the tracking error $p_i-p_i^{g}$ of agent $i$, while the remaining $n-n_d$ entries are zero-padded, and the immediate cost function is given by $r_i(e_i,u_i)=e_i^TQe_i+u_i^TRu_i$ with $Q \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ and $R \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m}$ being two symmetric positive definite matrices that weight the tracking error and control effort, respectively. The control objective is to minimize the cost \eqref{cost} for agent $i \in V_a$, subjected to safety constraints that the system state remains within the safe set $S$. Accordingly, for the MAS (\ref{mas}), the safe optimal control problem is formulated as 
\begin{subequations}\label{cop}
\begin{align}
&\min_{u_i\in U}\quad J_i(e_i(t_0),u_i)\label{cop1}\\
&\ {\rm s.t.}\quad \dot{x}_i=f_i(x_i)+g_i(x_i)u_i,\label{cop2}\\
&\qquad\ \  \bar{x}(t)\in S,\ \ {\rm for}\ {\rm all}\ t\ge t_0,
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
for all $i\in V_a$. 

