\section{HJB-GNN: Physics-informed Learning of Distributed Safe Navigation with Graph Neural Networks}\label{Section4}

In this section, we develop a novel HJB-GNN learning framework for training distributed safe multi-agent navigation policy.

\subsection{Neural Value Function, Graph CBF, and Distributed Safe Controller}\label{Section4.1}

To handle the complexity of arbitrarily time-varying interaction topologies and ensure scalability to large-scale MASs, GNNs have emerged as powerful tools for learning over graph-structured data. Traditional neural architectures, such as fully connected networks, cannot handle variable-size inputs and fail to generalize over dynamically changing graph structures. In contrast, GNNs provide a learning framework for encoding local interactions in MASs. 
Therefore, to effectively capture time-varying graph $G(t)$ of the MAS (\ref{mas}), we employ GNNs to parameterize both a graph CBF $h_{\omega}$ and a distributed safe controller $\pi_\varphi$, where $\omega$ and $\varphi$ denote the corresponding neural network parameters. Unlike previous safe learning methods \cite{zhang2025towardtro,saravanos2023distributed,Vinod2024tcst,yan2025tnnls}, which can only support fixed size input, GNNs inherently supports variable input sizes, which eliminates the need for padding or truncation when the size of neighbor set $|\tilde{N}_i(t)|$ does not equal to $M$. In addition, since the value function only depends on goal tracking error of each agent not topology graph, an MLP is used to parameterize the value function $V_\theta$, where $\theta$ denotes the network parameter.

% To characterize time-varying graph $G(t)$ of arbitrarily sized multi-agent systems, graph neural network is naturally a powerful tool and thus used to parameterize a graph CBF $h_{\omega}$ and a safe distributed controller $\pi_\varphi$ with two neural network parameters $\omega$ and $\varphi$. Since the value function does not depend on the topology graph $G(t)$, non-GNNs are used to parameterize a value function $V_\theta$ with a neural network parameter $\theta$. 

% Furthermore, since the optimal value function $V_\theta(e_i)$, $\forall i\in V_a$ only depends on the relative distance between each agent and its goal, $V_\theta$ is approximated using non-graph neural networks with the neural network parameter $\theta$, independent of the topology graph. 

