The value function loss $L_{vi}(\theta,\varphi)$ of agent $i\in V_a$ consists of \textit{candidate Lyapunov function loss} $\tilde{L}_{vi}(\theta)$ and squared \textit{Bellman error loss} $\delta_i^2(\theta,\varphi)$, i.e.,
\begin{align}\label{lvi}
L_{vi}(\theta,\varphi)=b_{v1}\tilde{L}_{vi}(\theta)+b_{v2}\delta_i^2(\theta,\varphi), 
\end{align}
where $b_{v1},\ b_{v2}>0$ are two constant weights. Minimizing the candidate Lyapunov function loss encourages the learned value function $V_{\theta}(e_i)$ to act as a candidate Lyapunov function for the system of goal tracking error $e_i$. Inspired by the requirement that a candidate Lyapunov function be both positive definite and radially unbounded~\cite{khalil1996nonlinear}, we design the following loss to enforce these properties:
\begin{align}\label{Lvir}
\tilde{L}_{vi}(\theta)=&\max\big\{0,\alpha_1(\|e_i\|)-V_{\theta}(e_i)\big\}\nonumber\\
&+\max\big\{0,V_{\theta}(e_i)-\alpha_2(\|e_i\|)\big\},
\end{align}
where $\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$ are of class $\mathcal{K}_\infty$ functions satisfying $\alpha_1(s)<\alpha_2(s)$ for any $s$. 
% It encourages $V_\theta(e_i)$ to be positive definite. 
The Bellman error, derived from the HJB equation (\ref{hjb-opt}) and the Hamiltonian
function (\ref{optimal-H}), is
\begin{align}\label{bell}
&\delta_i(\theta,\varphi)
=H_i(e_i,\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i),\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i))
-H_i(e_i,u_i^*,\nabla_{e_i} V_i^*(e_i))\nonumber\\
=&\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)f(x_i)+\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)g(x_i)\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i)+r_{i}(e_i,\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i)).
\end{align}
Minimizing the squared Bellman error $\delta_i^2(\theta,\varphi)$ in (\ref{lvi}) encourages the satisfaction of the HJB optimality condition in an approximate sense, i.e., $H_i(e_i,\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i),\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i))=0$, guiding $V_\theta(e_i)$ toward the true value function $V_i^*(e_i)$. Meanwhile, minimizing $\delta_i^2(\theta,\varphi)$ encourages the time derivative $\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)f(x_i)+\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)g(x_i)\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i)$ of function $V_\theta(e_i)$ to be negative definite along system trajectory of goal tracking error $e_i$. Together with the candidate Lyapunov loss $\tilde{L}_{vi}(\theta)$, this promotes asymptotic convergence of the goal tracking error $e_i$. It aligns with the fact that the true value function $V_i^*(e_i)$ serves as a Lyapunov function for the tracking error system in Theorem~\ref{the1}, and $V_\theta(e_i)$ is trained to inherit such property. 
% In addition, it steers the policy $\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i)$ toward minimizing the Hamiltonian function $H_i(e_i,\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i),\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i))$. As shown in the proof of Theorem~\ref{the1}, the optimal value function serves as a Lyapunov function. Therefore, minimizing such Hamiltonian function promotes negative definiteness of the Lyapunov derivative $\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)f(x_i)+\nabla_{e_i} V_\theta(e_i)g(x_i)\pi_{\varphi}(\eta_i)$ along system trajectory of tracking error $e_i$, and combined with the candidate Lyapunov function loss $\tilde{L}_{vi}(\theta)$, ensuring asymptotic convergence of $e_i$. The value function (\ref{lvi}) thus integrates learning with infinite-horizon optimal control theory, embedding goal-reaching performance and optimality guarantees into the learning framework based on the HJB formulation. 


