\emph{\textbf{Four Agents with Four Obstacles:}} 
The simulation results are plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig.3} under a $2{\rm m}\times1.5{\rm m}$ environment with four agents and four static obstacles. Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (a) shows that all agents safely reach their respective goals. Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (b) displays the graph CBF contour for agent 2 at $t=0.6{\rm s}$, where the color bar encodes the scalar learned graph CBF value $h_\omega(\eta_2)$. It spans from negative to positive values: negative values (gradient-red regions) indicate potential collisions with neighboring agents and obstacles, and positive values (gradient-blue regions) reflect safety margins, and the zero value (black line) denotes the learned safety boundary around the agent 2. Agent 4 lies outside its sensing range and thus does not influence the graph CBF value of agent 2. Our learned graph CBF enforces real-time safety guarantees in environments with unknown obstacle shapes and time-varying neighborhoods, unlike the handcrafted CBFs typically constructed requiring fixed geometric functions and prior knowledge of obstacle shapes\cite{Mrdjan2024cst,Mestres2024ral,cohen2023safe,Wang2017tro,Sabouni2024auto,bandyopadhyay2023hjb}. This enables more flexible and generalizable safety guarantees in unknown environments. 
 The evolution of the approximated Lagrange multipliers $\hat{\lambda}_i,\ i=1,2,3,4$ are depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (c). A nonzero value of $\hat{\lambda}_2$ for agent 2 occurs in a small time interval, indicating temporary activation of the graph CBF constraint in response to potential collisions. The Lagrange multipliers for the remaining agents stay at zero, reflecting inactive graph CBF constraints. These observations validate the effectiveness of the state-dependent Lagrange multipliers in dynamically balancing safety and goal-reaching performance. Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (d) shows the distributed control input of agent 2, which converges to zero as the agent reaches its goal position.
% We conduct a simulation in four agents and four obstacles case in a $2{\rm m}\times1.5{\rm m}$ region to present that all agents can achieve safe goal-reaching objective using the proposed IHO-GL approach, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (a). Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (b) illustrates the learned GCBF contour for agent 2 at time $t=0.6{\rm s}$. It is observed that agent 4 is outside the sensing range of agent 2 and and thus has no influence on the CBF value of agent 2, demonstrating that the distributed CBF only depends on the states of neighbors. Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (c) shows the evolution of the approximated Lagrange multipliers $\hat{\lambda}_i,\ i=1,2,3,4$ of all four agents. Notably, agent 2 exhibits a nonzero $\hat{\lambda}_2$ during a short time interval, which reflects a temporary activation of the distributed CBF constraint due to a potential collision, This makes the learned distributed controller to prioritize safety over goal-reaching. The multipliers for the other agents always remain zero, indicating that their safety constraints are inactive. Thus, the effectiveness of the state-dependent Lagrange multiplier is illustrated in achieving dynamic adjustment between safety and goal-reaching performance. The distributed control inputs of agent 2 are plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig.3} (d), which illustrates that the control inputs converge to zero when agent 2 reaches its goal position. 


