\section{Hardware Experiments}\label{Section6}
This section validates the practical applicability of our HJB-GNN approach through four sets of hardware experiments on antipodal position-swapping tasks shown in Fig.~\ref{fig.8}, using a swarm of Crazyflie 2.1 platforms\footnote{https://www.bitcraze.io/products/old-products/crazyflie-2-1/}.



\subsection{Experiments Setup}
The control architecture for the Crazyflie swarm used in this paper follows an offboard-onboard split, considering the limited computation resources of Crazyflies.
% , as illustrated in Fig.[]. 
The distributed safe controller is running offboard using a PC that is connected to the Crazyflies using Crazyradio PA\footnote{https://www.bitcraze.io/products/crazyradio-pa/}. We use the Crazyswarm package\footnote{https://crazyswarm.readthedocs.io/en/latest/} to communicate with Crazyflies based on ROS1, which allows for sending full state control commands to the Crazyflies. 
% The overall control structure can be divided into the following four components: \emph{i) Task setup:} For all experiments, each Crazyflie drone needs to complete antipodal position-swapping task, where all Crazyflie drones are uniformly initialized on a circle, and the goal position of each agent is set as the diametrically opposite to its initial position. \emph{ii) Offboard computation:} 
% The learned safe distributed controller from the proposed IHO-GL approach computes the control input based on the current state and relative observations of neighboring agents and obstacles. 

Based on our HJB-GNN approach, the distributed safe controller is trained using a double integrator model, inspired by \cite{zhang2025tro}, to generate the desired accelerations. Similar to the experimental setup in \cite{zhang2025tro}, we utilize the \texttt{cmd\_full\_state} interface provided by Crazyswarm. The difference is due to the significant latency in Crazyflie’s onboard state estimation provided by Crazyswarm, we directly employ position and velocity measurements obtained from the OptiTrack system. 
To compute the \texttt{cmd\_full\_state} control command, we simulate the double integrator model used during training to predict the future positions and velocities resulting from the application of the desired accelerations after a fixed duration $\Delta t$. In all hardware experiments presented in this paper, we set $\Delta t = 50~\mathrm{ms}$.

%For tracking desired accelerations in \emph{crazyswarm1}, it is needed to compute desired positions and velocities setpoints via forward simulation of the double integrator dynamic using these accelerations over a short time horizon $\Delta t = 50{\rm ms}$. \emph{iii) Communication and feedback:} The goal positions and full-state feedback (positions and velocities) are obtained using an OptiTrack motion capture system. All state estimation and control computation occur at 50 Hz on an laptop, which communicates with the drones using two Crazyradios and the ROS1-based Crazyswarm1 interface. \emph{iv) Onboard execution:} The Crazyflie drones receive the desired full-state setpoints via the \emph{$cmd\_full\_state$} interface in \emph{crazyswarm1} and track them using their cascaded PID controllers. 

% The architecture above achieves real-time deployment of the safe distributed controller in a closed-loop hardware setup with full-state feedback and offboard planning, while utilizing the existing onboard flight stabilization stack.

