A \tsedef{closed semiwalk} is a semiwalk  where the first and last nodes are the same. A \tsedef{semicycle} is a closed semiwalk where all the nodes in the semiwalk are distinct except for the first and last nodes which are equal to each other but to no other node in the semicycle. Again, not all semicycles are cycles but cycles are always a special type of semicycle. For example $(1,3,2,1)$ in \figref{f:symmnetex}.(i) is a cycle and therefore a semicycle but in the opposite order, $(1,2,3,1)$, is a semicycle but not a cycle in \figref{f:symmnetex}.(i).
Also, I place no restriction on the length of a semicycle so a trivial walk $(v)$ of length zero is a semicycle of length zero.

