\subsection{The representation of group graphs}

Groups are normally encountered as a representation so it makes sense to define a group graph in this context. 
%Again, I will only consider matrix representations.
%\begin{definition}[Representation of a Group Graph] \label{d:repgrpgrph}
%	A \tsedef{representation of a group graph} $D$ is is a representation of the group $G$ applied to the group elements for the links in a Group Graph $\Ggraph$ so each link $\link$ has a \tsedef{weight} $D(\linkmap(\link))$.
%\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[Representation of a Group Graph]
	A \tsedef{representation of a group graph} is a Group Graph $\Ggraph$ where the group elements for the links lie in one representation $D$ of the group where each link $\link$ is labelled by $D(g_\link)$.
\end{definition}
%%%Formally the representation $D$ of the group $\Gcal$ is a set of maps $D(g): \Rcal \to \Rcal$ on the representation space $\Rcal$ whose composition respects the group multiplication so that $D(g_1) \circ D(g_2) = D(g_1g_2)$ for all $g_1,g_2 \in \Gcal$. 

%%%Here I will focus on a linear representation where the link labels $D(g)$ are $d$-dimensional matrices $\Dmat(g)$ acting on a $d$-dimensional vector space $\Rcal$ (usually a real or complex vector space). 
%%% I will write $\Dmat(u,v) = \Dmat(g_{uv})$ for the matrices representing link labels $g_{uv}$ for an link $(u,v)$ from node $v$ to node $u$. 

I will only consider matrix representations so I have a $d$-by-$d$ matrix $\Dmat(\link) =D(\linkmap(\link))$ representing the group element $\linkmap(\link)$ for every link $\link$ in the group graph. I will use the notation $\Dmat(\link) \equiv \Dmat(g_\link) \equiv \Dmat(\linkmap(\link))$ to indicate the matrix $\Dmat$ used to represent the label of a link $\ell$, where $\{\Dmat(g) | g \in \Gcal\}$ is the matrix representation of the group\footnote{Formally I am mixing two maps in my notation. First a map from the set of links to a matrix, $D: \Lcal \to \Rcal \times \Rcal$ where $\link \mapsto \Dmat(\link)$. Then I have the usual notation for a matrix representation of a group where $D: \Gcal \to \Rcal \times \Rcal$ with $g_\link \mapsto \Dmat(g_\link) \equiv \Dmat(\linkmap(\link))$. The context should make it clear which map is being used.}.

