\subsubsection{Attribute Selection Strategies}
\label{sec:strategies}
\BG{Currently only strategies that utilize single attributes, we need to motivate / justify this.}
 % \textbf{The safety rules technique was developed in our previous paper on provenance-based data skipping\cite{ps2021}. In this work, we directly apply these safety rules to filter out candidate attributes. The safe provenance sketch attribute candidates are selected by the safety rules. Different attributes candidate can lead to varying selectivities, which influence the performance. By selecting the most appropriate strategy, we can more efficiently determine the optimal attribute.}
 % \BG{Cite the paper and refer to it for safety rules and explain that we treat the safety check as a black-box and apply it to filter candidate attributes before passing them to the strategies}

 Many strategies can be applied to choose the attribute for building a provenance sketch for a query.  Different strategies include random picking from all attributes candidates after per-filtering (\stratRANall), randomly selecting from query-related attributes (\stratRANrelall), randomly selecting a group-by attribute (\stratRANgb), randomly selecting a primary key attribute (\stratRANpk). randomly selecting one of the aggregation input attribute  (\stratRANagg). These strategies utilizing random choice pick an attribute  uniformly at random from a set of candidate attributes specific to the strategy. For example, for randomly selecting a primary key attribute for a relation with a primary key $\{a,b\}$ we would pick each attribute with 50\% probability. In addition to the strategies that pick random attributes, we also evaluate strategies that use our size estimation techniques to select on attribute from a list of candidate attributes.
The \stratCBOPTrel strategy picks the attribute from the set of all safe attributes that is estimated to yield the smallest provenance sketch. The \stratCBOPTgb strategy applies the same selection criterion, but only considers group-by attributes.\BG{Why this strategy and not other subsets of attributes?}% \textbf{Because the samples is captured based on the group by attribute, capturing provenance sketch on group by attribute is consistent with the sampling procedure. We want to do research on the performance of the \stratCBOPTgb to see the relationship of the \stratCBOPTgb  and \stratCBOPT.}
To evaluate how our strategies compare against the optimal attribute choice (leading to the provenance sketch of minimal size), we also present results for this strategies which is based on our cost model to determine the optimal sketch (\stratCBOPT). What's more, \stratOPT is the actual optimal attribute and \stratNoPS is the original query without provenance sketch.

