One central operation is the shift of a condition along an arrow.  The
name shift is taken from an analogous operation for nested application
conditions (see \cite{p:development-correct-gts}).

\begin{definition}[Shift of a Condition]
  \label{def:shift}
  \label{prop:shift}
  Given a fixed class of representative squares $\kappa$,
  the \emph{shift of a condition $\mathcal A$ along an arrow
  $c \colon \RO(\mathcal A) \to B$} is inductively defined as follows:
  \[
    \Big( \bigland_{i \in I} \forall f_i.\mathcal A_i
    \Big)_{\downarrow c} = \bigland_{i \in
        I}\bigland_{(\alpha,\beta) \in \kappa(f_i,c)}
    \forall \beta.({\mathcal A_i}_{\downarrow \alpha})
    %
    \mkern100mu\hfill% TODO: consider moving somewhere else
    \begin{tikzpicture}[baseline={(0,-0.3)}]
