\begin{example}
  Let \(\arena\) be the arena of \Cref{fig:arena:nondeterministic} and
  \(L = \{(t, \natnonneg), (y_1, \{1\}), (y_1, \{1, 2\}),\allowbreak
  (y_2, \{1, 2\})\}\).
  The pair \((y_1, \{1\})\) is dominated by \(L\), as \((y_1, \{1, 2\}) \in L\),
  \((y_1, \{1\}) \parOrder (y_1, \{1, 2\})\), and
  \((y_1, \{1\}) \neq (y_1, \{1, 2\})\).
  Moreover, any \((t, K)\) with \(K \subsetneq \natnonneg\) is dominated by
  \(L\).
  However, \((y_1, \{1, 2\})\) is not dominated, as the only element \((v, K)\)
  of \(L\) such that \((y_1, \{1, 2\}) \parOrder (v, K)\) is equal to
  \((y_1, \{1, 2\})\).
  Likewise, \((t, \natnonneg)\) and \((y_2, \{1, 2\})\) are not dominated.
  Thus,
  \begin{gather*}
    \Dom(L) = \{(t, K) \mid K \subsetneq \natnonneg\} \cup
    \{(y_1, \emptyset), (y_1, \{1\}), (y_1, \{2\}),
    (y_2, \emptyset), (y_2, \{1\}), (y_2, \{2\})\},
  \end{gather*}
  and \(\reduce{L} = L \setminus \Dom(L) =
  \{(t, \natnonneg), (y_1, \{1, 2\}), (y_2, \{1, 2\})\}\).

  \Cref{fig:latticeKA} represents the complete lattice
  \((\latticeKA,\subseteq)\) for arena \(\arena\).  An edge between
  two sets of the lattice indicates that the one above includes
  the one below, \eg, \(\{1\} \subseteq \natnonneg\setminus\{2\}\).
