  \(c\)-edges from \(v\) in \(\arena\), we get the same lattice.
  Indeed, the element \(\{1, 2\}\) can be obtained via
  \(\{1\} \cup \{2\}\) (which label some edges of \(\arena\)),
  yielding in turn \(\natnonneg \setminus \{1, 2\}\).
\end{example}

\subsection{Fixed-point characterization of the winning region}%
\label{subsec:symbolic-algo}
Similarly to the case of standard two-player turn-based reachability
games, one can express the winning region of Eve in the knowledge game
as the least fixed-point of an analogous of the contrained-predecessor
operator. As announced earlier, this operator may apply to any
$\arena$-coherent set $\latticeK \subseteq \subsets{\natnonneg}$
(possibly $\subsets{\natnonneg}$ itself). Since
\(\final = \{(\target, K) \mid K \subseteq \natnonneg\}\), the
fixed-point is initialized with \((\target, \natnonneg)\), as well as
all \((v, \emptyset)\), for every
\(v \in \vertices \setminus \{\target\}\) (here $(v,\emptyset)$
  represents that a priori
  Eve loses against any number of players from
$v$). The operator then reflects the edges in \(\kGame\) by
incorporating all pairs \((v, K)\) such that for some action $a$
enabled at $v$, \((v', K \cap \opponents{v, a, v'})\) is already known
to be winning for every successor vertex $v'$.
In the following, $\latticeK$ denotes an $\arena$-coherent set.

\begin{definition}\label{def:symbolic}\label{def:fixpoint}
  For $(v, a) \in \vertices \times \alphabet$, let
  \(\KPredK{v,a}{\,\cdot\,} \) be the operator on $\latticeK$
  defined as
  \[
    \KPredK{v,a}{S} = \{K \in \latticeK \mid \forall v' \in
    \vertices : (v', K \cap \opponents{v, a, v'}) \in S\}.
  \]
  Let also:
  \[
    \PredK{S} = \bigcup_{v\in \vertices} \bigcup_{a\in \En(v)}\{(v,K)
    \mid K \in \KPredK{v,a}{S}\} \enspace.
  \]
  Finally, the sets \({(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat} \subseteq \verticesEK\) are
  defined inductively:
  \begin{align*}
    \WK^0 &= \{(\target, \natnonneg)\} \cup
    \{(v, \emptyset) \mid v \in \vertices \setminus \{\target\}\}
    \\
    \forall i \geq 0 : \WK^{i+1} &= \WK^i \lub
    \PredK{\expandK{\WK^i}} \quad\quad
    \text{\textrm{{\small (computation made in
    lattice $(\verticesEK,\parOrder)$)}}}.
  \end{align*}
  When $\latticeK$ is the full lattice $\subsets{\natnonneg}$, we
  simply write $\KPred{\cdot,\cdot}{\cdot}$, $\Pred{\cdot}$ and
  ${(\W^i)}_{i \in \nat}$.
\end{definition}
The sets ${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ are well-defined as subsets of
$\verticesEK$. By definition of the join operation, at each iteration,
we only keep the maximal elements with respect to \(\parOrder\): that
is, each \(\WK^i\) is an antichain of \((\verticesEK, \parOrder)\).
Furthermore, we only consider actions \(a\) that are enabled at \(v\)
to ensure that there exists \(v'\) such that \(\opponents{v,a,v'}\) is
not empty. Interestingly, there is no need to construct \(\kGame\) to define the
iterations of \(\WK^i\).

Note that the sequence ${(\expandK{\WK^i})}_{i \in \nat}$ is
non-decreasing, thus the limit
$\lim_{i \to +\infty} \expandK{\WK^i} = \bigcup_{i \in \natnonneg}
\expandK{\WK^i} $ always exists. Nevertheless, it is unclear whether
$\lim_{i \to +\infty} \WK^i$ exists. If $\latticeK$ is finite, the
sequence ${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \natnonneg}$ does stabilize, we denote its
limit $\WK^\infty$, and observe that
$\expand{\WK^\infty} = \bigcup_{i \in \natnonneg} \expand{\WK^i}$.
Towards the end of this section, we will show that the sequence also
stabilizes when $\latticeK = \subsets{\natnonneg}$.

\begin{example}\label{ex:WK}
  Let \(\game = (\arena, t)\), with
  \(\arena\) the arena of \Cref{fig:arena:nondeterministic}.
  We illustrate the approach with the full lattice \(\partition{\natnonneg}\).
  The initial set is
  \[
    \W^0 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, \emptyset), (x_1, \emptyset), (x_2, \emptyset),
    (y_1, \emptyset), (y_2, \emptyset), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  Now, \(\KPred{v, a}{\expand{\W^0}}\) contains \(>2\), as
  \((t, >2) \in \expand{\W^0}\) and, for every \(v' \neq t\),
  \((v', \emptyset) \in \expand{\W^0}\).  Similarly,
  \(\KPred{v, a}{\expand{\W^0}}\) also contains \(>3, >4\), and so on.
  Recall that \(\W^0 \sqcup \Pred{\expand{\W^0}}\) only contains the
  non-dominated elements, \ie, only \(>2\) in this case.  Moreover,
  \(\KPred{y_1, b}{\expand{\W^0}} = \KPred{y_2, b}{\expand{\W^0}}\)
  contains \(\natnonneg\) and, thus, every subset of \(\natnonneg\).
  However,
  \(\KPred{x_1, b}{\expand{\W^0}} = \KPred{x_2, b}{\expand{\W^0}} =
  \emptyset\), as \((v, \emptyset), (y_1, \emptyset)\), and
  \((y_2, \emptyset)\) all belong to \(\W^0\).  Thus, we obtain
  \[
    \W^1 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, >2), (x_1, \emptyset), (x_2, \emptyset),
    (y_1, \natnonneg), (y_2, \natnonneg), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  Observe that, at each iteration, it is sufficient to consider only
  the predecessors of the vertices whose knowledge set changed, \ie,
  only \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) in this example.  Indeed, for any other
  vertex \(v'\) and action \(a'\),
  \(\KPred{v', a'}{\expand{\W^0}} = \KPred{v', a'}{\expand{\W^1}}\).
  Let us thus focus on \(x_1\), as the computations for \(x_2\) will
  be similar: \(\KPred{x_1, b}{\expand{\W^1}}\) does not contain
  \(\natnonneg\), as \((v, \neq 1) \notin \expand{\W^1}\).  However,
  \(\neq 2 \in \KPred{x_1, b}{\expand{\W^1}}\), as
  \((v, \neq 2 \cap \neq 1) = (v, > 2) \in \expand{\W^1}\) and
  \((y_1, \neq 2 \cap =1) = (y_1, =1) \in \expand{\W^1}\).  Hence,
  \[
    \W^2 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, >2), (x_1, \neq 2), (x_2, \neq 1),
    (y_1, \natnonneg), (y_2, \natnonneg), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  As only the knowledge sets of \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) are different, it suffices
  to compute \(\KPred{v, a}{\expand{\W^2}}\).
  Clearly, \(\natnonneg \notin \KPred{v, a}{\expand{\W^2}}\), as
  \((x_1, \natnonneg) \notin \expand{\W^2}\).
  Likewise, \(\leq 2, \neq 1, \neq 2, =1\), and \(=2\) do not belong to the set.
  However, \(> 2 \in \KPred{v, a}{\expand{\W^2}}\) (with the same arguments as
  above).
  Hence, \(\W^3 = \W^2\), that is, the sequence
  \({(\expand{\W^i})}_{i \in \natnonneg}\) stabilizes.

  To conclude this example, we highlight that every knowledge set that
  appears in some \(\W^i\) is an element of the $\arena$-coherent set
  \(\latticeKA\) (see \Cref{fig:latticeKA}).  Moreover, if we also
  compute the sequence \({(\WKA^i)}_{i \in \natnonneg}\), we observe
  that \(\W^0 = \WKA^0, \W^1 = \WKA^1\), and \(\W^2 = \WKA^2\) and,
  for every \(i > 2\), \(\W^{i} = \W^{i-1} = \WKA^{i-1} = \WKA^i\).
  This suggests that we can actually use the sequence
  \({(\WKA^i)}_{i \in \natnonneg}\) to compute the winning region of
  Eve in finitely many iterations, as we will prove in the remaining
  of the section.
\end{example}

Let us argue that when $\latticeK = \subsets{\natnonneg}$ the least
fixed-point of the operator in \Cref{def:fixpoint} corresponds to the
winning region for Eve in the knowledge game.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{fixpointcorrectness}\label{thm:symbolic:strategy:region}
  Let \((v, K) \in \verticesE\) such that \(K \neq \emptyset\).  Eve
  has a winning strategy from \((v, K)\) in \(\kGame\) if and only if
  there exists $i \in \natnonneg$ such that
  \((v, K) \in \expand{\W^i}\)
\end{restatable}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
  The proof follows the same line as the correctness proof of the
  fixed-point computation of the winning region in standard two-player
  turn-based reachability games. A crucial difference however, is that
  our operator restricts to antichains of maximal elements: this will
  be very useful to compactly store large winning regions.

  From every \((v, K) \in \expand{\W^i}\), one can construct a winning
  strategy by induction on the least index \(i\) such that
  \((v, K) \in \expand{\W^i}\). To do so, one selects an action such
  that any successor \((v', K')\) satisfies
  \((v', K') \in \expand{\W^{i-1}}\). Conversely, if Eve has a winning
  strategy from \((v, K)\), she can ensure to progress towards a
  target vertex.  Since any target vertex necessarily belongs to the
  winning region, we can show that \((v, K)\) is also in the winning
  region.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
  The correctness is stated in the full lattice
  $(\subsets{\natnonneg},\subseteq)$, and it is not straightforward to
  extend the proof to the sublattice $(\latticeK,\subseteq)$ for any
  $\arena$-coherent set $\latticeK$. Nevertheless, in the next
  subsections we will see that ${(\WKA^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ also
  characterizes the winning region.
\end{remark}

From Theorems~\ref{thm:knowledge:strategy} and
\ref{thm:symbolic:strategy:region}, we conclude that
\(\bigcup_{i \in \natnonneg} \expand{\W^i}\) characterizes the winning
region for Eve in the concurrent parameterized game \(\game\).

\begin{restatable}{corollary}{fixpointcorollary}
  Eve has a winning strategy from a vertex \(v_0\) in \(\game\) if and only if
  there exists $i \in \nat$ such that \((v_0, \natnonneg) \in
  \W^i\). Furthermore, if $\W^\infty$ is defined, then Eve has a
  winning strategy from \(v_0\) in \(\game\) if and only if
  \((v_0, \natnonneg) \in \W^\infty\).
\end{restatable}

\subsection{An alternative fixed-point approach}
The previous fixed-point characterization cannot be immediately turned
into an algorithm, because, even if the parameterized arena is finite,
the operator $\KPred{\cdot,\cdot}{\cdot}$ collects an a priori
unbounded number of sets $K$. Therefore, it is not obvious how to use
it to compute the winning region in the knowledge game. We thus
propose an alternative approach, with an operational viewpoint, to
compute \(\W^{i+1}\) from \(\W^i\).  Let \(v \in \vertices\) and
\(a \in \En(v)\).  Before providing the definition, we give the
intuition behind it:

\begin{itemize}
  \item
    Fix, for each \(v' \in V\), a knowledge set \(K_{v'}\) such that
    \((v', K_{v'}) \in \W^i\).
  \item We want to focus on the vertices \(v'\) that can be reached from
    \(v\) by taking an edge labeled with \(a\) and some constraint on
    the number of opponents.  Since the arena may be non-deterministic,
    there can be multiple such vertices.  Hence, we iterate over each
    alternative, by taking every possible subset \(V'\) of \(V\),
    representing the vertices that we want to reach.

  \item We first compute the intersection of every \(K_{v'}\), with
    \(v' \in V'\).  This yields a set of numbers of opponents for which
    we are sure to have a winning strategy from each \(v'\).  Indeed,
    for every \(v' \in V'\), \((v', K_{v'}) \in \W^i\).  As
    \(\bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'}\) is a subset of each \(K_{v'}\), we have
    \((v', \bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'}) \in \expand{\W^i}\).

  \item It remains to check that we can guarantee to \emph{only} reach
    vertices in \( V'\), while avoiding any vertex \(v''\) with
    \(v'' \notin V'\).  That is, we want to reach $\opponents{v,a,V'}$,
    as defined in~\eqref{nablaI} page~\pageref{nablaI}.

  \item Finally, we take the intersection of
    \(\bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'}\) and \(\opponents{v,a,V'}\), to obtain
    the number of opponents for which we know that we have a winning
    strategy from \(v\).
  \item We repeat this procedure for every possible combination of sets
    \((v', K_{v'}) \in \W^i\), by varying the witnesses
    ${(K_{v'})}_{v' \in V}$.
\end{itemize}

Formally:
\begin{definition}\label{def:symbolic-alt}
  For $(v, a) \in \vertices \times \alphabet$, let
  \(\KPredAlt{v,a}{\,\cdot\,} \) be the operator on families of
  subsets of $\natnonneg$ defined as
  \[
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{S} = \left\{ \bigcup_{V' \subseteq V}
      \opponents{v,a,V'} \cap \bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'} \ \middle\vert\
    \forall v' : (v', K_{v'}) \in S \right\}.
  \]

  Let also
  \[
    \PredAlt{S} = \bigcup_{v\in \vertices} \bigcup_{a\in
    \En(v)}\{(v,K) \mid K \in \KPredAlt{v,a}{S}\} \enspace.
  \]
  Finally, the sets \({(\WAlt^i)}_{i \in \nat} \subseteq \verticesE\)
  are defined inductively:
  \begin{align*}
    {\displaystyle \WAlt^0} &{\displaystyle = \{(\target, \natnonneg)\} \cup
    \{(v, \emptyset) \mid v \in \vertices \setminus \{\target\}\}}
    \\
    {\displaystyle \forall i \geq 0 : \WAlt^{i+1}} &{\displaystyle =
      \WAlt^i \lub
    \PredAlt{\WAlt^i}.}
  \end{align*}
\end{definition}

Interestingly, as soon as $S$ is a subset of $\verticesEK$, then
$\KPredAlt{v,a}{S}$ belongs to $\latticeK$, and $\Pred{S}$ is included
in $\verticesEK$. $\verticesEK$ is thus stable by the operator. Since
$\WAlt^0 \subseteq \verticesEK$, the sets $\WAlt^i$ all belong to
$\verticesEK$.  Assuming $\latticeK$ is finite, the fixed-point
computation of the sequence ${(\WAlt^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ will thus
converge in finitely many steps. In contrast with the previous
operator, we also observe a monotonicity property with respect to the
preorder.

\begin{lemma}
  \label{lemma:PredAlt}
  For every $(v,a) \in \vertices \times \alphabet$, for every two sets
  $S,S' \subseteq \verticesE$ such that $S \parOrdersim S'$,
  \[
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{S} \subseteq \KPredAlt{v,a}{S'}
    \quad\text{and}\quad \PredAlt{S} \parOrdersim \PredAlt{S'}.
  \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  Assume $S \parOrdersim S'$, and pick $K \in
  \KPredAlt{v,a}{S}$. There exist $(v',K_{v'}) \in S$ (for each \(v' \in V\))
  such that
  \[
    K = \bigcup_{V' \subseteq V} \opponents{v,a,V'} \cap
    \bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'}.
  \]
  For every $v' \in V'$, let $K'_{v'}$ be such that $(v',K'_{v'}) \in S'$ and
  $K_{v'} \subseteq K'_{v'}$, and define
  \[
    K' = \bigcup_{V' \subseteq V} \opponents{v,a,V'} \cap
    \bigcap_{v' \in V'} K'_{v'}.
  \]
  Then $K' \in \KPredAlt{v,a}{S'}$ and $K \subseteq K'$. Hence,
  $\KPredAlt{v,a}{S} \subseteq \KPredAlt{v,a}{S'}$.

  As an immediate consequence $\PredAlt{S} \parOrdersim \PredAlt{S'}$.
\end{proof}

As a consequence, we get the following corollary, relating the maximal
elements computed by $\KPredAlt{\cdot,\cdot}{\cdot}$ on a set or its
downward-closure.
\begin{corollary}\label{coro:PredAlt}
  For every $v,a$, for every set $S \subseteq \verticesEK$,
  \[
    \reduce{\KPredAlt{v,a}{S}} =
    \reduce{\KPredAlt{v,a}{\expandK{S}}}.
  \]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
  Since $S \parOrdersim \expandK{S}$ and $\expandK{S} \parOrdersim S$,
  by \Cref{lemma:PredAlt},
  \[
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{S} \subseteq
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{\expandK{S}},\;\;\text{and}\;\;
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{\expandK{S}} \subseteq
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{S}.
  \]
  By \Cref{lemma:reduce}, this implies the expected result.
\end{proof}

The latter result justifies that when defining $\WAlt^{i+1}$, there is
no need to apply the downward-closure to $\WAlt^i$, in contrast to the
definition of the sequence ${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat}$.

\begin{example}\label{ex:WAlt}
  We illustrate the above alternative symbolic approach on the game
  \(\game = (\arena, t)\) with \(\arena\) the arena of
  \Cref{fig:arena:nondeterministic}. As in \Cref{ex:WK}, initially
  \[
    \WAlt^0 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, \emptyset), (x_1, \emptyset),
      (x_2, \emptyset)
    (y_1, \emptyset), (y_2, \emptyset), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  We focus on computing \(\KPredAlt{v, c}{\WAlt^0}\).  Given
  \(\WAlt^0\), the choice of \(K_{v'}\) is unique for each
  \(v' \in \vertices\).  Let us thus consider the different
  \(V' \subseteq \vertices\).  Clearly, if \(y_1 \in V'\),
  \(\opponents{v, c, V'} = \emptyset\), as
  \(\opponents{v, c, y_1} = \emptyset\).  Likewise,
  \(\opponents{v, c, V'} = \emptyset\) when \(y_2 \in V'\),
  \(v \in V'\), \(x_1 \in V'\), or \(x_2 \in V'\).  We then assume
  that these vertices are not in \(V'\), which leaves us \(s\) and
  \(t\).  When \(s \in V'\), we immediately conclude that
  \(\bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'} = \emptyset\).  It thus remains to
  treat \(V' = \{t\}\), which yields
  \(\opponents{v, c, t} \cap K_t = {>}2\).  Hence,
  \(\KPredAlt{v, c}{\WAlt^0} = \{\emptyset, >2\}\).  Analogously, one
  can compute
  \(\KPredAlt{y_1, b}{\WAlt^0} = \KPredAlt{y_2, b}{\WAlt^0} =
  \{\emptyset, \natnonneg\}\). Thus,
  \[
    \WAlt^1 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, >2), (x_1, \emptyset), (x_2, \emptyset),
    (y_1, \natnonneg), (y_2, \natnonneg), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  Using similar steps, we obtain that
  \(\KPredAlt{x_1, b}{\WAlt^1} = \{\emptyset, =1\}\) and,
  symmetrically, \(\KPredAlt{x_2, b}{\WAlt^1} = \{\emptyset, =2\}\).
  Thus,
  \[
    \WAlt^2 = \{(s, \emptyset), (v, >2), (x_1, =1), (x_2, =2),
    (y_1, \natnonneg), (y_2, \natnonneg), (t, \natnonneg)\}.
  \]
  To iterate another step, we compute
  \(\KPredAlt{v, a}{\WAlt^2}\), and focus on the sets of vertices
  \(\{x_1\}, \{x_2\}\), and \(\{x_1, x_2\}\), as any other set
  immediately yields \(\emptyset\), since there is no \(a\)-transition
  from \(v\) to any other vertex.  For \(V' = \{x_1\}\), we get that
  \(\opponents{v, a, V'} = \opponents{v, a, x_1} \setminus \bigcup_{v'
    \neq x_1} \opponents{v, a, v'} = \natnonneg \setminus \natnonneg =
  \emptyset\), due to the non-determinism.  The same holds for
  \(V' = \{x_2\}\).  It thus remains \(V' = \{x_1, x_2\}\).  This
  time, \(\opponents{v, a, V'} = \natnonneg\), but
  \(\bigcap_{v' \in V'} K_{v'} = (=1) \cap (=2) = \emptyset\).  Thus,
  \(\KPredAlt{v, a}{\WAlt^2} = \emptyset\) and \(\WAlt^3 = \WAlt^2\).

  Observe that, for every index \(i\), \(\WAlt^i = \W^i\) (with
  \(\W^i\) computed in \Cref{ex:WK}). This is not a coincidence, and
  we prove this general fact in the next section.
\end{example}

\subsection{Two fixed-point computations?}%
\label{subsec:twofixedpointcomputations}
Let $\latticeK \subseteq \subsets{\natnonneg}$ be an $\arena$-coherent
set. As we shall see, the two approaches presented in the previous
subsections compute the same sets. Precisely, the sequences
${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ --also when
$\latticeK = \subsets{\natnonneg}$-- and ${(\WAlt^i)}_{i \in \nat}$
coincide.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{fixpointequality}\label{thm:same-fixed-point}
  For all \(i \in \nat\), \(\W^i = \WAlt^i = \WK^i\). \\ The sequences
  ${(\W^i)}_{i \in \nat}$, ${(\WAlt^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ and
  ${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ stabilize, and
  \( \W^\infty = \WAlt^\infty = \WK^\infty \).
\end{restatable}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
  We give here the main ingredients of the proof, which can be found
  in details in Appendix.

  We first observe a strong relationship between the
  $\KPredK{\cdot,\cdot}{\cdot}$ and $\KPredAlt{\cdot,\cdot}{\cdot}$
  operators.  For every \(v \in \vertices\), \(a \in \En(v)\) and
  $S \subseteq \verticesEK$,
  \begin{equation}
    \label{eq:kpred-almost-equal}
    \KPredK{v, a}{S} \subseteq \KPredAlt{v, a}{S},\quad\text{and}\quad
    \KPredAlt{v,a}{S} \subseteq \KPredK{v,a}{\expandK{S}}.
  \end{equation}
  Notice that the second inclusion requires to take the
  $\latticeK$-downward closure of $S$. Consider for instance an
  edge $v \xrightarrow{a,[2,5]} t$ with the assumption that
  $(t,\natnonneg) \in S$. Then $K_t = \natnonneg$ and
  $\opponents{v,a,t} \cap K_t = [2,5]$, which is not in $S$. This
  explains is why the operator defining ${(\WK^i)}_{i \in \nat}$
  requires a $\latticeK$-downward closure.

  When instantiating Equation~\eqref{eq:kpred-almost-equal} to a
  $\latticeK$-downward-closed set $S \subseteq \verticesEK$, we
  derive that
  \[\forall S  \subseteq \verticesEK,\ \quad S = \expandK{S} \quad
    \Longrightarrow \quad
  \KPredK{v, a}{S} = \KPredAlt{v, a}{S}\] and therefore
  \(\PredK{S} = \PredAlt{S}\).

  Clearly enough $\WAlt^0 = \WK^0 = \W^0$.  Unfortunately, this
  set is not $\latticeK$-downward-closed: it contains
  $(t,\natnonneg)$ and none of its dominated elements. However,
  we still manage to prove by induction on $i$ that for every
  $i\in \nat$, $\WAlt^i = \WK^i = \W^i$. Indeed:
  \begin{align*}
    \WAlt^{i+1} &= \WAlt^i \lub \PredAlt{\WAlt^i} \\
    &= \WK^i \lub \PredAlt{\WAlt^i} \quad \text{by
    induction hypothesis}\\
    &= \WK^i \lub \PredAlt{\expandK{\WAlt^i}}  \\
    &=\WK^i \lub \PredK{\expandK{\WK^i}}\\
    &= \WK^{i+1}.
  \end{align*}

  Finally, to prove the convergence in finitely many iterations, we
  recall that $\latticeKA$ is finite, so that the sequence
  ${(\WKA^i)}_{i \in \nat}$ necessarily stabilizes. Since all above
  sequences coincide, they all stabilize and converge to the same
  fixed-point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
  The antichains $\W^\infty$, $\WAlt^\infty$ and $\WK^\infty$ describe
  the maximal elements of the winning region for Eve in $\kGame$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  According to Theorem~\ref{thm:same-fixed-point}, all sequences
  stabilize towards the antichain $\W^\infty$, which we know, by
  \Cref{thm:symbolic:strategy:region}, represents the maximal elements
  of the winning region of $\kGame$.
\end{proof}

\section{Experimental evaluation}\label{sec:experiments}

\paragraph*{The tool \textsf{ParaGraphs}}
We implemented our two new algorithms \(\WAlt\) and \(\WKA\) based on
antichains, and the original \PSPACE algorithm of~\cite{BertrandBM19}
in an open-source C++20 tool called
\textsf{ParaGraphs}\footnote{\url{https://gitlab.inria.fr/gstaquet/paragraphs}}.
It currently supports constraints given as finite unions of intervals,
but is designed in a modular way and permits to easily define new
types (for instance semi-linear sets), as well as specific
implementations of the algorithms for these types.

For the symbolic algorithms, the first step is to construct
\((\latticeKA, \subseteq)\).  In \textsf{ParaGraphs}, \(\latticeKA\)
is stored in an array, which permits to denote an element of the
lattice by its index (without interpreting its semantics). The
construction of the finite lattice is performed so as to maintain the
set of direct parents and the set of direct children of each
element. These relationships are used to efficiently compute meet and
join operations, as well as the effect of one iteration of the
operators.

\subparagraph{Implementation of \(\WKA\).}  Once the lattice is
computed, the \(\KPredKA{\cdot, \cdot}{\cdot}\) operator is
implemented by iterating over the elements of the lattice, starting
from \(\natnonneg\).  To compute \(\KPredKA{v, a}{S}\), if the current
element \(K\) is such that
\((v', K \cap \opponents{v, a, v'}) \notin S\), we then consider the
children of \(K\) in the lattice.  Otherwise, \(K\) is a maximal
element of the set to return, and it is useless to explore the subDAG
rooted at \(K\).  We thus remember which nodes to ignore, in order to
avoid useless computations. Finally, we highlight that there is no
need to compute \(\expandKA{\WKA^i}\) to obtain \(\WKA^{i+1}\).
Indeed, we are only interested in knowing whether
\((v', K \cap \opponents{v, a, v'}) \in S\), which can be performed
directly on the maximal elements of the antichain, thanks to the
lattice.

\subparagraph{Implementation of \(\WAlt\).}

Thanks to \Cref{thm:same-fixed-point}, the sets that appear in
\(\WAlt^i\) are exactly those of \(\WK^i\).  Thus, in order to to
minimize the amount of intersections, unions, and set differences to
be computed (as they can be expensive, in general), we exploit the
complete lattice \((\latticeKA,\subseteq)\) to perform these
operations. Interestingly, it also permits to not consider the
semantics of the constraints.

\subparagraph{Implementation of the original algorithm
from~\cite{BertrandBM19}.}

The original algorithm in~\cite{BertrandBM19} does not compute the
whole winning region, but determines whether Eve has a winning
strategy in \(\kGame\) from a given initial vertex
\((v_0, \natnonneg)\).  It performs a decomposition of the knowledge
game into sub-games, each of which is defined for a pair \((v, K)\)
and is a restriction of \(\kGame\) to vertices \((v', K, a)\) and
\((v', K')\) that are reachable from \((v, K)\) via vertices with the
same knowledge set \(K\) only.  That is, except in the \enquote{exit
vertices}, the knowledge set is not refined within a sub-game.
Interestingly, each sub-game can be solved in polynomial time in the
size of \(\game\). The algorithm tags each node of exponential-size
tree \(\tree\) of sub-games with \Win or \Lose in a DFS fashion. This
DFS exploration is implemented straightforwardly in
\textsf{ParaGraphs}, together with the resolution of each sub-game by
standard attractor computation.

\paragraph*{Experimental results}

The experiments we conducted were carried out on two types of
parameterized arenas: on the one hand, scalable games derived from an
illustrative example in~\cite{BertrandBM19}, and on the other hand
games obtained from quantified Boolean formulas (QBF). Benchmarks were
executed on a HP EliteBook 840 14 inch G10 Notebook PC, running Fedora
Linux 41.
\textsf{ParaGraphs} was compiled with g++ 14.2.1.

\subparagraph{Synthetic arenas} To exercise
our tool \textsf{ParaGraphs}, we considered four families of scalable
instances, with the objective to cover a wide range of
cases. Each family has a parametric size: the parameter
is $n$, and the arena size (precisely the number of vertices and of
different constraints) is linear in $n$; some are deterministic
(D), some non-deterministic (ND), some are winning for Eve (W), some
are losing for Eve (NW). They are given in \Cref{app:experiments}.

\Cref{tab:experiments:solve:games} compares the running time of the three
algorithms, with and
without the time needed to construct the lattice (for symbolic
algorithms). To be more specific, the instances with
\(n = 11\) (resp.\ \(n = 12\)) generate a lattice consisting of 4096
(8192) sets that is constructed in about 17s (85s). Computing larger
lattices (\ie, from 18384 sets) exceeds our timeout of five
minutes.  We highlight that the algorithm from~\cite{BertrandBM19}
solely determines whether Eve has a winning strategy, while our new
symbolic algorithms compute the whole winning region, thus returning a
more precise information.

\(\WAltK\) times out more often than \(\WK\) and the algorithm
from~\cite{BertrandBM19}.  This can be explained by the need to
compute many intersections, unions, and set differences.  These
operations may receive further optimizations in \textsf{ParaGraphs},
which would potentially improve the running time of \(\WAltK\) up to
those of \(\WK\).  Furthermore, \(\WK\) and the DFS-algorithm seem
incomparable. Perhaps surprisingly, the latter performs much better
when Eve wins. We leave as future work to better understand which
algorithm is more efficient on which kind of arenas.

