\subparagraph{Arenas obtained from quantified Boolean formulas} Our
tool \textsf{ParaGraphs} also handles quantified Boolean formulas
(QBF) and check their satisfiability by reduction to solving a
concurrent parameterized reachability game, as in the \PSPACE-hardness
proof~\cite[Prop.\ 13]{BertrandBM19}.

As in the general case, one first constructs the lattice (which here
only depends on the number of variables). Interestingly, given the
constraints in arenas generated from QBF instances, the lattice
construction can be tailored to be very efficient: a clever order on
insertions makes it possible to always avoid costly equality checks
(with elements already in the lattice). For formulas with 5 (resp.\ 6)
variables, the lattice, whose size is 2048 (resp.\ 8192), is obtained
in about 100ms (resp.\ 1.5s). Notably, our tailored construction
permits to build considerably larger lattices: for 8 variables, the
lattice contains 131072 sets and is obtained in slightly less than 5
minutes.

We exercised the implementation of the three algorithms on bigger
arenas than the previous ones by randomly generating one formula with
\(n\) variables and \(m\) clauses, for every \(n \in \{4,5,6,7,8\}\)
and \(m \in \{10,20,\dotsc,100\}\).

\Cref{fig:experiments:solve:qbf} gives the time taken by the three
methods on the formulas with 5 and 6 variables and with 50 to 100
clauses.  Interestingly, unlike in \Cref{tab:experiments:solve:games},
there are instances on which \(\WAltK\) outperforms both \(\WK\) and
the original algorithm from~\cite{BertrandBM19}.  Further experiments
could be undertaken to determine the feasibility of using
\textsf{ParaGraphs} on larger QBF instances, and the impact of the
numbers of variables and clauses.

