The first situation in which binary search (\bs{}) performs exceptionally can be seen in Figure~\ref{fig:rx:scaling}, where we show the cumulative lookup time of \bs{} in comparison with two other state-of-the-art indexes, namely an open-addressing hash table (\hash{})~\cite{lit:warpcore, lit:hash-warpcore-code} and a B+-Tree (\bp{})~\cite{lit:bptree, lit:bptree2} (after bulk-loading the nodes half-full and full) while varying the build set size.
While the performance of all indexes increases when the dataset becomes smaller, \bs{} benefits far more than the remaining indexes, even starting to outperform \bp{} and \hash{} for very small sizes in the range of the L2~cache ($6$MB on our NVIDIA RTX A6000). 
This nicely demonstrates the core advantage of binary search: With its minimal memory footprint, it can remain fully cache-resident longer than its competitors, which consume more space due to building auxiliary data structures (\bp{}) or having to over-allocate memory (\hash{}). Further, due to its simplicity, its code complexity is very low.   

The second situation in which binary search outperforms its competitors can be seen in Figure~\ref{fig:rx:sorted_probes}, where we show the impact of sorting the lookups by key before performing them. Again, while all indexes benefit from the optimal access locality caused by ordered lookups, \bs{} benefits most because of the small number of memory accesses required during a lookup combined with high memory locality, making it significantly faster than all competitors. 

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Contributions and Structure of the Paper}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

Obviously, in general, we cannot assume that our key set fits in cache or that our lookups arrive in an ordered fashion --- however, we can learn from these observations what is important for binary search and bring it closer to this ideal situation by applying a set of lightweight optimizations. 

To do so, we \textbf{(1)}~first tune the naive variant in terms of its \textbf{scheduling behavior} to identify the ideal starting point. Then, we \textbf{(2)}~integrate two variants of \textbf{cache pinning} to keep the initial steps of the binary search in L1~cache at all times. Next, we (3)~perform both \textbf{local lookup reordering} and \textbf{local result reordering} to further improve the locality of memory accesses while keeping the overhead of ordering low. Our results show that the fully optimized variant of \bs{} becomes a practical alternative to \bp{} while having a minimal memory footprint and fast build times. Additionally, \textbf{(4)}~we generalize binary search to \textbf{$K$-ary search}, which further improves upon binary search such that \bp{} is consistently outperformed. 

In Section~\ref{sec:setup}, we start by discussing the hardware architecture and the experimental setup. Then, in Section~\ref{sec:naive}, we present the naive variant of \bs{} for which in Section~\ref{sec:bs}, we present the three optimizations. In Section~\ref{sec:ks}, we present $K$-ary search. Finally, we conclude in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}.  

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\section{Hardware Architecture and Setup}
\label{sec:setup}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

We conduct all following experiments on an NVIDIA RTX A6000 (Ampere) GPU, a high-end workstation GPU.
We will later refer to details of the hardware architecture, which is visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}.
Processing on the A6000 is distributed across 84 \textit{streaming multiprocessors} (SMs) containing 4 vector units of 32 cores each.
Typically, one unit of work is assigned to one thread.
When running a task, the GPU distributes small batches of threads (called \textit{thread blocks}) among all SMs, which then schedule even smaller batches of 32 threads (called \textit{warps}) onto the 32-core vector units.
Each vector unit has a shared register file, allowing warps to efficiently synchronize and exchange data (called \textit{warp shuffling}).
Similarly, each SM has its own shared L1 cache (128~KB), which can be partially (up to 100~KB) reconfigured to serve as fast user-programmable scratch memory, allowing for fast data movement and storage within a thread block.
To ensure scalability, NVIDIA restricts direct communication across SMs, only allowing indirect communication though the GPU's globally-accessible main memory.
Similar to block storage devices, loading larger contiguous chunks (called \textit{coalescing}) from main memory is more efficient than small random accesses.
Consequently, memory throughput is maximized if neighboring threads in a warp access neighboring memory addresses.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, page=1, trim={0 11.3cm 14.5cm 0}, clip]{figures/caching-sorting.pdf}
  \caption{Architecture of the NVIDIA RTX A6000.}
  \label{fig:architecture}
  \vspace*{-0.4cm}
\end{figure}

In the experiments, unless specified otherwise, all data structures are built from a key set of $2^{26}$ unique and uniformly-drawn 32-bit unsigned integers (as \bp{} only supports 32-bit keys).
A subsequent lookup phase simultaneously probes $2^{27}$ uniformly-drawn keys from the build set in random order.
We assume all data to be GPU-resident and therefore do not include memory allocations or CPU-GPU transfers in our measurements.
Further, we only consider workloads where the key set does not change during execution, i.e., no insertions or deletions take place.
We compile our implementation using version 12.8 of the CUDA toolchain.

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\section{Starting Point: Offset-based Binary Search}
\label{sec:naive}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

As our starting point, we consider a particularly simple variant of binary search referred to as \bs{} that traverses a sorted input array of size $n$ from the last entry to the first entry using incrementally smaller steps.
Initially, the step width is chosen as the largest power of two that is not greater than $n$ (\smalltt{LPOW2(n)}), which can be determined efficiently using a count-leading-zeros instruction.
Given a key $k$ to search for, we can perform a step safely if the entry at the stepped-onto position is not smaller than $k$.
When the search terminates, \smalltt{offset} will point to the first entry not smaller than $k$.
Since the step width is halved in each iteration, we only require $\log_2(n)$ many array accesses to locate $k$.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=pseudo, caption={Pseudo-code of naive binary search kernel.}, label={listing:binary_search_kernel}, xleftmargin=4.4ex, escapechar=|]
def bs(buffer, offset, step):
  while step > 0:
    if step <= offset:
      if sorted_keys[offset - step] >= lookup:
        offset -= step
    step >>= 1
  return offset

kernel naive(sorted_keys, lookups, results):
  lookup = lookups[TID]
  offset = len(sorted_keys) - 1
  step = LPOW2(len(sorted_keys))
  results[TID] = bs(sorted_keys, offset, step)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Listing~\ref{listing:binary_search_kernel} shows the pseudo-code for our GPU implementation of this variant: 
Each thread (identified by an integer \smalltt{TID}) loads a single key for lookup, then performs the binary search, and then stores the result in a separate buffer.
Figure~\ref{fig:naive_binary_search_search_path} visualizes the possible search paths for an example dataset with $n = 14$ entries. The number below each entry corresponds to the number of search steps after which the element is first visited.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=.95\linewidth, page=3, trim={0 16cm 18.5cm 0}, clip]{figures/caching-sorting.pdf}
  \caption{Search paths of the naive binary search.}
  \label{fig:naive_binary_search_search_path}
  \vspace*{-0.2cm}
\end{figure}

In Figure~\ref{fig:rx:scaling}, we compared this exact implementation of binary search in a sorted array (\bs{}) to \hash{} and \bp{}.
As we have mentioned, when scaling the number of inserted keys from $2^{15}$ to $2^{28}$, \bs{} initially outperforms \hash{} and \bp{} for smaller build set sizes, but unfortunately quickly falls behind.
We suspect memory access patterns to be the limiting factor:
While \hash{} and \bp{} are carefully engineered to maximize the number of coalesced accesses during traversal, for \bs{}, two neighboring threads are very likely to take different paths through the sorted array after the first few steps, leading to simultaneous, but noncontiguous memory accesses.
These noncontiguous accesses do not impact performance as long as the sorted array is small enough to be entirely cache-resident.
However, starting at size $2^{21}$ (which exceeds the $6$~MB L2~cache), entries must be swapped between cache and main memory frequently, leading to a severe performance degradation.

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\section{Pushing Binary Search to the Limits}
\label{sec:bs}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

To counteract this slowdown, in the following, we propose three different optimizations for binary search to push it to its limits. Let's see how close we can get to the full-fledged index structures.  

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Tuning the Scheduling}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

Interestingly, one can already achieve reasonable speedups by
