The colored horizontal lines correspond to the baseline indexes of Figure~\ref{fig:rx}.
For dynamic scheduling, varying the 
number of threads per block has little impact, as the scheduler can assign multiple thread blocks to the same SM if it has sufficient hardware resources available. So even if we choose a small number of threads per block, we still cannot effectively limit the amount of concurrently scheduled threads.
In contrast, static scheduling ensures that the exact amount of specified threads per block are active on an SM. The experiment reveals that this results in the best performance when using 256 threads per block. 
We suspect that a large amount of threads results in a large amount of concurrent random accesses, and therefore, heavy cache thrashing.
As a consequence, we will always use a static schedule for binary search, but still consider multiple different threads per block configurations in the following.

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Cache Pinning}
\label{sec:cache_pinning}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

Across all possible search paths, the first few steps of the binary search always access the same few entries and hence should ideally remain cached all the time. Unfortunately, by default, these entries may get evicted and replaced by entries down the search path due to the limited space in the cache. While for CPUs, there is nothing we could do about this as the L1 cache is automatically managed, for GPUs, it is possible to actively pin entries in the cache by storing them explicitly in the SM-resident scratch memory, which is carved out of the L1 cache.  
Consequently, we can exempt top-level entries from being repeatedly flushed during search.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  %
  \begin{subfigure}[b]{.9\linewidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, page=6, trim={0 16.1cm 18.7cm 0}, clip]{figures/caching-sorting.pdf}
    \caption{Naive \bs{}.}
    \label{fig:caching:off}
  \end{subfigure}
  %
  \begin{subfigure}[b]{.9\linewidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, page=6, trim={0 9.4cm 18.7cm 4.3cm}, clip]{figures/caching-sorting.pdf}
    \caption{\bs{} (\pinningSteps{}). The upper buffer is in scratch memory.}
    \label{fig:caching:on}
  \end{subfigure}
  %
  \caption{Looking up key $6$ with and without cache pinning. Orange arrows denote steps not taken.}
\label{fig:caching}
\end{figure}

To realize this concept, in the first version called \bs{}~(\pinningSteps{}), we fill the scratch memory with all entries required for performing the first $M$ steps of the search\footnote{To simplify the algorithm, we also store the first entry of the search path (the largest element of the array) in the scratch memory, although it is not accessed.}, where we select the largest $M$ so that the amount of extracted entries does not exceed the available shared memory capacity. Conveniently, the subset of entries traversed in the first $M$ steps of the search is located at positions $n - 1 - \tfrac{2i \cdot{} S}{2^{M}}$ for $i = 0, 1, \ldots{}$ (where $S$ is the initial step size) and is therefore easy to locate and extract. 
As an example, consider Figure~\ref{fig:caching}, which shows the search for key~$6$ with and without cache pinning. Without cache pinning (Figure~\ref{fig:caching:off}), all four steps of the search are carried out in global memory. With cache pinning (Figure~\ref{fig:caching:on}) and a hypothetical 6-slot scratch memory region, we can carry out the first $M=2$ steps required to find the smallest entry greater than or equal to $6$ entirely in scratch memory. 
Only after that, the search falls back to global memory for the last two steps. 


Note that in our example, two slots of the scratch memory were left unused as the next step does not entirely fit in anymore. In our second version called \bs{}~(\pinningFull{}), we make use of this unused space by partially caching the entries that would be accessed during step $M+1$ of the binary search.
%The resulting buffer is visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:caching_partial}, consisting of all entries for the first $M = 2$ steps and the two rightmost entries belonging to step $M+1 = 3$ (green color).
The corresponding changes to the search algorithm are simple:
During step $M+1$, we check (via index comparison) if the required entry is present in scratch memory, and load it from there if possible. Otherwise, we retrieve it from global memory. 

