The results indicate that both the number of threads per block, as well as the number of registers used for sorting, has an impact not only on the overall performance, but also on the efficacy of the proposed optimizations.
As expected, local reordering performs best when the total amount of allocated registers in a block is high, but also seems to prefer lower thread counts.
Interestingly, the 128/8 configuration achieves the same performance as \bp{} with half-full nodes.

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Putting It All Together}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

Listing~\ref{listing:optimized_bs} shows the pseudo-code of all optimizations materialized in a single kernel. 
The code is run by each thread individually, which is identified via its block-local thread ID (\smalltt{TID}) and its block ID (\smalltt{BID}).
Each thread takes part in populating the scratch memory, then pre-fetches some of the lookup keys such that they can be sorted across the block. Afterwards, for each reordered lookup key, the thread performs a search in scratch memory, continuing in global memory once we reach the capacity limit. Finally, results are re-shuffled into their original locations and written to the output buffer.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=pseudo, caption={Pseudo-code of \bs{} (all optimizations).}, label={listing:optimized_bs}, xleftmargin=4.4ex, escapechar=|]
kernel opt(cache_step_size, sorted_keys, lookups, results):
  // extract first steps of the binary search
  extract_into_scratch(cached_keys, cached_partial_keys)

  // static scheduling: loop over chunks of lookups
  lookup_stride = NBLOCK * NBTHREAD * NREG
  lookup_offset = BID * NBTHREAD * NREG
  while lookup_offset < len(lookups):
    // pre-fetch lookups into registers
    for 0 <= reg < NREG:
      l[reg] = lookups[lookup_offset + NBTHREAD * reg + TID]
    // sort lookups locally
    block_sort(l)

    for 0 <= reg < NREG:
      lookup = l[reg]
      offset = len(cached_keys) - 1
      step = LPOW2(len(cached_keys))
      // binary search in scratch memory
      offset = bs(cached_keys, offset, step)
      rev_offset = len(cached_keys) - 1 - offset
      // check if next entry is cached
      if rev_offset < len(cached_partial_keys):
        revrev = len(cached_partial_keys) - 1 - rev_offset
        if cached_partial_keys[revrev] >= lookup:
          offset -= step
        step >>= 1
      // compute global offset
      scaled = rev_offset * cache_step_size
      offset = len(sorted_keys) - 1 - scaled
      step = cache_step_size >> 1
      // binary search in global memory
      r[reg] = bs(cached_keys, offset, step)

    // apply reverse permutation to results
    block_unsort(r)
    // write all results
    for 0 <= reg < NREG:
      results[lookup_offset + NBTHREAD * reg + TID] = r[reg]  

  lookup_offset += lookup_stride
\end{lstlisting}

