\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\section{From Binary Search to $K$-ary Search}
\label{sec:ks}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

Since the amount of available scratch memory is quite limited and can only hold a few steps of the binary search, for large arrays, there will still be a significant amount of accesses to global memory which penalize the performance. 
Conveniently, there is one opportunity for optimization that we have not considered yet:
Currently, each lookup is assigned to exactly one thread.
But if we distribute a single lookup over multiple adjacent threads, we have greater control over access locality.

To generalize binary search to multiple threads, we introduce $K$-ary search, inspired by the good old cache-sensitive search trees~\cite{lit:css}:
Abstractly speaking, $K$-ary search recursively selects $K-1$ "separator" entries at evenly spaced positions, thus partitioning the array into $K$ chunks, and decides which chunk to search next based on how the lookup key compares to the separators, similar to how a B-Tree decides which child node to visit next. This comparison step can be performed by $K-1$ threads in parallel, after which all threads communicate their results to figure out which partition to search next. While the communication step can be done efficiently (for small $K$) using warp shuffles, however, loading the separators would require $K-1$ random accesses due to their relative spacing. We can circumvent this issue by pre-fetching all possible separators that can occur during traversal and then densely storing them in an auxiliary buffer in global memory, similar to the scratch memory caching in Section~\ref{sec:cache_pinning}.
The result is a tree-like structure, where the leaf layer is the initial sorted array.
By building the tree bottom-up with fixed chunk sizes, we do not need to store child pointers, resulting in minimal memory overhead.

Figure~\ref{fig:k-ary-search} shows how it works for $K=3$, where $7$ and $14$ are chosen as the $K-1=2$ separators for the first step, and $3$/$6$ or $10$/$12$ are materialized as the separators for the second step. To perform a lookup of key~$13$, two threads are now able to concurrently compare the separators $7$ and $14$ with the search key $13$. As $14$ is greater to $13$ while $7$ is not, we continue the search in the middle (second) chunk, for which we find the separators $10$ and $12$. Both compare less than the search key $13$, so the rightmost (third) chunk will be checked next. This final chunk is part of the sorted array, and can be searched concurrently to locate key~$13$ at position~$10$.

