\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Applying Optimizations to $K$-ary Search}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

For our evaluation, we test whether performance improves by applying the previously discussed optimizations, namely \pinningSteps{}, \pinningFull{}, \lookupReordering{}, and \resultReordering{}, to \ks{}.
Figure~\ref{fig:exp:optimizing_ks} mirrors the experiment from Figure~\ref{fig:exp:reordering}, where we compare the lookup times of various combinations of optimizations and thread configurations.
We pre-tested multiple choices of $K$ and found $K=17$ to perform best across all configurations.
We also re-ran the comparison between static and dynamic scheduling and found that \ks{} always runs faster when using dynamic scheduling.
We therefore only show results for $K=17$ and dynamic scheduling for space reasons.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  \vspace*{-0.2cm}
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, trim={0 0.4cm 0 0}, clip]{plots/comparison_static_tree.pdf}
  \caption{Impact of \bs{} optimizations on \ks{}.}
  \label{fig:exp:optimizing_ks}
  \vspace*{-0.2cm}
\end{figure}

The results imply that 512 threads per block and two lookup registers per thread is the best parameter configuration, and that none of the optimizations manage to reduce lookup times further.

Note that this does not contradict our previous findings:
We proposed \lookupReordering{}/\resultReordering{} to increase the chance of coalesced memory accesses.
But $K$-ary search always accesses the densely packed separator entries, so each memory access is already coalesced.
Similarly, we proposed \pinningSteps{}/\pinningFull{} because we suspected the frequently-accessed entries that we access at the beginning of the search to be evicted during later steps.
However, $K$-ary search requires fewer traversal steps, and the packed separator entries likely share a cache line, immensely increasing cache efficiency, so the positive impact of explicit pinning probably becomes negligible.

However, even without optimizations, \ks{} outperforms both variants of \bp{} by a considerable margin.

