Our optimized version of \bs{} outperforms the naive \bs{} while retaining the same memory footprint, and also performs better than \bp{} up to around $2^{25}$ inserted keys.
\ks{} has a slightly longer build time and slightly higher memory footprint, but consistently yields the lowest lookup times among all order-based index structures, only being only surpassed by \hash{}, which, in turn, requires more time and space to build.

\vspace*{-0.2cm}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace*{-0.1cm}

In this work, we pushed binary search to its limits by tuning its scheduling behavior, applying cache pinning, and locally reordering lookups and results. By this, binary search becomes a viable alternative to \bp{} while offering a lower memory footprint and build time. Our $K$-ary search was even outperforming \bp{} in all cases (up to a factor of $2.7\times$), while having a negligible memory overhead over \bs{} (around 3.1\%).  

\textbf{Outlook:} In case of acceptance, we will extend the paper by discussing additional profiling results. Further, we will include results for different GPU architectures. Further, we will discuss the cost of performing batch-wise updates. 


