\section{Introduction}

% The shift from passive Large Language Models (LLMs) to agentic AI systems, which are autonomous agents capable of perceiving, deciding, and acting, introduces novel challenges for HIPAA compliance. Modern healthcare workflows demand AI agents that can dynamically interpret policies (for example, minimum necessary access), collaborate with human clinicians, and self-adapt to evolving threats. However, existing HIPAA frameworks rely on static access controls that are inadequately designed for agentic environments characterized by several critical considerations: first, autonomy requires real-time policy adjustments such as emergency access overrides; second, multi-agent coordination necessitates complex consensus protocols for assessing protected health information disclosure risks; and third, the unpredictable outputs generated by large language models mandate proactive strategies for containing potential protected health information leaks.



Agentic AI system is an emerging paradigm in Artificial Intelligence (AI) where autonomous systems pursue complex goals with minimal human intervention \cite{shavit2023practices}. The integration of these systems powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) into healthcare workflows represents a paradigm shift in clinical decision making and administrative efficiency \cite{sudarshan2024agentic}. Unlike passive LLMs, Agentic AI systems can dynamically interact with Electronic Health Records (EHRs), synthesize multimodal patient data, and execute context-aware tasks such as generating clinical documentation, summarizing medical literature, or offering real-time diagnostic recommendations \cite{sudarshan2024agentic,10.1145/3709365,10825266}. 

%The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into healthcare systems offers transformative potential to improve patient care, clinical workflows, and administrative processes. These AI systems can help generate clinical documentation, generate radiology reports, summarize medical literature, provide personalized healthcare recommendations, and support clinical decision-making \cite{10.1145/3709365,10825266}. 

