      \label{fig:pf_vs_ls}
      \vspace{-1em}
\end{figure}


\subsection{TransfMAPPO}

The complexity of training multi-agent system with RL typically scales with the number of agents involved. To address this, we introduce TransfMAPPO, inspired by TransfQMix \cite{transfqmix}, which maintains the core concepts of the latter but uses PPO as the RL backbone instead of DQN, since PPO is more natural for vectorized environments. In TransfMAPPO, we implement a Transformer Actor and Critic that process the features describing the observed entities as vertices of a coordination latent graph learned through self-attention. The resulting policy and Value function are therefore invariant from the number of entities observed, allowing curriculum learning procedure described in \autoref{sec:curr_learning}. More formally, the Transformer Critic observes the state of the environment represented as $n$ entities each one described by $z$ features, which in our case are the position, velocity and angles of the agents and targets. The Transformer Agent also observes the $n$ entities, but from its own partial observability perspective, which is constraint by the acoustic communication protocols defined in \autoref{sec:method}. An overview of TransfMAPPO can be seen in \autoref{fig:transfmappo}. Following \cite{gallici2024simplifyingdeeptemporaldifference}, we found that normalizing the entire network with LayerNormalization greatly improves stability and performances.


