Moreover, according to the Efficient Market Hypothesis \cite{efficient_market}, prices absorb information with a lag, which provides a theoretical basis for exploiting multi-source signals not yet fully reflected in stock prices to predict future movements. Consequently, robust and reliable predictive models must assimilate heterogeneous data to capture the full complexity of price dynamics \cite{chai2020multi, fu2024incorporating}.

% Time series regression models have long served as the cornerstone for valuation and forecasting tasks in finance. In pure time-series prediction, traditional statistical approaches \cite{weisberg2005applied, ariyo2014stock, bauwens2006multivariate} focus exclusively on specific numerical features, overlooking the open-ended knowledge embedded in human society—namely, information drawn from multiple modalities \cite{cheng2024sociodojo}. However, since the financial markets are, by nature, constructed upon diverse information sources, models that integrate multimodal inputs often outperform their single-modality counterparts. For example, in equity investment decision-making, investors not only analyze historical stock-price time series but also synthesize a variety of real-time data modalities to guide buy, sell, or hold strategies. These modalities include structured financial tables, unstructured textual data (e.g., financial news), and visual artifacts (such as technical analysis charts). Structured tables provide key financial indicators for assessing a firm’s fundamentals; financial news reflects shifts in market sentiment via sentiment analysis; and technical charts reveal long-term price trends through quantitative measures like moving averages. 

% The efficient market hypothesis~\cite{efficient_market} posits that stock prices incorporate market information with a certain lag, thereby providing a theoretical basis for forecasting future price movements using multi-source signals that have not yet been fully absorbed by the market. Consequently, building more robust and reliable forecasting models demands the integration of heterogeneous multimodal data to achieve a more comprehensive understanding and prediction of price dynamics.

Recently, the natural language processing~(NLP) models enable sentiment analysis of financial news, event extraction from disclosures, table parsing in earnings reports, and automated chart summarization \cite{souma2019enhanced, araci2019finbert, yang2018dcfee, chapman2022towards, la2020end}.
Despite rapid advances in NLP models, existing multimodal datasets remain constrained. Most integrate only price and sentiment within a single market, risking information loss (Table \ref{table:dataset_comparison} ); recent efforts \cite{koval2024financial} incorporate quarterly tables but suffer from limited temporal coverage and low update frequency. Such datasets are too small to train large models or to validate generalization across market regimes \cite{liu2020finrl, mik2017smart, liu2023fingpt}, and they exacerbate large language models’ propensity for hallucinations in rapidly evolving financial environments \cite{gupta2023gpt, yang2023investlm}. 

To address these limitations, we introduce FinMultiTime, a bilingual, large-scale dataset. FinMultiTime temporally aligns data from year $2009$ to $2025$ by four modalities, including text, tables, images, and time series. Our dataset includes $4213$ S\&P 500 constituents and $892$ HS 300 constituents. After rigorous cleaning and preprocessing, FinMultiTime comprises 112.6 GB of minute, daily and quarterly level data covering both U.S (Table \ref{Overview of Bilingual Financial Dataset}). and Chinese markets. Real-time updates ensure the dataset reflects the latest market conditions, providing a comprehensive foundation for developing and validating multimodal forecasting models. Experimental results demonstrate that incorporating large-scale multimodal data significantly reduces prediction error and improves trend-direction accuracy, with high-quality sentiment and long-term trend information proving especially critical.

