\subsection{Vector quantization}
The k-means problems aim to find $k$ clusters within data in $d$-dimensional space, so as to minimize the \eqref{eq:sse}.  However, solving this problem is NP-hard even $k$ is restricted to $2$ \citep{10.1023/B:MACH.0000033113.59016.96, 10.1145/1374376.1374452} or in the plane \citep{MAHAJAN201213}. Typically, the sub-optimal k-means problem can be solved by Lloyd’s algorithm \citep{1056489}. In the implementation of ABBA software\footnote{https://github.com/nla-group/ABBA}, the k-means algorithm is performed by \texttt{scikit-learn} library \citep{scikit-learn} which runs a few times (this is controlled by the parameter $\texttt{n\_init}$\footnote{The default to \texttt{scikit-learn} is 10 before the version 1.3.2.}) of Lylod's algorithm with $D^2$ seeding and pick up the best result. In the setting of this paper, we found setting  $\texttt{n\_init}$ to 1 is good enough for the ABBA performance.  

As already mentioned, Lloyd's algorithm is a widely used method to solve the k-means problem, it starts with uniformly sampling $k$ centers from data, often referred to as seeding, and then each point is allocated to a cluster with the closest center, and the mean centers of clusters are recomputed again. The procedure keeps repeating until the iteration converges.  

The seeding has a great impact on the final result. The improved algorithm is combined with optimal seeding ``$D^2$ weighting'' introduced by \cite{1283494}, which can significantly improve Lloyd's algorithm. Lloyd's algorithm with $D^2$ weighting is called the ``\texttt{k-means++}'' algorithm.  The k-means algorithm with  ``$D^2$ weighting'' shows $O(\log k)$-competitive with the optimal clustering. 

\begin{algorithm}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{$D^2$ weighting}\label{algo:dsampling}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \Statex \textbf{Input:} $P  = [p_i]_{i=1}^{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times N}$, $k$
    \State Initialize the first center $c_1$ uniformly arbitrarily from $P$ 
    \State Select the next center $c_i \in P (i \ge 2)$  with probability $\frac{D(p')^2}{\sum_{p \in P}D(p)^2}, p' \in P$ 
    \State Repeat Step~2, until a total of $k$ centers is chosen. 
    \State \textbf{Return:} $c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_k$
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% pseudocode
\begin{algorithm}[H]
\centering
\caption{\texttt{k-means++} algorithm}\label{algo:kpp}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} $P = [p_i]_{i=1}^{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times N}$, $k$
\State Use Algorithm~\ref{algo:dsampling} to select $k$ initial centers $c_1, \ldots, c_k$  from $P'$
\For{$c_i \in \{c_1, \ldots, c_k\}$} 
\State Compute $C_i$, the set of points in $P$ where $c_i$ is the closest center
\State Update $c_i$ by computing the mean center of cluster $C_i$, i.e., $c_i=\frac{1}{|C_i|}\sum_{p \in C_i}p$   
\EndFor
\State Repeat Steps~$2\sim 5$ until meet maximum iterations or clusters set converge
\State Assign $p_i$ to the closest center $c^i$ with a unique cluster label
\State \textbf{Return} Assigned points $p_1,p_2,\ldots,p_N$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

ABBA digitization using this clustering method has been shown incredibly slow speed, though the reconstruction error meets the needs of most applications.  It is very desired to design a faster clustering alternative while retaining the original reconstruction error to an ultimate degree. For this reason, we propose a sampling-based k-means clustering algorithm that can address the above concern. The idea is to perform \texttt{k-means++} on a uniform sample of data where only $r$ percent of original data is used.  The algorithm is as described in Algorithm~\ref{algo:samplekpp}. Section~\ref{sec:exp} will demonstrate its performance empirically. 


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\centering
\caption{Sampling-based \texttt{k-means} algorithm}\label{algo:samplekpp}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} $P = [p_i]_{i=1}^{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times N}$, $k$, $r$
\item Uniformly sample $\round{r \cdot |P|}$ points from $P$ as $P'$
\State Use Algorithm~\ref{algo:dsampling} to select $k$ initial centers $c_1, \ldots, c_k$ from $P'$
\For{$c_i \in \{c_1, \ldots, c_k\}$}
\State Compute $C_i$, the set of points in $P'$ where $c_i$ is the closest center
\State Update $c_i$ by computing the mean center of cluster $C_i$, i.e., $c_i=\frac{1}{|C_i|}\sum_{p \in C_i}p$   
\EndFor
\State Repeat Steps~$2\sim 5$ until iterations end or clusters set converge
\State Assign $p_i$ to the closest center $c^i$ with a unique cluster label
\State \textbf{Return} Assigned points $p_1,p_2,\ldots,p_N$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\figurename~\ref{fig:compkm} shows the simulation of \texttt{k-means++} and sampling-based k-means (sampling $r=10\%$ of data) on Gaussian blobs data with 10 clusters, proceeding by increasing data sizes. The result is as illustrated in \figurename~\ref{fig:compkm}. 
We can observe that sampling-based k-means runs in a fraction of the time compared to
\texttt{k-means++}, while giving competitive performance in terms of adjusted mutual information (AMI).

