The parallel speedup $m$ processors is given by
\begin{equation*}
     \Phi(m) = \frac{\upsilon(1)}{\upsilon(m)}
\end{equation*}
where $\upsilon(m)$ is referred to as the runtime of the $m$ processors. Without loss of generality, we only evaluate the speedup of Parallelism for JABBA (GA) as shown in \figurename~\ref{fig:speedup}. We can see the speedup $\Phi(m)$ scale almost linearly with the number of threads $m$. Since our algorithm is partially parallel in compression, which is hindered by the sequential part of the algorithm, that is, the digitization. This phenomenon can be naturally explained by Amdahl's law which gives the theoretical speedup at a fixed workload where there are limits on the benefits one can derive from parallelizing a computation.  


