Revisiting the research questions posed in Subsection \ref{sec:questions}, we can now provide answers based on the comprehensive analysis presented in this section.

\textbf{How much RAM is required for acceptable performance?} It may be defined taking account the size of the model whose file contains the network weights. As it resides in RAM, only one GB more is needed for the operating system and the software composing the web service. The results showed no increase in memory consumption face to increase in the concurrent messages processed. That is, there is evidence RAM does not interfere with latency since the average memory consumption between acceptable and unacceptable latencies was similar. In this way, acquiring more memory is a cost that can be avoided since it is an expensive resource. For example, machine G has twice as much memory as machine F, but they presented very close average latency values for the same scenarios. However, the last one (machine G) cost 43\%, 35\%, and 43\% more, for AWS, GCP, and Azure, respectively (Table \ref{tab:cost}).

\textbf{How many virtual CPUs (vCPUs) are necessary to handle the expected workload?} The results showed that more vCPU power reduces the solution’s latency. However, the amount of cache memory is the most critical parameter for architectures without GPU. This difference occurs because the cache memory is of the SRAM type, which is ten times faster than main memory (DRAM). So, the processor gets the data from the cache memory faster \citep{Chi2016,Hameed2013,Wang2008}. This result is important, as it indicates a cost reduction around 50\% for machine C concerning machine E, as seen in Table \ref{tab:cost}. It is essential to highlight that machine C achieved the best overall performance among those with low resources. Still on these machines, it was also observed that the acceptable latency threshold was often exceeded even with little vCPU load (around 20\%). An alternative to bypass this limitation would be to create a queue in the application layer to control submission flow taking this processing threshold into account.

\textbf{Can the model achieve acceptable performance without a GPU?} GPU solutions obtained the best results, as expected. However, they have an average cost in US\$ of 300\% more as can be verified by Table \ref{tab:cost}. Thus, for a feasibility test of a product such as a POC, the results show that non-GPU alternatives can be used.

